2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT When Uintah County, Utah, was organized in 1880. Sterling was appointed County Sheriff, which office he held for five years. During that period the county was often terrorized by outlaws who congregated at the "Hole in the Wall," 
2 CONC a hideout located north of Vernal on the Green River.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the years that Sterling was sheriff only one prisoner ever escaped and he was recaptured within twenty-four hours, One time, while Sterling, was bringing in a prisoner, the man drew his gun to shoot him. The man died, which
2 CONC  was very hard on both Sterling and his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then he served as Justice of the Peace in the North Ashley precinct during the years 1891-1892. He was elected County Commisioner in 1893.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just ten years after entering Ashley Valley (Vernal, Utah) Sterling built a huge red brick house. He was more affluent financially by then and the family had outgrown their log home. The house was a two-story structure with a deep
2 CONC  foundation and very thick walls. There were many windows and the ceilings were high. The roof, eves and windows were decorated with what is known today as "gingerbread" trim of wood painted white or green.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Excerpts from Sterling Driggs Colton History
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: Flora Elsie Colton, Sterling Leroy Colton, Don Byron Colton, Frank Edwin Colton, Warren Alfred Colton, Charles "Charley" Henry Colton, Lewis Lycurgus Colton, Nancy Fern Colton, Zora Maria Colton and Hugh Wilkins Colton.
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT Funeral Rites Held for S.D. Colton on Tuesday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pioneer, Miner, Stockman, Banker and Merchant Came to Ashley Valley in Winter of 1870. Was One of the First Sheriff's of Uintah County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mayor Sterling D. Colton was born March 22, 1851at Provo. His parents were Philander and Polly Merrill Colton. He went through the common grades of school as taught in those pioneer times. When fifteen years of age he went to Mont
2 CONC ana spending the summer there. He returned to Provo that fall and stayed there until 1868. That spring he went to South Pass, Sweet Water County, Wyoming, where there was a great mining excitement. From there he went to the Union 
2 CONC Pacific, working on the railroad until the Union Pacific connected with the Central Pacific. He then returned to Provo and shortly after went to Tintic with the first discoverers of the mining district, this was in december 1869.
2 CONT
2 CONT He worked in the mines there until March 1870, and on the 21st of that month he married Nancy Wilkins who was born at Provo on July 14, 1853, and the daughter of John D. and Nancy Kennedy Wilkins.
2 CONT
2 CONT The activities of the deceased until his complete retirement were many and varied from being the first sheriff of Uintah County to mayor of Vernal. Bishop of Maeser Ward, banker, stockman and merchant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Colton was elected the twelth mayor of Vernal, succeeding Mayor Ed D. Samuels. He served two terms from January 1, 1918 to January 1, 1922. The councilmen serving the first term with him were C.S. Carter, J., Wilbur Sowards, I
2 CONC saac Burton, John C. Bates, A.S Richardson and Leslie Ashton, recorder, Lyle Fisher, treasurer, Mabel Hacking, Marshal and Pundkeeper, Art Rich, City Attorney, Charles DeMoisy, Justice of the peace, Irvin Eaton, Superintendent of 
2 CONC Waterworks, Frank Hatch.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the second term, John N. Davis served as a councilman, H. Walter Woolley as recorder, Mrs. Hacking and Ethel Deming as treasurer, B.S. Eaton as Marshal and Jomes Dobbs as night marshal.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mayor ruled with fairness showing favors to none. It was during his administration that the extention of Vernal City water system was extended from the settling tank to the present intake. No person loved to see the completion
2 CONC  of a task undertaken better than did Mayor Colton. It was during this time that the beautifying of our streets by tree planting and keeping of beautiful yards was inauguerated.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the organization of Uintah County in 1881, Mayor Colton was elected sheriff, serving five years. During this period the country was terrorized by outlaws who congregated in the "Hole in the Wall." Sheriff Colton, however, never
2 CONC  went after an outlaw but what he got him. During all the years only one prisoner evergot away from him and her was captured within twenty-four hours.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Colton served as justice of the pece of Ashley precinct for two years 1889 and 1888. In 1893 he was elected county commissioner.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 1896, he was called on a mission to the Northern States laboring in Illinois and Michigan. However, he had to return in November on account of bad health. In August, 1896, he was ordained and set apart to be Bishop of 
2 CONC Maeser Ward. This position he held until September 1910 when he was honorably released on account of failing health. He then went to California and spent the winter of 1910 in an effort to regain his health.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1911, he returned to Vernal and built a home, retiring from active work. In 1917, he was re-elected mayor of Vernal, and re-elected in 1919. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/28238060/sterling-driggs-colton
