1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Mormon Pioneer=
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Pomeroy, Francis M. – (10th Ten) Born Feb. 22, 1822, at Somers, Tollard County, Connecticut, to Martin and Sybil Hunt Pomeroy. He was hired out to an uncle at the young age and when he reached 15, he joined a whaling crew. He 
2 CONC eventually became harpoonist, and worked his way to first mate. At 21, he was in a shipwreck off Peru, of which he was the lone survivor. A Castillian family took him in and he stayed for two years, learning Spanish. He returned t
2 CONC o Salem, Massachusetts, where he married Irene Ursula Haskell and was baptized in 1844. They moved to Nauvoo. With the original company on June 18, 1847, he was among nine men left to operate the upper ferry of the Platte River. W
2 CONC hile at the ferry, his wife arrived in a westbound company and he continued west with them. They settled in the Twelfth Ward in Salt Lake City, but he was soon called on a mission to Lower California with Apostle Charles C. Rich t
2 CONC hat lasted a year. He returned to Salt Lake City where he acted as host and interpreter to a delegation representing Mexico President Benito Juarez, who met with Brigham Young. In 1862, he moved to Weber Co., Utah, until 1864 when
2 CONC  he helped settle Paris, Idaho. He moved to Salt River Valley in Arizona and became a founder of the city of Mesa, Maricopa Co., Ariz. He was widely respected by the Indians there and was set apart as president of the Indian Missi
2 CONC on in 1881. Two years later, on Feb. 28, 1883, he died in Mesa at age 60..."
2 CONT >SOURCE: http://www.ldschurchnews.com/articles/58061/Biographies-of-the-original-1847-pioneer-company.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The son of Martin and Sybil Hunt Pomeroy and the third child in a family of nine. While still very young he was hired out to his uncle as a farm hand until he was fifteen years of age. One night he tied his belongings in a red han
2 CONC dkerchief and journeyed to New London where he became a crewmember of a whaling vessel. Six years later the ship was dashed against the rocks off the coast of Peru, but Francis managed to swim ashore and was found by the son of a 
2 CONC family who took him home. He remained for two years and learned to read and write in Spanish. He then made his way to New Orleans and finally Salem, Massachusetts where he met Irene Ursula Haskell who had recently joined the LDS C
2 CONC hurch and he also became a member. Francis and Irene were married the following year and they moved to Nauvoo, Illinois. Francis became a member of the first company of pioneers and came with them to the Salt Lake Valley. In 1849 
2 CONC he went on a mission to California returning the following year and on April 3, 1853 he married Sarah Matilda Colburn and in 1858 married Jessamine Routledge as plural wives. On June 15, 1860 Irene Haskell Pomeroy died. She had bo
2 CONC rne him seven children. Two years later Francis sold his farm and city property and moved his family to Weber, Utah where he remained for two years. Apostle Charles C. Rich later persuaded him to join him in Idaho. Moving his fami
2 CONC ly to Paris he took charge of the building of the first sawmill in that valley. The cold weather soon compelled him to move to a warmer climate and after hearing reports of the Salt River Valley in Arizona he moved there and event
2 CONC ually settling in Mesa. He was instrumental in bringing water to that vicinity through the utilization of the old Montezuma canal and later became one of the directors of the canal. He was also made trustee of the township and jus
2 CONC tice of the peace. As "pacifier" in the district, not only among the white population but also among the Indians and Spaniards, Francis was often called upon to help settle disputes. This, no doubt, inspired the authorities of the
2 CONC  Church to set him apart as Indian missionary. A year later he was made president of the Indian mission which position he filled until his death. — adapted from bio by Francis T. Pomeroy
2 CONT (Alt. birthdate 20 Feb. 1820)
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=10634834 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jan 29 2017, 18:30:33 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Marriage 1: July 1844 to Irene Ursula Haskell, Peterborough, Hillsborough, New Hampshire, USA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 2: 27 Feb 1853 to Sarah Matilda Colborn, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah, USA.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Francis Martin Pomeroy was the son of Martin and Sybil Hunt Pomeroy and the third child in a family of nine.  While still very young he was hired out to his uncle as a farm hand until he was fifteen years of age.  One night he tie
2 CONC d his belongings in a red handkerchief and journeyed to New London where he became a crew member of a whaling vessel.  Six years later the ship was dashed against the rocks off the coast of Peru, but Francis managed to swim ashore
2 CONC  and was found by the son of a family who took him home.  He remained for two years and learned to read and write in Spanish. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He then made his way to New Orleans and finally Salem, Massachusetts, where he met Irene Ursula Haskell who had recently joined the LDS Church and he also became a member.  Francis and Irene were married the following year and the
2 CONC y moved to Nauvoo, Illinois.  Francis became a member of the first company of pioneers and came with them to the Salt Lake Valley.  In 1849 he went on a mission to California returning the following year and on April 3, 1853 he ma
2 CONC rried Sarah Matilda Colburn and in 1858 married Jessamine Routledge as plural wives. On June 15, 1860 Irene Haskell Pomeroy died. She had borne him seven children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later Francis sold his farm and city property and moved his family to Weber, Utah where he remained for two years.  Apostle Charles C. Rich later persuaded him to join him in Idaho.  Moving his family to Paris he took ch
2 CONC arge of the building of the first sawmill in that valley.  The cold weather soon compelled him to move to a warmer climate and after hearing reports of the Salt River Valley in Arizona he moved there and eventually settling in Mes
2 CONC a.  He was instrumental in bringing water to that vicinity through the utilization of the old Montezuma canal and later became one of the directors of the canal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also made trustee of the township and justice of the peace.  As "pacifier" in the district, not only among the white population but also among the Indians and Spaniards, Francis was often called upon to help settle disputes
2 CONC . This, no doubt, inspired the authorities of the Church to set him apart as Indian missionary.  A year later he was made president of the Indian mission which position he filled until his death. — adapted from bio by Francis T. P
2 CONC omeroy
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate birth date 20 February 1820.
2 CONT
