1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biographical Summary:==
2 CONT
2 CONT "...'''Jessamine Routledge''' was born in London to Isaac and Martha Routledge. They had a lovely home and were quite well do. There were six children when the mother died in child­ birth. Jessamine and a girl friend heard the Mor
2 CONC mon elders singing in the streets. They stopped to listen and attended some of their meetings. She felt the truth of their teachings and, much to the disapproval of her family, she asked to be baptized. er father died and she deci
2 CONC ded to go to America with the Saints. She left England on the ship ''Horizon'' in May, 1856.
2 CONT
2 CONT They landed in Boston in June and proceeded to Iowa city. Each one was outfitted with a 'handcart. Jessamine was in the last handcart company to leave that year, the ill-fated Martin Company. The Saints voted to leave, even though
2 CONC  it was late in the season. Snow and bad weather caught them in the high country. The rations became low and weariness, hunger, sickness, and death plagued them. Jessamine lost her shoes in crossing a stream, but was fortunate in 
2 CONC finding another pair. Her pretty clothes and belongings were worthless to her. The party was finally rescued and taken to Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jessamine was a very pretty girl with wavy brown hair and dark brown eyes. She was rather small and had a quick wit. After she had been in the valley over a year she married Francis Pomeroy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene's children loved "Aunt Jessie" and she always had cookies and goodies for them..."
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE'': Original source unknown. Retrieved from http://www.orsonprattbrown.com/Skousen/francis-martin-pomeroy1822-1882.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary From Find A Grave.com:  Mrs. Jessie Pomeroy of Mesa, died yesterday at 9 o'clock a.m. Mrs. Pomeroy, or "Aunt Jessie," as she was called by all who knew her, was 62 years of age, having born in England, and when 16 years o
2 CONC ld came to America, going to Utah with the handcart company that crossed the plains in 1856.  This experience was as hard as usually falls to the lot of mortals but this girl endured it, assisting in pulling a two wheeled cart ove
2 CONC r 200 miles through a wilderness, surrounded at times by savages, who were more anxious than the wolves for the scalps of the travelers. In 1877, in company with her family and others she came to Arizona and has since lived in Mes
2 CONC a.
