1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Andrew Myron Colton.  - as written by his 4x great grandson  Bill Erickson At 16 years old his father enlisted in the civil war and his grandfather drafted into the war as well. He was made "the man of the house" a
2 CONC nd went to work.  One day he told his mother and sisters he was going to work. Instead, he ran away and went straight to a recruiting office to join his father and grandfather in the fight. He lied about his age and had to stand o
2 CONC n his tippy toes in order to reach the minimum height of 5'3". His mother and sisters never learned he enlisted until they saw him marching with a group of soldiers.  He then started to serve in the 44th Missouri Volunteer Infantr
2 CONC y. At one point he was injured in the battle of Franklin Tennessee. It says no more after that about his injury.  He was captured and spent 4 months in a southern prison (Meridian, Miss). He saw action at Pilot Know, Missouri; Col
2 CONC umbus, and Duck River. He was parolled 18 April 1865. He returned home after the war along with his father and grandfather. In 1868 he was united in marriage with his wife Viola. He moved to Bigelow Kansas and raised a family, He 
2 CONC farmed in that area until his death in 1920. He was buried in Antioch Cemetery south of Frankfort where most of his descendants are buried and live.
2 CONT At age three, Andrew Myron Colton travelled with his family with the William Snow/Joseph Young Company with a covered wagon group from Iowa to Salt Lake City, Utah. Andrew and his father and some of his siblings left Utah and went
2 CONC  to Kansas sometime in the late 1850's. One account states that Andrew's mother, Fidelia Reed Colton, remained in Utah when Andrew and the rest of the family left. 
