1 OCCU In the 1930 census, Minnie was working as a confectioner at home
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Minnie Elizabeth Colton was the first child and daughter of Joseph Philander Colton and his wife Adelaide Lois Rollins Colton.  In the 1880 census for Ashley, Uintah, Utah, Minnie was 2 months old and, her mother, 
2 CONC Ada, was 19, and her father, J.P. was 24 years old.  He was raising stock and Ada was keeping house.  They were both born in Utah, and Joseph's father in Michigan and his mother in Ohio.  Ada's father was born in New York and her 
2 CONC mother in England.  
2 CONT
2 CONT By the 1900 census, the family was living in Santa Rosa, Sonoma, California, and were listed as follows:  Joseph P., 43, Lois A., 37, Minnie E., 20, Minerva E., 18, and Harry L., 9.  Joe and Addie had been married for 21 years and
2 CONC  had given birth to 4 children, 3 of whom were still living.  Joe was working as a dairyman, and Minnie was working as a teacher of elocution.  They owned their own home free from a mortgage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Minnie and Minerva were all born in Utah and Harry in California.  In this census it showed that their father, Joseph, was also born in California and not Utah, which makes sense, since the Mormon Pioneers were in San Bernardino, 
2 CONC California, from about 1851 to 1857 and Joe was born in 1856.  At the same time, Adelaide's parents, James Henry Rollins, and his first wife, Eveline Walker Rollins, and his plural wife, Hannah Hulme Rollins, were also living in S
2 CONC an Bernardino.  Adelaide was not born until 1861, about four years after the Mormons moved back to Utah in 1857 at the time of Johnson's Army arriving there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not long after 1900 Minnie must have married a man by the name of Morris Rope, who was almost 20 years older than she, and in 1910 census taken April 16th, they were living in San Bernardino, California, in a home located at 530 C
2 CONC ourt Street, which they were renting.  They were listed in the census as follows:  Morris Rope, 49, Minnie, 30, Blanche, 8, and Leon, 7.  They had been married for 10 years, had given birth to 5 children, but only 3 were living at
2 CONC  the time.  However, the census showed that only 2 of them were in the same household at the time..  Morris Rope indicated he was Russian/Polish, as were his parents, Minnie was born in Utah and their children in California.  Morr
2 CONC is was working as a tailor in his own shop.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the 1920 census, Minnie was married to her second husband and they were living in Los Angeles at 3819 Vermont Avenue, a home they were renting.  They were listed as follows:  Robert Pierson, 58, Minnie, 39, Blanche Rope, 18, Le
2 CONC on, 16, Joseph Pierson, 7, and Virginia Pierson, 2 years one month old.  Robert had come to America from England in 1869 and was naturalized in 1875.  Robert was working as a trimmer in an auto top company, Blanche was working at 
2 CONC the information desk of a dry goods company, and Leon was a finisher at a lithographic company.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1930 census Minnie was divorced, and was living with her parents and children and they were listed as follows in the census record:  Joseph P. Colton, 73, Addie L, 69, Minnie, 48, Joe Pierson, 16, Virginia Pierson, 12, and 
2 CONC Leslie Pierson, 7.  She was working as a confectionery at home and her father was working as a city patrolman.  Joe owned his own home located at 620 East Lake Street in Burbank, California, which was valued at $6,000.00 and they 
2 CONC had a radio in their home.  Minnie's parents had been married for 50 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1940 census Minnie was listed as a 60-year old widow, living with her daughter, Blanche L. Steventon, 38, and they were renting a home located at 1203 N. Central Avenue in Glendale, California, for $25 per month.  Minnie ha
2 CONC d 5 years of college, and her daughter Blanche 2 years of high school.  Blanche was the owner of a dairy products company, but Minnie was not working in this census.
2 CONT
2 CONT Minnie Elizabeth Colton Rope Pierson passed away two years later on June 26, 1942, in Los Angeles, California.
