1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Garth M Colton
2 CONT
2 CONT     SANTA CLARA, Utah — Garth M Colton, 77, died at his home, February 3, 1998 from causes incident to colon cancer.
2 CONT
2 CONT     He was born July 2, 1920 in Maeser, Utah to Frank Edwin and Phebe Jane Merkley Colton. He married Betty Stringham, May 29, 1943. Their marriage was solemnized in the Salt Lake Temple, August 18, 1954.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Colton grew up in Vernal, Utah and graduated from Uintah High. A veteran of World War II, Colton served in the 115th Engineer Combat Battalion. He graduated from Utah State University in 1949. He worked for the Bureau of Land 
2 CONC Management for approximately 30 years. He was the District Manager of the Arizona Strip District at the time of his retirement. He received the United States Department of the Interior's Meritorious Service Award for his contribut
2 CONC ion to the management of public lands. He and his family lived in Vernal, Salt Lake City, Brigham City, and Lewistown, Montana. For the last 30 years, they have lived in Santa Clara as adopted Dutchmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT     An active member of the LDS Church, Colton served as a scoutmaster, high councilor, bishop, welfare services region agent, and many other priesthood and auxiliary callings. He served a full-time mission with his wife in the So
2 CONC uth Dakota Rapid City Mission. He served as a St. George Temple ordinance worker and sealer.
2 CONT
2 CONT     He is survived by his wife, Betty; his three sons: Stanley Garth Colton, Denton, Montana; Scott W. (Cokie) Colton, Hurricane; Kim S. Colton, Santa Clara; five grandsons: Shane, Luke, McKay, William, and Paul; three grandaughte
2 CONC rs: Tricia, Amber, and Katy; two brothers and one sister: Frank E. Colton, Salt Lake City; Sterling Boyd Colton, Bountiful; Mrs. Clyde (Clara) Hall, Vernal. He was preceded in death by two brothers and six sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Colton family wishes to thank Dr. Philip McMahill and his staff for their excellent care.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Funeral services will be Saturday, February 7, 1998, 10:30 a.m. at the Santa Clara 1st Ward Chapel, Santa Clara, Utah. Friends may call Friday, February 6, 7 to 8:30 p.m. at Metcalf Mortuary, 300 West St. George Blvd., St. Geo
2 CONC rge, Utah and Saturday, 9 to 10 a.m. at the Santa Clara 1st Ward Chapel prior to services. Interment in the Santa Clara City Cemetery under the direction of Metcalf Mortuary.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Salt Lake Tribune | Salt Lake City, Utah | February 5, 1998
