1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The above article about Almira's mother, Lucy Ann Merrill, was found on Family Search.org.  Almira was born May 4, 1878, at Saint David, Cochise Country, Arizona, the daughter of Seth Adelbert Merrill and Lucy Ann 
2 CONC Merrill, who were first cousins once removed.  Almira married Cicero Morris, October 4, 1894, in Safford, Graham County, Arizona.  She passed away on September 6, 1955, in South Gate, Los Angeles County, California.
2 CONT
2 CONT One-minute history bu Daryl James, from "James/Hatch One Minute Stories" written in 1994:
2 CONT
2 CONT Almira Merrill was born May 14, 1878, to Seth Adelbert Merrill and Lucy Ann Merrill.  She was the first child born in the Mormon colony at St. David, Arizona.  Her parents were called by Brigham Young to leave their homes and farm
2 CONC s in Utah to help settle southeastern Arizona.  Pioneering the new territory was difficult.  When Almira was five years old her mother died at age 23.  Almira went to live with her grandparents, Philemon Christopher and Cyrena Dus
2 CONC tin Merrill.  She lived with them 11 years until her marriage in 1894.  Almira went to school in St. David.  She remembered the 1883 earthquake there that shook down the school house and many homes.
2 CONT
2 CONT At age 12 Almira moved with her grandparents to Layton, Arizona, in the Gila Valley.  There she attended the Academy in Thatcher, Arizona.   Also in the Gila Valley she met Cicero Morris, whom she married October 4, 1894, in Saffo
2 CONC rd, Arizona.  Almira was 16 and Cicero was 23 at the time.  Together Almira and Cicero had 13 children over the next 28 years.  One child died at birth, one child died in Italy during World War II, and the rest lived long lives. A
2 CONC lmira and Cicero established a farm in the Gila Valley and worked hard to maintain it.  Almira cooked on a wooden stove, pumped water from a well, and did laundry on a scrubbing board.  Trips into town were rare and always a treat
2 CONC .  Almira's family was poor but did its best to survive.  "She was a faithful, proud woman," writes her daughter, Lorene Morris Umland.  "She survived many serious illnesses and hardships.  She was a true pioneer wife and mother."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1927 Almira, Cicero, and their oldest child, Cyrena, traveled to the Arizona Temple in Mesa, Arizona, to be sealed as a family.  Many of the other children were sealed to them during a later trip in 1948.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerald Heber James, a grandson, said Almira and Cicero lived with his family at 1515 W. Palm Lane in Phoenix for quite a while in their later lives.  "They were very quiet," Gerald said.  Gerald recalled one highlight of his grand
2 CONC parents' lives:  "One time Grandpa and Grandma were going to go visit Aunt Cyrena in California, and they were going to fly.  It was the first time they had ever flown.  They even had a reporter that took their picture, and it was
2 CONC  in the paper.  "Grandpa was sitting in his chair on the airplane, and he sat there and sat there.  He finally called a stewardess over and said, `When are we going to take off?'  The stewardess said, `Well, if you look out your w
2 CONC indow, there's the Colorado River down below."
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerald said he used to visit the Morris farm in Safford on vacations as a boy.  "I remember it as basically having four rooms and a bathroom," he said.  The front room was reserved for guests and not used much.  Almira died Septem
2 CONC ber 6, 1955, at South Gate, Los Angeles County, California, at age 77.  She was buried next to Cicero in Safford.  Cicero had died about nine months earlier.  Their posterity includes 54 grandchildren and more than 210 great-grand
2 CONC children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Typed history of Almira Merrill, by Lorene Morris Umland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Tape recorded interview with Gerald Heber James (grandson). October 1994;  Glendale, Arizona. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT As Almira's parents or grandparents were noted Morman pioneers I am including their stories. LB. Some trees have the following two people listed as Almira's parents (eg Find A Grave) and others have her parents listed as Seth Adel
2 CONC bert (Delbert or Dell) Merrill and Lucy Ann Merrill. Dell married at aged 17 and his wife Lucy died very young leaving him with 3 children under the age of four. It is more likely that Dell & Lucy were the parents and Philemon & C
2 CONC yrena were Almira's grandparents.LB
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieut Philemon Christopher Merril 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth:  Mar. 12, 1820 Byron Genesee County New York, USA  Death:  Sep. 16, 1904 Safford Graham County Arizona, USA 
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Samuel Merrill & Phoebe Odell
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Cyrena Dustin, 20 Sep 1840, Nauvoo, Hancock, Ill
2 CONT
2 CONT Children- Sabrina Lodena Merrill, Philemon Christopher Merrill Jr., Lucy Cyrena Merrill, Melissa Jane Merrill, Morgan Henry Merrill, Albina Atamira Merrill, Thomas Stephen Merrill, Seth Adelbert Merrill
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Mary Jane Smith, 5 Apr 1851
2 CONT
2 CONT Children- John Smith Merrill, Jedediah Grant Merrill, Hannah Ann Merrill, Cyrena Imogean Merrill, David Elmer Merrill, Joseph Lot Merrill, Henry Morgan Merrill, Peter Herbert Merrill
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Rhoda Sylvia Collett, 9 Oct 1873
2 CONT
2 CONT Children- Adrian Collett
2 CONT
2 CONT Enlised July 16, 1846 at Council Bluffs as Private Company B, promoted 3rd Lieutenant of the Company at Fort Leavenworth, Kansas, in August; promoted 2nd Lieutenant and appointed Battalion Adjutant, to suceed Lieut. G.P. Dykes, on
2 CONC  the Rio Grande near Socorro, N.M, October 30, 1846; marched with the Battalion across southern Arizona and honorably discharged as 1st Lieutenant at Los Angeles, July 16, 1847.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon Christopher Merrill went through the early hardships, persecutions and trials of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. He was born Nov. 12, 1820, at Byron, Genesee County, N.Y., to Samuel Merrill and Phoebe Ode
2 CONC ll.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 11 he accompanied his parents to Nauvoo, Ill., arriving in 1837. He was baptized into the Mormon Church in March 1839 and filled a mission to Wisconsin in 1840-41.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Sept. 20, 1840, he married Cyrena Dustin and went into housekeeping in Nauvoo. Philemon worked on the Temple which the Saints were trying hard to complete. He was closely associated with the Prophet Joseph Smith and tradition s
2 CONC ays he was one of the Prophet's bodyguards. Philemon helped evacuate Nauvoo when persecutors drove the Saints out, and he was one of the guards to cross the Mississippi River with Church records.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon enlisted as a volunteer in the Mormon Battalion Company B and endured the long march to the Pacific Coast. He left his wife and three children in Iowa to await his return.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once in California, Philemon stayed at headquarters in San Diego until the army discharged him in 1847. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philemon Merrill's sisters also were in the Mormon Battalion, they are Albina Marie Merrill Williams, wife of Thomas Stephen Williams. and Phoebe Lodema Merrill, she served as a nurse. From Diary of Albina Merrill Williams: "Sprin
2 CONC g came at last and then the word was conveyed to the isolated little colony that a start had been made by the pioneers at Council Bluffs to find a new home in the far west. No time was lost in bidding farewell to the old fort on t
2 CONC he Arkansas. Captain Brown's Company fell in the wake of the pioneers and all but overtook them before the Valley of the Great Salt Lake was reached. As a matter of fact, some of the Battalion people came in with the pioneers, but
2 CONC  the wagon containing Mrs. Williams and her sister did not arrive on the banks of City Creek until five days after President Young had pitched a tent there." --Heart Throbs of the West, Vol. 2, 1940, page 72. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He then made his way back to Iowa to rejoin his family; however, Philemon never saw his youngest child again because she had died shortly after his enlistment. In the spring of 1849 the Merrills made ready and crossed the plains t
2 CONC o Salt Lake Valley, arriving Oct. 16, 1849.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the permission of Cyrena, Philemon entered plural marriage April 5, 1851, with Mary Jane Smith. Then in June 1853 Philemon left to fill a mission for the Church to England until 1856. After his return, the Church called him t
2 CONC o help settle parts of southern Idaho. The two families moved to Liberty, Idaho, near Bear Lake in 1869 and then to Soda Springs, Idaho. While at Liberty, Philemon married a third woman, Rhoda Sylvia Collett, on Oct. 9, 1873.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1877 the Church called Philemon to help settle parts of central and southeastern Arizona. By then his second wife had died, so he took his two remaining families in August 1877 and headed for the San Pedro Valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had a great deal of faith, for the departure was with provisions only enough to last two days. The company made camp about one half mile south of the present town of St. David, Arizona, building a small stone fort of six or e
2 CONC ight rooms. Philemon had first seen the area with the Mormon Battalion in 1847. It was here in 1878, while nearly all the settlers were suffering with chills and fever, that Erastus Snow set apart Philemon to preside over the Sain
2 CONC ts in the area. In 1881 Philemon asked that he be relieved of his responsibilities as Presiding Elder and left St. David in 1890, settling in Safford, Ariz.
2 CONT
2 CONT He later held the office of Patriarch, being ordained by John Henry Smith. Philemon was a good public speaker and loved to talk about the Prophet Joseph Smith. About 1900 he moved to San Jose, Ariz., and died at his home there Sep
2 CONC t. 15, 1904, at the age of 84.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mormon Battalion members
2 CONT
2 CONT Many thanks to Mike H. for these wonderful photo's
2 CONT
