1 OCCU Married Byron Morley Anderson on October 9, 1902, in Salt Lake City, Utah
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Eleanor Roseltha Lutz Anderson:  Mrs. Anderson, 69, 780 3rd Avenue, died Sunday at 8 a.m. at her residence of complications following an operation.  A daughter of Thomas Jefferson and Mary Melissa Merrill Lutz, she
2 CONC  was born in Smithfield, Cache County, October 17, 1882.  She moved to Rexburg, Idaho when 2 years old, and as a young woman was well known there as a contralto soloist.  She was married to Byron Morley Anderson in the Salt Lake T
2 CONC emple of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints in October, 1902.  Byron died July 13, 1939.  Mrs. Anderson was the mother of 12 children, eight of whom survive.  Survivors include the following sons and daughters:  Laure
2 CONC l Anderson, Mrs. R.E. (Andrea) Whitney, Mrs. Grant W. (Mitzi) Needham, Don L. Anderson and DeWayne Anderson, all of Salt Lake City;  Mrs. T. W. (Lucille) Curley, Fort Knox, Kentucky;  Mrs. James E. (Betty) Davidson, Grand Rapids, 
2 CONC Michigan, and Rulon L. Anderson, Rocky River, and 18 grandchildren.  Funeral services will be conducted Wednesday at 12:30 p.m. in the Twenty-first Ward Chapel, 1st Avenue and K Street.  Friends may call at 280 E. South Temple Tue
2 CONC sday evening and Wednesday prior to services.  Burial will be int the Wasatch Lawn Memorial Park.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==================================
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor Roseltha Lutz married Byron Morley Anderson in 1902 in Salt Lake city, Utah.  When Byron Morley Anderson completed his U.S. World War I Draft Registration Card on September 12, 1918, in Albion, Cassia, Idaho, he described 
2 CONC himself as being tall with a stout build, grey eyes and brown hair. He was 42 years old, having been born August 30, 1876, and was working as a farmer for himself.  Byron and Rose were living in Burley, Cassia, Idaho, with their 9
2 CONC  children.  Two more children were born to them in the next few years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1920 census for Heyburn, Minidoka County, Idaho, Byron, 43, and Rose, 37, were living with their children, Laurel, 17, Leland, 15, Lucille, 13, Maurine, 11, Andrea, 10, Don L., 8, Cordelia, 6, DeWayne 4 years, 3 months old,
2 CONC  Myrtis, 2 years, 5 months old, and Rulon, 7 months old.  They were living in a rented house on Riverside Drive and Byron was working as a farmer.  Sadly, their son, Leland Frederick Anderson passed away at the very young age of o
2 CONC nly 19 years old on August 21, 1924, due to endocarditis following a burst appendix.  He had been working as a farmer at the time of his death in Idaho Falls, Bonneville county, Idaho.  He was buried in Rexburg, Idaho.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1930 U.S. Federal Census for Salt Lake City, Utah, Byron and Rose were living at 146 Third Avenue in a home they owned valued at $5,500 and Byron was working as an automobile salesman.  They were listed in the census record
2 CONC  as follows:  Byron, 53, Rose L., 47, Laurel R., 27, Lucille, 24, Maurine, 22, Andrea, 20, Don L., 19, Cordelia, 17, DeWayne, 14, Myrtis, 13, Rulon, 10, and Betty J., 4.  Laurel was working as a clerk in a dry goods store, Lucille
2 CONC  as a sales lady in a dry goods store, Maurine as a bookkeeper in a dry goods store, Andrea as a stenographer in the bonds industry, Don as a clerk in a grocery store, and Cordelia as a telephone operator.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sadly, Byron passed away just 9 years later in 1939 as a result of a pulmonary embolism due to an injury he had when he fell on a slippery walk and broke his femur bone in his right leg.  He was only 62 years old at the time of hi
2 CONC s death.  In the 1940 census, Rose was a 57-year old widow, living at 780 Third Avenue with her children:  Laurel, 36, Andrea, 30, DeWayne, 24, Rulon, 20, and Betty Jean, 14.  Laurel was working as a sales lady of retail ladies ap
2 CONC parel, Andrea as a stenographer for the social security administration, DeWayne as a stenographer for a wholesale chemical product company, and Rulon was an apprentice at a blue print company.  For the 52 weeks that Andrea worked 
2 CONC in 1939 she earned $1,200;  DeWayne earned $960; and Rulon $390.  Laurel did not disclose her income.
2 CONT
2 CONT Byron and Rose's daughter, Cordelia, had married Walter Leslie Dayton, and in the 1940 census they were living at a home they owned on East 2nd South in Salt Lake City, valued at $2,500.  Walter was working as a lithographer for a
2 CONC  printing company, and for the 52 weeks he worked at 48 hours per week in 1940, his income was $2,475.  Walter was 32 years old and Cordelia was 27 years old.  But sadly, just 7 months later, more tragedy struck on November 6, 194
2 CONC 0, when Cordelia Anderson Dayton passed away at the young age of only 27 years, 9 months and 12 days, from a brain tumor.  Cordelia was buried in the Mt. Olivet Cemetery.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later, Walter Leslie Dayton was inducted into the Army and served in World Wark II starting February 6, 1942.  Later Walter married a woman named Esther.  He passed away in 1970 and Esther in 1985 and they were also both
2 CONC  buried in the Mount Olivet Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor Roseltha Lutz Anderson passed away in 1951 and was buried with her husband in Salt Lake City.
