1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Died From His Injuries
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr Joseph Lull (not Low as Printed in the Gazette of Saturday) an account of whose injuries we published at the time of the accident, died at the residence of Mr Burdick, corner of Jackson and Pleasant streets, this morning. For t
2 CONC he past four years, Mr Lull has been a resident of Hardin, Carlton County, Iowa. He had disposed of his business there, and was on his way to Scipio, Cayaga County, NY where he formerly resided. The deceased, on his way east, had 
2 CONC stopped in Edgerton at the residence of Daniel Coon, an old friend, for a short visit, and came to this city on the day of the accident with a son of Mr Coon. While riding on Jackson street Saturday, the forward axletree of the ca
2 CONC rriage suddenly broke, letting theat end of the box down upon the ground and the frightened horse kicked violently to free himself from the buggy. Striking Mr Low (Lull) a violent blow upon his right leg, both bones of that limb, 
2 CONC between the knee and the ankle, were broken and driven through the flesh. The other leg also received the impress of the horses feet in about the same spot, but the force was not sufficient to fracture the bone. On Monday morning 
2 CONC the symptoms of the decay of the wounded limb were alarming, and the utmost skill of the attending surgeons failed to stay the progress of the disease to a fatal termination. Deceased was a widower, about forty years of age and le
2 CONC aves a son of eleven years. He was to have been married in his native town on the 15th inst. He was a member of Scipio Lodge F.A.M. and has been the recipient of kindly attention from members of the fraternity in this city during 
2 CONC his brief illness. The remains were taken to Auburn NY this afternoon, in charge of Mr George Lull, of Iowa, brother of the deceased.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: The Gazette, Janesville, WI. Wednesday 11 June 1873.
