1 NAME Norma Elizabeth /Morris/
2 GIVN Norma Elizabeth
2 SURN Morris
2 _MARNM James
1 NAME Norma Morris James
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 MAY 1914
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lone Star
3 STAE Arizona
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 2002
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 MAR 2002
1 OCCU Married George Heber James, 9/13/1931 in Clifton, AZ
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Norma Morris James - Essentials
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 8 May 1914; Lone Star (Safford), Graham County, Arizona
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of: Cicero MORRIS and Almira MERRILL
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: George Heber JAMES (Jr.); 13 September 1931; Clifton, Greenlee County, Arizona
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 5 March 2002; Phoenix, Maricopa County, Arizona
2 CONT
2 CONT Page contents
2 CONT
2 CONT One-minute history
2 CONT
2 CONT Life Sketch Read at Funeral Service
2 CONT
2 CONT "Norma, This Is Your Life"
2 CONT
2 CONT BY DARYL JAMES
2 CONT
2 CONT FROM "JAMES/HATCH ONE MINUTE HISTORIES"
2 CONT
2 CONT (1994; UPDATED 10 MARCH 2002)
2 CONT
2 CONT      Norma Morris was born on a farm near Lone Star, Ariz., on May 8, 1914, to Cicero Morris and Almira Merrill. She was the ninth of 12 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT      The farm had chickens, corn fields and watermelon patches, and the house where Norma's family lived had four rooms and a bathroom. "There was a great big living room and a great big sitting room," said Gerald James, Norma's s
2 CONC on. "The kitchen was a huge room, and then there was a bathroom between the kitchen and the bedroom."
2 CONT
2 CONT      Norma grew up in Lone Star and attended elementary school there. Norma was baptized into The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints in a big canal in Safford, Ariz., when she was 8 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT      She said attending Sunday School with her family was one of the highlights of her childhood. "We lived quite far out on our farm," Norma said. "It was always a treat to dress in our Sunday best and make the trip to Sunday Sch
2 CONC ool."
2 CONT
2 CONT      When Norma reached ninth grade, she moved to Safford to continue her schooling. She lived with her brother, Howard, and his wife and earned money working as a waitress. She left school before graduation to work full time. Dur
2 CONC ing this period she met George Heber James, whom she married at Clifton, Ariz., on Sept. 13, 1931. Norma was 17 and Heber was 20 at the time of their marriage. They were later sealed in the Mesa Arizona Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Norma kept her job long enough after the wedding to earn enough money to buy material for her younger twin sisters to make new dresses for school. Her sisters were excited and thankful for Norma's sacrifice and remembered the
2 CONC  gift throughout their lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Norma wrote that she spent most of her married life in Phoenix, but she and Heber also owned a summer home in Pine Top, Ariz., a cabin in Pine, Ariz., and later a home in Thatcher, Ariz. At the time of this article (1994), No
2 CONC rma and Heber were retired and living again in Phoenix. Although Norma spent most of her married life as a master homemaker, she spent some time as a professional caterer, cake decorator and curtain maker.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Together Norma and Heber had one son and six daughters. Jacalyn Sue, the fifth daughter, died shortly after birth in 1950 due to a bad blood transfusion. Jacalyn died in Phoenix and was buried in Thatcher at the city cemetery
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT      Norma loved the Lord and gave her talents freely to the LDS Church. She served as a Primary president, a Relief Society counselor and a Mutual Improvement Association counselor. In her later life, she and Heber spent two days
2 CONC  a week working in the Mesa Arizona Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT      "My parents have always been dedicated to the Church and have always tried to do what's right," Gerald said in 1994.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Norma loved crafts and cooking all her life. For awhile she belonged to a sewing club. She entered crafts in county and state fairs and sold others at boutiques and craft sales. Almost all her children and grandchildren have 
2 CONC pieces of her handiwork hanging on walls or sitting on shelves in their homes.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Many people thought of Norma as being quiet and shy, but Gerald said she could also laugh and have a good time when she was around friends -- especially her sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Norma's 80th birthday party was a large family affair at her daughter Joyce's house in Glendale, Ariz. Alzheimer's disease afflicted Norma during the last decade or so of her life, and in the years before her death she retain
2 CONC ed little memory. She lived with Heber at their home at 4126 W. Eva St. in Phoenix until her condition grew rapidly worse early in 2002. She spent the final weeks of her life at a care home, and then died March 5, 2002, at Hospice
2 CONC  of Arizona in Phoenix. She was 87.
2 CONT
2 CONT      She was survived by Heber, her husband of 70 years, and 125 descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT -- Sources: 1. Typed, 2-page autobiography of Norma Morris. 3. Tape-recorded interview with Gerald Heber James (son) in October 1994 at Glendale, Arizona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Return to top
2 CONT
2 CONT Norma, This Is Your Life
2 CONT
2 CONT BY NORMA G. NIX
2 CONT
2 CONT FAMILY FRIEND
2 CONT
2 CONT WRITTEN ABOUT 1950
2 CONT
2 CONT      In days of old -- in ancient times
2 CONT
2 CONT      When kings and pharoahs ruled --
2 CONT
2 CONT      When pyramids were used as tombs
2 CONT
2 CONT      And wise men oft were schooled --
2 CONT
2 CONT      When mummies many times were wrapped
2 CONT
2 CONT      In muslin, fine and white
2 CONT
2 CONT      And buried deep in pyramids
2 CONT
2 CONT      To never see the light.
2 CONT
2 CONT      In those old days quite often though
2 CONT
2 CONT      The past was buried deep --
2 CONT
2 CONT      And mummies had for centuries
2 CONT
2 CONT      Lain quietly asleep --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Explorers came with shovel,
2 CONT
2 CONT      With pick and spade at last --
2 CONT
2 CONT      And with no sign of trouble
2 CONT
2 CONT      They dug up the ancient past.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Today we live in modern worlds
2 CONT
2 CONT      With tools of every kind,
2 CONT
2 CONT      With hammer, bits, electric torch
2 CONT
2 CONT      And ascetalyne you'll find --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Electric engines, atom bombs,
2 CONT
2 CONT      H-bombs, lathe and last --
2 CONT
2 CONT      But what a time I still have had
2 CONT
2 CONT      In digging up the past.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Cleopatra had her charms
2 CONT
2 CONT      And history oft repeats them --
2 CONT
2 CONT      But in our midst so soon to leave
2 CONT
2 CONT      Is one whose charms unseats them.
2 CONT
2 CONT      She isn't buried in a tomb --
2 CONT
2 CONT      No pyramid stands guard --
2 CONT
2 CONT      But I've never known a person's past
2 CONT
2 CONT      To uncover -- quite so hard.
2 CONT
2 CONT      But wait -- I still have a story.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Call it history or fiction --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Call it literature or fancy
2 CONT
2 CONT      Or a novel of good diction --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Call it past, or present or future --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Call it love or peace or strife --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Call it happiness or sorrow --
2 CONT
2 CONT      But Norma: "This is your life."
2 CONT
2 CONT      It began -- oh many years ago --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Thirty-six years long,
2 CONT
2 CONT      That nineteen fourteen set the treble
2 CONT
2 CONT      For this cradle song --
2 CONT
2 CONT      May the eighth in a cottage
2 CONT
2 CONT      From Stafford not too far --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Was born a little baby
2 CONT
2 CONT      In a place they called Lone Star.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Lone Star -- it sounds so peaceful
2 CONT
2 CONT      As if sheep were there abiding --
2 CONT
2 CONT      As though care and toil and sorrow
2 CONT
2 CONT      Would forever be in hiding --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Lone Star -- a little farming town
2 CONT
2 CONT      So small and yet so nice --
2 CONT
2 CONT      But after a baby's first wee cry
2 CONT
2 CONT      So very full of spice
2 CONT
2 CONT      And though the town had just a school
2 CONT
2 CONT      And not a single store --
2 CONT
2 CONT      It was forever famous
2 CONT
2 CONT      Though it hadn't been before.
2 CONT
2 CONT      But such a bouncing baby,
2 CONT
2 CONT      And such a bundle of heaven,
2 CONT
2 CONT      And such a wonderful mother and dad
2 CONT
2 CONT      And brothers and sisters eleven.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Yes such a precious baby
2 CONT
2 CONT      Made the small town grow in fame --
2 CONT
2 CONT      And Mr. and Mrs. Morris said:
2 CONT
2 CONT      "Norma is her name."
2 CONT
2 CONT      And so she cried and slept and laughed
2 CONT
2 CONT      And cut her teeth and grew --
2 CONT
2 CONT      And maybe she had tantrums
2 CONT
2 CONT      But I doubt that -- don't you?
2 CONT
2 CONT      And I suppose she often teased
2 CONT
2 CONT      Her sisters and her brothers
2 CONT
2 CONT      And took her turn at playing with
2 CONT
2 CONT      And caring for the others.
2 CONT
2 CONT      About this time, far to the south
2 CONT
2 CONT      In the Land of the Siesta,
2 CONT
2 CONT      Mr. and Mrs. Heber James
2 CONT
2 CONT      Were celebrating la fiesta --
2 CONT
2 CONT      For added to their family
2 CONT
2 CONT      Was Norma's barefoot beau.
2 CONT
2 CONT      A handsome red-faced baby
2 CONT
2 CONT      Who was born in Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Oh pyramids, your mystery
2 CONT
2 CONT      Has nothing now on me --
2 CONT
2 CONT      For to this part of Norma's life
2 CONT
2 CONT      I couldn't find the key.
2 CONT
2 CONT      But I am sure she went to school
2 CONT
2 CONT      And had her barefoot beau --
2 CONT
2 CONT      And pigtails flying in the breeze
2 CONT
2 CONT      And smudges on her nose --
2 CONT
2 CONT      And even though she's grown up now --
2 CONT
2 CONT      And good as you can see --
2 CONT
2 CONT      She often sat in closets
2 CONT
2 CONT      That were dark as they could be.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Can't you hear her wail of terror,
2 CONT
2 CONT      Sisters -- years and years ago?
2 CONT
2 CONT      She was just a little girl then
2 CONT
2 CONT      But soon she had to grow.
2 CONT
2 CONT      She grew and grew and grew and grew --
2 CONT
2 CONT      And then she fell in love.
2 CONT
2 CONT      And when I tell you her great age
2 CONT
2 CONT      You'll say, "Why stars above!!!!" --
2 CONT
2 CONT      She couldn't be an old maid
2 CONT
2 CONT      Or someone's spinster aunt --
2 CONT
2 CONT      She hadn't learned at fourteen
2 CONT
2 CONT      Two words that say, "I can't."
2 CONT
2 CONT      So what do you suppose she did,
2 CONT
2 CONT      Though supposing isn't in it?
2 CONT
2 CONT      At fourteen she fell right in love.
2 CONT
2 CONT      And got engaged that minute.
2 CONT
2 CONT      And though her fiance went away
2 CONT
2 CONT      And left her all alone --
2 CONT
2 CONT      She went with many other boys
2 CONT
2 CONT      Who called her on the phone.
2 CONT
2 CONT      And often, when Heber was away,
2 CONT
2 CONT      And she was off a-dancing --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Her feet would follow her partner
2 CONT
2 CONT      But her thoughts were off romancing --
2 CONT
2 CONT      Because she was so much in love
2 CONT
2 CONT      Her heart was always true --
2 CONT
2 CONT      But she spent many hours at home
2 CONT
2 CONT      With nothing more to do
2 CONT
2 CONT      Than wish and wish for Heber.
2 CONT
2 CONT      And so they soon were married
2 CONT
2 CONT      In the little town of Clifton
2 CONT
2 CONT      Where the wedding party tarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT      September the thirteenth of the year
2 CONT
2 CONT      Nineteen-thirty-one.
2 CONT
2 CONT      This was when Norma and Heber's    trip of matrimony begun.
2 CONT
2 CONT A FEW OF HER FAVORITE THINGS:
2 CONT
2 CONT Norma's funeral program contains a list, prepared by her family, of the following things she enjoyed:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Her family
2 CONT
2 CONT     * The Church and the gospel
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Her friends
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Visiting with her sisters
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Her sewing club
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Camping at Little Rosey
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Her trip to Hawaii
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Working in the Mesa Arizona Temple 
2 CONT
2 CONT The program also lists some of Norma's special talents and skills:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Writing and directing roadshows
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making enchilada dinners
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Crocheting
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making crafts with dried flowers
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sewing baby dresses
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making rafia dolls
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Working with ceramics
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making wedding cakes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making drapes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Copper tooling
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making crafts with resin grapes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Quilting
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making beaded pictures
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sewing
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Organizing ward bazaars
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Painting
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making trapunto displays
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Baking bread and cinnamon rolls
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Making and dressing porcelain dolls
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Weaving macrame
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Designing floral arrangements 
2 CONT
2 CONT
