2 SURN Abbott
2 _MARNM Brown
1 NAME Phoebe A. Brown (Abbott)
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Thatcher, Graham, Arizona
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 18 MAY 1831
2 PLAC Hornellsville, Steuben, New York
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 APR 1914
2 PLAC Thatcher, Graham, Arizona
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Mormon Pioneer:==
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 7th of July we started across the Plains in Brother George A. Smith's company. We had a good time until we reached Sweetwater. Then we had snowstorms and lost many cattle. We had to throw away trunks and baggage to make the
2 CONC  loads lighter. I took malarial fever. We had to burn buffalo chips for wood. We saw many buffaloes. Helped to care for John Henry Smith while crossing the plains.
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE'':: http://www.orsonprattbrown.com/phebe-journals.html] 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ==Autobiography:==
2 CONT
2 CONT "...I was born in New York, Steuben County, New York, May 18, 1831, the daughter of Stephen Abbott and Abigail Smith Abbott. When I was five years of age, my father moved to Illinois, Pike County. Father and mother joined the Chur
2 CONC ch of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints in 1843, and moved to Nauvoo. I was baptized when I was eight years old, in the Mississippi River by William McWinteres, and led out of the water by the Prophet Joseph Smith. What joy I felt
2 CONC ! My whole body was filled with joy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1844, my father died, leaving mother with eight children, the oldest being fifteen years and the youngest three months. They had to endure all the hardships of that period. I was the third girl in the family. I had to go to wor
2 CONC k for my living among strangers. I attended Relief Society meetings with mother and heard the Prophet speak to the sisters. I saw him after his martyrdom. Then the Saints were driven from Nauvoo. I started out with Brother Church,
2 CONC  but was taken sick and came near dying.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was at Mount Pisgah when the Mormon Battalion was called. This separated many families and there was great sorrow among the Saints.
2 CONT
2 CONT My mother and family, including my oldest sister, who had been married, had come on as far as Garden Grove. My sister Charilla Abbott Browning came with me expecting to have a home with our married sister Emily Abbott Bunker. My b
2 CONC rother-in-law, Edward Bunker, had joined the Battalion and had to go to meet them. My sister was heartbroken.
2 CONT
2 CONT I soon went to live with a Sister Baldwin. Sister Charilla went with a friend to Missouri. Mother and the rest of the children came in the fall and then our joy was full. I had to work out of doors in the snow. I came down with th
2 CONC e pleurisy and was sick for three months. Mother taught school and it was necessary for her to cut wood to keep us warm. I came near dying and was just able to walk when my sister, Mrs. Bunker was confined. We had hard work to sav
2 CONC e them. Sister Charilla came in the spring. We cleared off three acres of land; planted it; and raised a good crop.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall, we moved to Winter Quarters. In January Brother Bunker came in hungry and ragged. We soon got him some clothes and fed him. I worked out all winter. Went to Kimball's farm and while there, the family moved to Willow C
2 CONC reek, thinking I would get a chance to follow. One of Captain James Brown's boys and I started and had to walk all the way, twenty-five miles, carrying my bundle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring I helped Mr. Bunker on the farm. In the fall, I worked for Mr. McCenny, an Indian agent, and cooked for forty people three months. I then went home and started to work for a Mrs. Hammers. The Indians were having an e
2 CONC pidemic of cholera. Father came to me in a dream and said, "Phebe, go home quickly." I told Mr. Hammers, but could not tell him why. I got the Indians to take me over the river and then had four miles to walk in order to reach hom
2 CONC e. The Hammers family was taken with cholera. Mrs. Hammer and her babe, little girl, and hired man died that night, so you see how father and the Lord watched over me.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 7th of July we started across the Plains in Brother George A. Smith's company. We had a good time until we reached Sweetwater. Then we had snowstorms and lost many cattle. We had to throw away trunks and baggage to make the
2 CONC  loads lighter. I took malarial fever. We had to burn buffalo chips for wood. We saw many buffaloes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Helped to care for John Henry Smith while crossing the plains. Reached Brown's Fort or Ogden on the 17th of October, 1849.
2 CONT
2 CONT I worked for Mary McRee Brown and went to school. My sister Charilla taught school that winter. In the spring the flood came. Our calves, goats, chickens and everything else were swimming and we ourselves were almost waist deep in
2 CONC  water. The neighbors came and helped us out. We camped under some trees. Brother Brown made a stockade and milk houses. The boys and girls did the milking; made butter and cheese; made straw hats for the family. When the emigrant
2 CONC s came, we made hats for them..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...n the fall of 1850, I was married to Captain James Brown [when 19 years old]. I took care of his four boys. We passed through two grasshopper years, and the people suffered for want of food.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was treasurer of the first Relief Society. My husband, James Brown, was called on a mission for two years. I lost my little baby boy [Stephen Abbott Brown, born August 22, 1851, and died December 22, 1853]. My husband's son Jame
2 CONC s Morehead Brown lived with me. We worked in the garden. I had fifty pounds of wool. Got it carded and went to spinning. I found plenty to do. I had the wool spun and seventy pounds of carpet rags sewed. When my husband came back,
2 CONC  he brought me some warp. It had to be doubled and twisted. I got it all done and wove a carpet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain James Brown returned from his mission to England in 1852. He brought a black silk shawl beautifully embroidered and fringed for his dear wife Phebe Abbott Brown. Phebe gave this shawl in 1882 to her daughter Mrs. Snyder. P
2 CONC hebe's shawl is now a part of the D.U.P. Treasures of Pioneer Heirlooms exhibit on display at the State Capital. --Heart Throbs of the West, Vol. 8, DUP 1947, Page 11.
2 CONT
2 CONT In October, my baby girl (Phoebe Adelaide Brown) was born [October 24, 1855."Addie"] She later married Henry Theodore Snyder (see photo) in 1877. [They had eight or more children: Sarah Ethel Snyder [Toller], Phoebe Abigail Snyder
2 CONC  , Frank Henry Snyder [Evans], Leona L. Snyder [Dignau], Harrison Blaine Snyder [Snyder], Leon Ogden Snyder, Orson Joseph Snyder, Austin Joe Snyder] Phoebe Adelaide Brown Snyder died June 11, 1930 in Venice, California, buried in 
2 CONC Ogden, Weber, Utah].
2 CONT
2 CONT Phoebe Adelaide Brown Snyder
2 CONT & Henry Theodore Snyder
2 CONT
2 CONT We always had a great deal of company and had the privilege of entertaining all the apostles except one; Brother George A. Smith; President Brigham Young; Heber C. Kimball; Parley P. Pratt; Orson Pratt; Daniel H. Wells. We always 
2 CONC enjoyed it and always had something to give them to eat. They are all on the other side now.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then came Johnson's Army. My husband James had received some flax. We fixed it and I spun and wove three table cloths; three long roller towels while he was moving some of his family south. Then I drove the horses and made two tri
2 CONC ps. I had the privilege of returning home. My husband had three hundred bushels of volunteer wheat, but had no one to help him to harvest it. I went in the field, helped to bind and haul until it was done. We saved our bread.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next excitement was Connor's fight with the Indians at Bear River. It was very cold and the Indians had shot and wounded many of our people. We had to bring them in on sleds. We had to give them beds and something to eat. We w
2 CONC ere up all night and what terrible suffering they had to endure! When the rest came, they were so badly frozen that they could not feed themselves. Several died that night. This was in winter of 1863.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring, May 22, [1863], my baby boy (Orson Pratt Brown) was born. There was seven years between him and my daughter Addie (Phoebe Adelaide). On the 30th of September [1863] my husband James Brown died (on his birthday) thro
2 CONC ugh an accident that happened while grinding sugar cane. [Phebe was 32 years old when Captain James Brown died.] Mother Abigail Abbott came to live with me and helped to pass away the time. (After James died I wove cloth and carpe
2 CONC t to make a living, took boarders.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Orson Pratt Brown
2 CONT on May 1886. Age 32.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel William Nicol Fife
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall of 1866 [October 9, 1866] I married [as a plural wife to] Colonel William Nicol Fife.[Colonel of the first Regiment, First Brigade, Weber Military District. Colonel William Nicol Fife, was married to Phebe's younger si
2 CONC ster. Cynthia Abbott  married WNF on 2 November 1867. Members of the Layton family say WNF had up to 13 wives.]  In July 22, [1867,] my little girl was born [Cynthia Fife]. In two years another girl was born, but died at birth. In
2 CONC  another year another came, but it was taken with smallpox and died. I had many trying times and I worked hard (and) felt as though I could not live long.
2 CONT
2 CONT  (See http://students.cs.byu.edu/`heath/family/white/c2943.htm for more WNF info)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1880, we moved to Sulphur Springs Valley, Arizona. There were several Mormon families to haul lumber from the Mill to Tombstone mining camp. My health became better, than it had been for years. We lived in a tent for eleven mon
2 CONC ths. Then we built a nice frame, four-room house. We had a great deal of company between rustlers and Indians and prospectors; altogether it was an exciting time. The Mormons all moved away, leaving us the only Mormons there, but 
2 CONC always had company. Our teams were busy; our house like a boarding house. The third year, my sister Emily Abbott Bunker and her husband Edward Bunker, came and were there all summer. In the fall, Mrs. Fife [Diana Fife and her 13 y
2 CONC ear old daughter, Agnes Ann] came and I went to St. George to meet mother and work in the Temple. There were mother, three girls, one son, and three grandchildren to have their work done.
2 CONT
2 CONT My daughter Cynthia Fife my sister Charilla Abbott Browning, and I went by team to the Wah Wah Springs [Beaver,Utah] to our sister, Lydia Lucina Abbott Squire. We stayed two days and then went on the train to Ogden, where we met m
2 CONC y daughter Addie and Harry Snyder; and also many of my old friends. What great joy I felt! I had lived there for thirty years and made many friends. I stayed at my daughter's for eleven months. I had never spent such a pleasant ti
2 CONC me. Word came that Mrs. Fife [Diana Fife] had been killed by the Mexicans. I had to hurry home so that Mr. Fife could come with his son William Fife to Ogden. Oh, the sorrow and confusion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cynthia A. Fife married
2 CONT Joseph Layton in 1886
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Layton
2 CONT
2 CONT On arriving home we found my husband and my son Orson there alone. Made arrangements for Mr. Fife to go with his daughter, Agnes Fife, to Ogden. I was to stop on the ranch. Cynthia Fife and Sarah Brown came later, then it was not 
2 CONC so lonesome. Nothing happened but a few Indian scares until the brethren on the underground came. It was a good place to hide and be safe. They were very welcome. After two years they went home.
2 CONT
2 CONT My daughter Cynthia Abigail Fife was married to Joseph Layton [18 September 1886 in Safford, Graham, AZ. They had six children: Joseph Christopher Layton, Glenna Selina Layton, Edna Cynthia Layton, William Walter Layton, Iretta La
2 CONC yton, Phoebe Caroline Layton].
2 CONT
2 CONT Orson came to Thatcher to get some land and we were alone. Brothers Snow and Thatcher came and advised me to go to Mexico; Mr. Fife was to sell out and come down with family later on.
2 CONT
2 CONT Orson was called on a mission. He and I went to Juárez. We arrived there May 30th. Orson was taken with chills and fever and was very sick for three weeks. Some of the boys rented his team; he went along; killed some deer. He bega
2 CONC n to get better. We plowed the garden. The neighbors were very kind and let me have garden stuff until mine grew. I had some beautiful Plymouth Rock chickens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Everybody wanted eggs and gave me garden stuff in exchange. The people were very ragged, but I had some overalls and coats, together with tread, needles and pins. The sisters worked them over for their boys. I was not idle, but he
2 CONC lped many babies come into the world; took care of their mothers; and nursed many sick. I took care of my garden. Orson went to work. He sent me some wheat. I marketed it and browned and ground it in my large coffee mill. It made 
2 CONC good bread. That fall, I had a good deal of dried corn and beans; made a lot of pickles. Orson was married to Mattie Romney [10 October 1887] and built an adobe room in front of my tent. We went along fine.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sisters had no stocking for themselves nor for their children. I was appointed Relief Society President. We had some wheels made and got some cards; spun and carded wool that we bought of the Mexicans. We soon had some yarn ma
2 CONC de. The sisters were united and worked with a will. Several raised cotton and had that for summer. In October, on the 30th, Mattie's baby was born. In February, I went to Thatcher to take care of my daughter Cynthia Abigail Fife L
2 CONC ayton in her confinement. I stayed there eleven months. Then I returned home. In May, Mattie's little girl died. She gave birth to a boy not long after.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January, 1890 I went to St. David [Arizona] to attend Cynthia. In May I went to Ogden to be with my daughter Addie and stayed there two years. Orson came to go to the Logan Temple. I went with them and did some work. Was presen
2 CONC t at the dedication of the Logan Temple; also the dedication of the Salt Lake Temple in 1893. Then went to Mexico to live where my son Orson lives..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...In the year 1905 Phebe moved to Thatcher Arizona to live with her daughter, Cynthia Layton; she was much afflicted with rheumatism, that she had contracted in Mexico, she walked on crutches for some time, then had a wheel chai
2 CONC r, was always cheerful, and happy, although she suffered much pain from her afflictions, she lived and died a true Latter-day Saint, and taught her family to do the same.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died January 9, 1914 , in Thatcher, Arizona, at the age of 84 years. [Headstone at Thatcher Cemetery states date as January 9, 1915]
2 CONT
2 CONT President Andrew Kimball, Brigham Stowell, and Caroline Eyring, were the speakers at her funeral services; she was buried in Thatcher, Graham, Arizona..."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Retrieved from http://www.orsonprattbrown.com/phebe-journals.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
