2 CONT ==Biographical Summary:==
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher Layton reveals himself as a common man who achieved great success as a business man, a Church man and particularly as a family man, being a father of sixty five children and a husband to ten wives. The courage, perseve
2 CONC rence, and faith of the man during trials, sorrow, despair, persecutions and rebuff inspires the reader and marks Layton as one of the great men in pioneer Mormonism.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher Layton was a diamond in the rough, an Englishman by birth. His first practical experience was at the age of seven when he kept crows off the wheat fields for 36c a week.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1841, when he was twenty years of age, Latter-day Saint missionaries came to Thorncut. Christopher Layton heard and believed their message and was baptized on January 1, 1842. The following year he was married to Mary Matthews,
2 CONC  and in 1843 the two set sail for America with 212 Saints on the ship Swanton led by Elder Lorenzo Snow. They arrived in Nauvoo in April, 1843, and established a home. At Nauvoo they met the Prophet Joseph Smith who shook Brother 
2 CONC Layton's hand. He said, "God bless you," so fervently that the words "sank deep into our hearts giving us a feeling of peace such as we never had before." From here on to his final days, the life of Christopher Layton was full of 
2 CONC dedication to the Church, loyalty to its leaders, activity in the settlement of the western Zion, and service to his fellow church men. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the English hamlet of Big Mound, eight miles from Nauvoo, Christopher Layton was putting in sod corn when he heard the news of the death of Joseph, and he dropped his load and walked slowly out of the field. Two years later Mar
2 CONC y Matthews Layton died, leaving her husband with a thirteen-month-old daughter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A friendly family took the child to rear, and, in 1846, Christopher Layton departed for the West with the exiled Saints. Arriving at the Missouri River he enlisted in the Mormon Battalion with Company C, and made the long journey 
2 CONC on foot to California. He was only a private in the Mormon Battalion, but his military service spread over nearly a quarter of a century. In 1868 he was commissioned a lieutenant colonel.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his discharge at Los Angeles, in July, 1847, he made his way to the gold fields. There he forsook mining for the buying and selling of horses. Layton worked as a ranch foreman. He bought a band of horses at $1.50 a head and 
2 CONC sold each one for $100. He made considerable money and decided to sail to England to visit his parents. After a long, stormy voyage he reached his homeland, only to find that his mother had died two weeks before his arrival. With 
2 CONC this small fortune he brought bride, six relatives, and 250 friends to America. He engaged passage for all of them. While in England he married Sarah Martin. After a delay of more than a year in St. Louis, he led a large company o
2 CONC f Saints across the plains and mountains to Salt Lake Valley. For the next thirty years Christopher Layton pioneered in Carson Valley, Nevada, where he built for himself a large herd of cattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT When it was known that the government was sending an army to Utah, the outlying settlements were abandoned, and Christopher Layton was called to Utah to the headquarters of the Church. He settled himself in Davis County and establ
2 CONC ished Kaysville. Here he pioneered dry farming and introduced alfalfa which proved to be an epoch-making experiment. He also owned a farm on Antelope Island. He was general superintendent for the Utah Central Railroad. He served a
2 CONC  term in the Territorial legislature (1866-67). In 1876 he was elected a director of Zion's Co-operative Mercantile Institution, Salt Lake City. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Kaysville he became a man of wealth and affluence and considered himself settled for life when the call came from the First Presidency to take charge of the settlements in southern Arizona Christopher Layton had been set apart 
2 CONC to preside over a new stake to be named St. Joseph in honor of the martyred Prophet, then sent south to colonize a desert whose occasional Mexican and white villages were isolated by Apaches and desolation. Leaving six wives behin
2 CONC d in Utah, Layton moved south with his youngest, Elizabeth, and with $21,000 set to work building an empire in southeastern Arizona. He bought two thousand acres, called the place Thatcher, divided it into lots, and sold them to M
2 CONC ormon settlers. He bought a gristmill, built roads, canals, homes. By 1898 he was seventy-six, ill, and close to death. On June 13, 1898, left for Utah. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: ''Christopher Layton, Colonizer, Statesman, Leader''. Edited by: Myron W. McIntyre, Noel R. Barton. https://byustudies.byu.edu/showTitle.aspx?title=8125
2 CONT
