2 CONT === BIOGRAPHY;   ===
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY;  
2 CONT Christopher Layton was born March 8, 1821 in Thorncut, Bedforshire, England.  Christopher Layton was the youngest son of Samuel and Isabella Wheeler Layton.  He was baptized into the Latter-day Saint Church January 1, 1842.  On Ju
2 CONC ly 10th of that year he married Mary Matthews and the following January they, with other converts, left England for America. Mr. Layton joined the Mormon Battaliion and was listed as private No. 47 in Company "C."  While the Batta
2 CONC lion was stationed at San Diego Christopher Layton carried the mail to Los Angeles and back until companies C.A.D. and E. were called to hold Los Angeles.  It took the compaines four days to make the trip along the seashore.  Thei
2 CONC r provisions were gone, so they dispatched on 8-mule team for a fresh supply of food from San Diego.  Christopher drove that team.  On April 6 a petition was circulated asking for their discharge, but it was not granted until July
2 CONC  16th.  At that time five companies of teh Battalion were formed in line.  Lt  Smith then marched down and back between the lines saying, "You are discharged."  No thanks or appreciation was tiven after all they had goine through,
2 CONC  but the brevity of it all pleased the men. In the spring of 1849 Christopher Layton went to Sutter's ranch and worked at the gold diggings, but not liking that type of work he stayed only three days.  From there he went back to S
2 CONC outhern Carlifornia, bought a band of horses for $1.50 each and started for San Francisco.  On the way he sold some of the horsed for as high as $100 each.  With the money received he bought a ticket to England to visit his loved 
2 CONC ones.  Two months after his arrival he married an old friend, Sarah Martin.  He returned to Utah. On September 25, 1852 he married Sarah Barned.  Later he married Isagella Golightly and, two years after, Caroline Cooper became his
2 CONC  wife on April 12, 1856.  The town of Layton was named in his honor.  Christopher Layton died in August 1898.
2 CONT
2 CONT -- The Mormon Battalion, Kate B. Carter assisted by Clara B. Steele, Daughters of Utah Pioneers, Utah Printing Co., Utah, USA, 1956.
2 CONT === BIOGRAPHY 2:   ===
2 CONT Due to family financial reverses, Christopher began to work outside the home at age eight. The thirty-three cents a week he earned was used to help support his family. On 1 January 1842 he was baptized and ordained a priest in The
2 CONC  Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints in Thornecote, England, by Samuel Howard.
2 CONT In January 1843 Christopher immigrated to America on the sailing vessel Swanton with a company of Latter-day Saints under the direction of Lorenzo Snow. In Nauvoo, Hancock, Illinois, he met the Prophet Joseph Smith on the banks of
2 CONC  the Mississippi River. He underwent trying ordeals and persecutions in the Nauvoo area from 1843 to 1846. During this time he served as a temple guard. He was ordained an elder in 1845 (Jenson, LDS Biographical Encyclopedia, 1:36
2 CONC 3). He was ordained a seventy, received his endowment, and was sealed to his wife, Mary, on 31 January 1846 in the Nauvoo Temple (Nauvoo Temple Register). Religious persecution forced them to flee from Illinois to Iowa Territory. 
2 CONC In Council Bluffs, Christopher enlisted in the Mormon Battalion. He marched with the battalion from Council Bluffs to Ciudad de los Angeles. In October 1846 he was on duty as a teamster (Muster Roll). He was discharged on 16 July 
2 CONC 1 847. After his discharge he searched for gold near Sutter's Fort until 1849. He then crossed the United States from California to the east coast, and from there to England. Eventually, he returned to America and resided in St. L
2 CONC ouis, St. Louis, Missouri, for two years before crossing the plains to the Salt Lake Valley. In the valley Christopher reported a real wealth of $1,000 and a personal wealth of $3,800 by 1850 (Utah Federal Census, 1850). In 1852 h
2 CONC e and his families resided in West Jordan, Salt Lake County, where he operated two butcher shops. With family responsibilities pressing, his real and personal wealth began to diminish (Utah Federal Census, 1860). However, his invo
2 CONC lvement in community and ecclesiastical responsibilities increased. In 1862 he was a delegate to the Utah State Constitutional Convention. From 1862 to 1877 he served as bishop of the Kaysville Ward. He was a lifetime member of th
2 CONC e Deseret Agricultural and Manufacturing Society. In addition, he served in the Utah Territorial Legislature from 1867 to 1868, as director of the Utah Central Railroad, as director of the Kaysville ZCMI, and as an owner of a sawm
2 CONC ill. He was also responsible for the Church sheep herd in 1872. From 1877 to 1880 he served both as a counselor in the Davis Stake presidency and as president of the Farmer's Union (Jenson, LDS Biographical Encyclopedia, 1:363). L
2 CONC aws against plural marriage forced Christopher into exile in Arizona, where he served as president of the St. Joseph Stake from 1880 to 1898. During these years he founded Thatcher, Graham County, and bought lands for the Church i
2 CONC n Mexico. In 1898 he was ordained a patriarch (Journal, Christopher Layton). He died after a long illness.
2 CONT === Please use  PID: LC7H-9W9 as the correct Christopher Layton. ===
2 CONT Christopher Layton is in the database several times, already. Please do not use this one but rather use PID: LC7H-9W9.  It is the most correct family unit.
2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT NOTE:  This synopsis of the life of Christopher Layton is taken from the Autobiography of Christopher Layton.  The information has been condensed and includes summaries which I have added of some time periods.
2 CONT
2 CONT “My father, Samuel Layton, married Isabell Wheeler, by I whom he had six children, namely, Bathsheba, John, Priscilla, Susanna, Christopher, and Avis. I was born in the small village of Thorncote, Northill, Bedfordshire, England, 
2 CONC on March 8, 1821. There were no schools in our village so I had no chance for an education; and as my parents were poor, I was obliged to help in supporting the family by working while very young. My first employment was keeping c
2 CONC rows off the wheat fields, and I was paid thirty-six cents a week and boarded myself. This was when I was only seven years old. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “When I was eight years old, I went to work for a Mr. Fuller on a farm. His horses were large and when I stood by them I was only as high as their shoulders, and when I put the collars and harness on them I was obliged to stand on
2 CONC  the plowbeam to reach their backs. My main work was plowing, and here I stayed for several years. Next I worked for a well-to-do farmer named Sargent, who lived eight miles from Bedfordshire. He employed me as a foreman on his la
2 CONC rge farm, and gave me a good salary. In this position I stayed until I joined the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, for which I received my discharge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “I was at this time keeping company with an excellent girl, Mary Matthews, both of us belonging to the Wesleyan Methodist Church. A man named Sam Howard, who was an elder in the Latter-day Saints Church, told me that he had a reve
2 CONC lation that there would be four persons baptized at a meeting, and I was one of them. I laughed at the idea, but he cordially invited me to attend a meeting and 1 went out of curiosity mostly. When I heard the gospel I believed.  
2 CONC Mary Matthews also attended these meetings, and we went into the waters of baptism together.  I was confirmed and ordained a priest. I worked on Mr. Coleman’s large farm from this time until I came to America. . 
2 CONT
2 CONT “On June 20, 1842, Mary Matthews and I were married at Northill, Bedfordshire, England, by Reverend John Taddy, and on January 1, 1843, we left Thorncote with Mr. Coleman’s family, in a large baggage wagon en route for America. Le
2 CONC aving our wagons at Wolverhampton, we went by train to Liverpool where we joined other Saints and were with the  nineteenth company of Latter-day Saint emigrants, with Lorenzo Snow as the company’s captain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “On January 16, 1843, we set sail from Liverpool, and as we slowly saw the land disappear in the distance we sang one of the songs of Zion and cheered each other with sympathizing words.  We had a pleasant voyage across the Atlant
2 CONC ic, during which time just before reaching the American shore, Mary gave birth to a little son, whom we named William Matthews Layton. We sailed for seven weeks and three days before we arrived at New Orleans and were transferred 
2 CONC to the steamer Amaranth in which we sailed up the Mississippi River. Our baby died before we reached St. Louis, being only about six weeks old. It was buried on shore. We arrived at St. Louis Mar. 29, 1843. We were now transferred
2 CONC  from the steamer to a barge, and here we had to stay two weeks waiting for the ice to break up in the river. About the seventh or eighth of April a small steamer fastened a cable on to our barge and tugged us up the river to Nauv
2 CONC oo where we arrived one very cold morning, April 12. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “How rejoiced we were when we were safely across! And there stood our Prophet on the banks of the river to welcome us! As he heartily grasped our hands, the fervently spoken words, “God bless you,” sank deep into our hearts, givin
2 CONC g us a feeling of peace such as we had never known before. Then Brother Philemon C. Merrill took my wife and me home with him; his wife Cyrena gave me the first cup of milk I had in Nauvoo. On the following day the Prophet Joseph 
2 CONC called to see us and blessed us. After staying with Brother and Sister Merrill a few days my wife, who was still sick, and I went home with Jacob Butterfield. The first work I did in Nauvoo was digging a well for Brother Wilson, w
2 CONC orking with Brother John Marriott, for which I received cash. The Lord had truly blessed me as the Prophet had said, for I had many friends and brothers, and although I had come to Nauvoo with only eight cents in my pocket I was n
2 CONC ow able to pay my debts and be a free man again, and always had work. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “Brother Marriott and I went in with some more of the brethren to build a house each, but as we thought we were not fairly dealt with and were not satisfied with the apportionment of community property, we drew out and told Joseph
2 CONC  about it. After counseling and instructing us he gave us two and a half acres of land each and said, “You shall live to see the day when you can buy out everyone who has oppressed you.” This prophecy has come true, as has all tha
2 CONC t noble man ever uttered concerning me. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “In the spring of 1844 I became acquainted with some of the apostles and had many friends among the Saints, for we loved each other and shielded and protected Joseph as much as lay in our power. One of my duties was to guard the a
2 CONC uthorities and also to help guard the Temple. During the months of May and June the life of the Prophet Joseph was harassed. But the work of the Church was still carried on in Nauvoo, for in May missionaries were set apart for a m
2 CONC ission to England and the apostles were scattered over the Eastern States. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “On the sixteenth of June Brother Joseph preached to the assembled Saints in the grove east of the Temple, while the rain fell heavily. After the city had been declared under martial law, the Legion was drawn up in front of the Ma
2 CONC nsion House and the Prophet, standing upon the framework of a building opposite, addressed them. He asked us if we loved him, if we would stand by him and sustain the laws of our country? And we all answered, “Yes, yes.” Then he s
2 CONC aid he was content; he would die for us. “I love you, my brethren; greater love hath no man than that he lay down his life for his friends; you have stood by me in the hour of trouble, and I am willing to sacrifice my life for you
2 CONC r preservation.” Then drawing his sword, “I call God and angels to witness that this people shall have their legal rights or my blood shall be spilt upon the ground … and my body consigned to the tomb, but if there is one drop of 
2 CONC blood shed on this occasion, the sword shall never again be sheathed until Christ comes to reign over the earth …. Peace shall be taken from the land which permits these crimes against the Saints to go unavenged. May God bless you
2 CONC  forever and ever.” And we all answered, “Amen.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT “On the twentieth of June Brother Joseph sent word to all the apostles to return home immediately.  At this time I was living at Big Mound, an English settlement about eight miles from Nauvoo, and there, while engaged in putting i
2 CONC n sod corn, I heard of the Prophet’s and Hyrum’s death. The next morning I started to Carthage with those who went after the bodies. We met them on the road, Dr. Richards having dressed the wounds of John Taylor and started for Na
2 CONC uvoo with Joseph’s and Hyrum’s bodies. . The wailing of the Saints when they saw the martyrs was terrible.  The bodies were placed in coffins, the funeral was held, while deep grief filled our hearts and sorrow rested heavily upon
2 CONC  us, a stricken people.  On August 6 the apostles arrived from the East. We were still uncertain about choosing a guardian of the Church, and it was a great relief to greet them among us. A council of the priesthood was called, an
2 CONC d it was not long before, with the Twelve at the head, we felt that all things would be managed and directed aright. In the person of the President of the Twelve, Brigham Young, we knew that a great character had arisen to build u
2 CONC pon the foundation laid by Joseph Smith  a kingdom whose equal “there never was in the world.” Now feeling at peace, we pursued our usual work. The work on the Temple was pushed forward as rapidly as possible. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “On August 17, 1844, a little daughter was born to us, at Nauvoo, and we named her Elizabeth. In the fall of 1844, I moved to LaHarpe, Illinois. John Marriott and I worked for Mr. White, and in the spring of 1845 we rented the far
2 CONC m from him and put in a crop of corn and had a good yield. Four of us took up one hundred and sixty acres of land and divided it between us; I built a house on my forty acres one mile from the Mound. An old lady was living on one 
2 CONC corner of my forty, and she was taken sick with typhoid fever. It seemed too bad for her to be there all alone, and I asked my wife to go and take care of her. She said she had thought about it, but did not know how I would get al
2 CONC ong with the cooking work, etc.; but I told her to go, I’d get along all right. So she went, but the lady soon died, and then Mary was taken down with the same fever. I moved her to Samuel Payne’s house where she could have better
2 CONC  care, but in September, 1845, she quietly passed away from us. I walked three miles but could get no lumber and was obliged to take a log, and I helped to hew a coffin out of that; then I carried it back on my shoulders; then, wi
2 CONC th three teams, we went to Nauvoo and buried her. Thus I was left alone with my little girl of thirteen months. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “Among my neighbors were two good friends, Wm. B. Smith and his excellent wife, who had no children, and they took my baby and cared for her as tenderly as they could have done for their own; they learned to love her so dearly and
2 CONC  she became so attached to them that they could not give her up, and she remained in their family until she was married.  
2 CONT
2 CONT “In January, 1845, the legislature, yielding to popular clamor, had repealed the charter of the city of Nauvoo. We now had no protection whatever. In the spring I was ordained an Elder.  On April 8 Brigham Young received a letter 
2 CONC of advice from Governor Ford of Illinois, sa Samuel Layton born 1789 BIOGRAPHY: Daughters of Utah Pioneer submission:  Brief History of Samuel and Isabella Wheeler Layton turned in to the Daughters of Utah Pioneers:  Samuel Layton
2 CONC  was born October 4, 1789 to John Leigh
2 CONT === <Picture><Picture>
2 CONT
2 CONT The Iowa Trek
2 CONT
2 CONT <Pict ===
2 CONT <Picture><Picture>
2 CONT
2 CONT The Iowa Trek
2 CONT
2 CONT <Picture>
2 CONT
2 CONT Thursday, September 17, 1846
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Cutler's Park, Nebraska:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the morning, Brigham Young, Heber C. Kimball, Wilford Woodruff, and Alpheus Cutler went to look at land for farming near the new location for the ferry. Afterwards, they visited Brigham Young's brother, John, who was still quit
2 CONC e sick. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the evening, a joint-council meeting was held. A rule was put in place that no cattle should be taken from the herdsmen without an order. Jedediah M.
2 CONT Grant reported that Peter Sarpy had no objection with the plans to move the ferry to the north, near Cutler's Park. He also relayed the alarming report from Mr. Sarpy that a United States Marshal from Missouri was on the way to Co
2 CONC uncil Bluffs to arrest the Twelve. Mr. Sarpy promised to send a warning as soon has the marshal arrived. Brother Grant also reported that the Secretary of War had instructed the Indian agent, Robert Mitchell to have all the Mormon
2 CONC s removed from the Pottawatomie lands (east side of the Missouri River) by April, 1847. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The council meeting was adjourned, but another small private meeting was held to discuss what actions should be taken if the marshal did try to arrest the Twelve. It was decided to have Hosea Stout send two spies over to Council B
2 CONC luffs to see what was going on. Brigham Young also expressed his wish to have the Nauvoo Legion put into operation as soon as possible. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the evening, Brigham Young and Willard Richards worked on drafts of the design plot for Winter Quarters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mormon Battalion on the Santa Fe Trail, Kansas:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mormon Battalion set off for a long 90-mile march across the Cimarron Desert. As Levi Hancock looked back to the east where the star had been seen the night before, he saw John D. Lee and Howard Egan on the horizon catching up
2 CONC  with the battalion. They were on their mission to obtain the pay from the battalion members. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother Lee asked that the battalion stop their march so that letters could be read from Brigham Young. He was astonished to learn that Lt. Smith was commanding the battalion instead of Jefferson Hunt. Adjutant Dykes told Brother 
2 CONC Lee that they didn't have time to stop because they had to cross the desert. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John D. Lee became outraged when he learned of the terrible treatment of the sick. He learned of Alva Phelps death the day before which was blamed on the forced treatment of calomel only hours before his death. He wrote, "When I c
2 CONC ame up with the Bat. & saw the suffering & oppression of these Soldiers my blood boiled in my veins to such an extent that I could scarce refrain from taking my Sword in hand & ridding them of such Tyrants." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother Lee invited Lt. Smith and Jefferson Hunt to ride in his wagon and proceeded to verbally attach Lt. Smith. He accused him of inhumane treatment and abuse that was comparable to the persecution endured by the Saints in Misso
2 CONC uri. "I consider the oppression here as great as it was in Mo. They would say if you don't renounce Mormonism dam you I'll kill you. You way to them, if you don't take calomel, I'll cut your damned throats. I see no difference at 
2 CONC all." 
2 CONT
2 CONT He accused them of killing Alva Phelps. "Not withstanding his entreaties, the Dr. poured an even spoonful of calomel down him & about twice that amaount of spirits of turpentine, which ended his career." Lt. Smith replied that he 
2 CONC was not responsible for what the doctor did. Brother Lee was outraged and related reports about the Lt. threatening to cut men's throats if they didn't take the medicine and then he knew that the doctor purposely had administered 
2 CONC double portions of calomel "through spite saying that he did not care a dam whether it killed or cured and the more killed the better that the dam rascals out to be sent to hell as fast as possible." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, Brother Lee threatened mutiny, stating that the Mormons were "ready to revolt & it is with much difficulty that they can be constrained from rising up & bursting off the yoke of oppression." At the close of the tongue las
2 CONC hing, Brother Lee expected Lt. Smith to challenge him for a duel, but instead the Lieutenant simply walked away. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The enlisted men were on the side of John D. Lee. Henry Standage wrote, "The star last night that was seen moving was an omen of the arrival of the messengers in as much as the officers were consenting to almost anything that Lieu
2 CONC  Smith our Tyrant would propose. But we sill call upon the Lord to protect us." 
2 CONT
2 CONT The battalion marched on across twenty-six miles of desert. Christopher Layton noted that they kept seeing mirages, "It had the appearance of fog rising from water and then would look like a lake of clear water, but it went on ahe
2 CONC ad of us and stopped when we did." Henry Standage added, "We traveled...across one of the most dreary deserts that ever man saw, suffering much from the intense heat of the sun and for want of water." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Water was extremely scares and John D. Lee was appalled to watch men have to rush up to filthy puddles and strain the water through their teeth to keep the insects and mud from being swallowed. These puddles even contained buffalo
2 CONC  droppings. They would fill their canteens out of water in the tracks of oxen and mules. Christopher Layton wrote, "We put the water in a vessel and then sucked it through a silk handkerchief." Men started to become sick and colla
2 CONC pse on the trail. Brother Lee loaded up his wagon with three or four sick men who didn't have the strength to walk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A large number of buffalo were seen during the day and two of them came running near the line of men. Thirty or so muskets were fired which stopped and killed them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nauvoo, Illinois:
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout the night and during the morning, the Saints were fleeing across the river before the mob was to take possession of the city in the afternoon. Even many of the non-Mormon citizens left, especially those who participated
2 CONC  in the battle. They had no confidence that the terms of the treaty would be upheld by the mob. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor Ford's official observer, Mason Brayman, reported, "In every part of the city scenes of destitution, misery and woe met the eye." Families were leaving their homes without shelter, without tents, without even a day's prov
2 CONC isions, taking as much as they could carry in their hands. The sick were carried in their beds. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The formal treaty was signed during the morning and at about 3:00 p.m., the mob forces numbering between 1500-2000 marched into the city yelling, hooting and howling. Thomas Bullock arose from his sickbed and watched them march al
2 CONC ong Mullholland Street. He recorded, "I never heard [such howling] from men, or even the wild savages of the forest...terror and dismay surely for once overcame the sick, the poor women and children...such an awful and infuriated 
2 CONC noise I never again heard....We expected that an indiscriminate massacre was commencing. I, with others who were sick, was carried into the tall weeds and woods, while all who could, hid themselves; many crossed the river, leaving
2 CONC  everything behind." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel H. Wells reported, "As the mob came in, we left 2 blocks in advance. We met many of the Saints on this side of the river in distress & it drew tears from the eyes of some of the mob." They marched to the temple and received
2 CONC  the keys from the leader of the Quincy Committee. They marched around the temple and then camped on a field on Parley Street. Thomas Bullock wrote, "When they encamped, some speeches were made and the men yelled and screamed like
2 CONC  Savages. " 
2 CONT
2 CONT The mob ignoree the terms of the treaty and soon sent a company to search the wagons that were on the bank of the river waiting to cross. They took all the guns that they could find. An elderly Brother John Stiles was forced to th
2 CONC e river at the point of a bayonet and baptized face down in the name of Tom Sharp, a leader of the mob and editor of the Warsaw Signal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They entered the homes of Nauvoo Trustees, John S. Fullmer and Joseph L. Heywood and seized anything that looked like arms or ammunition. As they made this search they threatened the families. They also defiled the temple with dru
2 CONC nkenness, gambling and filthy song. Joseph Fielding wrote with sadness regarding the desecration of the temple. "They rendezvoused in the [Nauvoo] temple. We had guarded it by night and day, a long time feeling unwilling to leave 
2 CONC it in their hands, but they now had it to themselves. They even preached in it and cursed the Saints, but did no great damage to it, thinking it would add to the value of their property." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Ashby recorded, "At night we could hear the sound of the bell and the bass drum from the tower of the temple where the mob were carousing after banishing from their pleasant homes, innocent men, women and children to peri
2 CONC sh in the wilderness among tribes of savage Indians." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elden Watson (ed.), Manuscript History of Brigham Young, 389-90 Juanita Brooks, John Doyle Lee, 98-99 Juanita Brooks, Mormon Battalion Mission, 206 John F. Yurtinus, A Ram in the Thicket:  The Mormon Battalion in the Mexican War, 
2 CONC 140-44 Dan Talbot, A Historical Guide to the Mormon Battalion and Butterfield Trail, 26 Journal Extracts of Henry W. Bigler, Utah Historical Quarterly, 5:2:40 Journal of Henry Standage in Frank Alfred Golder, The March of the Morm
2 CONC on Battalion, 165-66 Juanita Brooks (ed.), On the Mormon Frontier, The Diary of Hosea Stout 1844-1861, 194 B. H. Roberts, Comprehensive History of the Church, Vol.3, Ch.70, p.16 - p.17 Louise Lyman Nielson and Dora Dutson Flack, T
2 CONC he Dutson Family History Our Pioneer Heritage, Vol. 8, p.234 Wandle Mace Autobiography, typescript, BYU-S, p.207 Russell R. Rich, Ensign to the Nations, 45 Joseph Fielding Diary in "Nauvoo Journal," BYU Studies 19 (1979), p.165 - 
2 CONC p.166 Wilford Woodruff's Journal, 3:88 Thomas Bullock Poor Camp Journal in Will Bagley (ed)., Pioneer Camp of the Saints, Sept 17, 1846. Benjamin Ashby Autobiography, copy of holograph, BYU-S, p.16
2 CONT
