1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Lucinda was the daughter of Rebecca Ann Sanders and Henry Weeks Sanderson. She was the youngest of nine girls and four of the five brothers. Lucinda was born in Fairview August 19, 1872. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucinda married John A, Anderson December 17, 1890 in Manti, Utah. Their first child, a beautiful little girl named Ora Lucinda Anderson, died very suddenly with pneumonia; then came John Clifton Anderson, William Calvin Anderson 
2 CONC and Buena May Anderson.  
2 CONT
2 CONT After their marriage the young couple lived in a small house in the middle of the block of her parents' lot until they built their own neat red brick home on the northwest corner adjoining Lucinda's parents' lot. After about fifte
2 CONC en years John and Lucinda built a lovely home, consisting of nine rooms (walk-in clothes closets in five bedrooms). The house was of white brick. It is at this time (1970) one of the best-built houses in Fairview, Because the youn
2 CONC g people in Fairview liked to hear our player piano, it was often the gathering place on Sunday evenings after church for all ages—and home for some of the relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The family went to the sheep camp with their father to spend a few days in October. The men were working with the sheep some distance away from the wagon and tent. The children were playing close by. Mother noticed something was 
2 CONC wrong. Soon Clifton and Calvin began having convulsions. Lucinda would question them between convulsions. They said they had found a bottle which they thought contained sugar, and they ate some of it. The bottle had fallen out of 
2 CONC one of the herder's pockets. Mother screamed for help, but the men were too far away. Some time later a man on horseback heard mother's screams and responded, but it was too late. Calvin was dead. Clifton gradually got better. Bue
2 CONC na must have been napping. She, at two and half years of age remembers playing around the sheep wagon with her brothers, John Clifton and William Calvin, six and four years of age. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In due time Eva Allowee Anderson was born; later David Lyle Anderson; then shortly before they moved from our red brick home (preparatory to tearing it down) Owen Leslie Anderson arrived—a cute little black-headed boy. They called
2 CONC  him our Indian. I think Ethan Olaf Anderson was born before we moved into a rented house. Lucinda's mother was near death, but before she died she had mother promise that we would move into her house until ours was completed.
2 CONT
2 CONT  John, Lucinda, and baby Ethan and Eva went to Salt Lake to attend Conference. The day they arrived home, Buena came down with the measles. Ethan, on his one-year birthday, became very sick with the measles. A new doctor had come 
2 CONC to town. When Ethan died, the doctor was told that Lyle had recovered from diphtheria only a short time before. The doctor immediately went after antitoxin for diphtheria and gave every one in the house shots. Owen, now the baby (
2 CONC 28 months) got several times the required dosage. Mother and Eva got more than they should have had. All were bedfast and very ill—eyes swollen shut—bodies black and blue. Not knowing the new doctor at the time, we soon learned th
2 CONC at he used dope. When he found what he had done he quickly left Fairview, no one knowing his whereabouts until years later. During Ethan's illness we had Aunt Delia Bonnie, a well-known family nurse, with us.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Edwin Max was born January 26, 1909, and for six years they had our sweet smiling son with us. He died March 17, 1915. Next our little daughter, Jessie Matilda, was born March 17, 1911. She and Robert Allen Anderson were a joy to
2 CONC  our family. Especially Lucinda and John enjoyed them through the years. It was while John was in Lucern Valley the first time that he met a lost cousin named Robert Allen. Their Robert Allen was born July 14, 1915. John returned 
2 CONC and told Lucinda that he would like to name the boy Robert Allen. Lucinda already had the name Robert in mind. Before Bob, Von Archibald came to them—on February 22, 1913. While still a very young baby he caught whooping cough, an
2 CONC d many times they thought that he was gone. He would choke, turn blue, and before they could bring him to the kids would have time to run to the field for John. John would douse him under the cold water tap and then under the warm
2 CONC , toss him in the air, roll him on the lawn; by that time the doctor would arrive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucinda with the chore boy, would drive the home stock about two blocks to the creek for water. As Clifton grew big enough, much was expected of him. Later, John hired regular sheepherders who went to the west desert in the winter
2 CONC . Clifton, Hyrum, Albert Peterson (perhaps Otto Peterson) and others, besides John's regular sheepherders, spent short periods with the sheep.
2 CONT
2 CONT  John was a man who put trust in people. Sometimes they would take advantage of him. Such was the case when John was shown beautiful pictures of Lucern Valley. The growth on the land looked so inviting that father and Clifton took
2 CONC  a trip to Wyoming. John thought that he was leaving his sheep in charge of two reliable herders—one who had worked for years for John. However, on his return home he discovered that between six and eight hundred sheep were missin
2 CONC g. The camp was situated between Fairview and the mining towns around Price, Utah. It would have been an easy matter to sell the sheep and drive them to the railroad for shipment to eastern cities, or butcher them and sell to the 
2 CONC markets. This was a discouraging experience. John decided to discontinue the business in which he had spent most of his life. He sold the sheep that were left and bought cattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT  John bought the Birch Springs ranch from Joe Duncan in what is now called McKinnon, Wyoming. The cattle were shipped to the ranch. The first winter proved to be a hard one for the stock. Deep snow, cold winds, wet weather, and th
2 CONC e lack of feed (which was very costly) proved disastrous to the animals.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Our Fairview home was sold to the station agent, Harry Rasmusson. Later Mr. Rasmusson moved away from Fairview and it was sold to Cleon Anderson, a nephew of Lucinda's. The dry farm was sold to Albert Madison. Their farm land was
2 CONC  sold, and in early spring of 1921 the family moved from Fairview to McKinnon, leaving many relatives and life-long friends. John and Lucinda did much, as did their parents and grandparents before them, in building up the Fairview
2 CONC  community. John served in many different capacities, business and otherwise, such as city councilman. He was extremely generous with donations and helped whenever and wherever help was needed. John was chosen to be a counselor in
2 CONC  the McKinnon Ward bishopric, where he served faithfully with three different bishops for many years. He completed a stake mission in Lyman and Mountain View, Wyoming. He served on the school board and was a member of the irrigati
2 CONC on and reservoir company, using men and teams to build and keep reservoirs and irrigation ditches in repair. He was instrumental in the erection of the McKinnon Ward chapel, and be was one member who stayed on the job until he saw
2 CONC  its completion and dedication.
2 CONT
2 CONT  John, Lucinda, and all their children appreciate the friendship, love, and help given to their family. Our McKinnon friends and those nearby have been many staunch and true leaders and friends. There have been times when true fri
2 CONC endship has been needed in the John A. Anderson family, and neighbors and friends have always responded. One such time was when after years of toil, their house burned to the ground. This was a time when we really appreciated true
2 CONC  and loyal friends. There are also many other instances.
2 CONT
2 CONT - Mrs. Buena A. Tillotson -Anderson, Buena. History of John A Anderson & Lucinda Sanderson. Courtesy of www.mckinnonwyoming.com
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT MCKINNON, WYOMING--Mrs. Lucinda Sanderson Anderson, 88, McKinnon, died Thursday in a Rock Springs hospital of causes incident to age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born August 19, 1872, Fairview, Utah, to Henry W. and Rebecca Sanders Sanderson. Married to John A. Anderson December 17, 1890, Manti, Utah Temple, Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints. He died June 9, 1959, Fairview reside
2 CONC nt until 1921, when moved to McKinnon where resided since. Member LDS Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors: five sons, three daughters, John Clifton, David L., Owen L., Von A., Mrs. Crystal W. (Jessie) Youngberg, all McKinnon; Robert A., Mrs. Porter S. (Buena) Tillotson, both Salt Lake City; Mrs. James A. (Eva) Watson, Spring
2 CONC  City, Utah; 28 grandchildren, 60 great-grandchildren, sister, Mrs. Hyrum Fistcher, California. Funeral at McKinnon. Graveside services Monday, Salt Lake City Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Salt Lake Tribune, March 10, 1961, transcribed by Rhonda Holton
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=25491649 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 13 2016, 19:08:12 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
