2 CONT
2 CONT Lucinda with the chore boy, would drive the home stock about two blocks to the creek for water. As Clifton grew big enough, much was expected of him. Later, John hired regular sheepherders who went to the west desert in the winter
2 CONC . Clifton, Hyrum, Albert Peterson (perhaps Otto Peterson) and others, besides John's regular sheepherders, spent short periods with the sheep.
2 CONT
2 CONT  John was a man who put trust in people. Sometimes they would take advantage of him. Such was the case when John was shown beautiful pictures of Lucern Valley. The growth on the land looked so inviting that father and Clifton took
2 CONC  a trip to Wyoming. John thought that he was leaving his sheep in charge of two reliable herders—one who had worked for years for John. However, on his return home he discovered that between six and eight hundred sheep were missin
2 CONC g. The camp was situated between Fairview and the mining towns around Price, Utah. It would have been an easy matter to sell the sheep and drive them to the railroad for shipment to eastern cities, or butcher them and sell to the 
2 CONC markets. This was a discouraging experience. John decided to discontinue the business in which he had spent most of his life. He sold the sheep that were left and bought cattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT  John bought the Birch Springs ranch from Joe Duncan in what is now called McKinnon, Wyoming. The cattle were shipped to the ranch. The first winter proved to be a hard one for the stock. Deep snow, cold winds, wet weather, and th
2 CONC e lack of feed (which was very costly) proved disastrous to the animals.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Our Fairview home was sold to the station agent, Harry Rasmusson. Later Mr. Rasmusson moved away from Fairview and it was sold to Cleon Anderson, a nephew of Lucinda's. The dry farm was sold to Albert Madison. Their farm land was
2 CONC  sold, and in early spring of 1921 the family moved from Fairview to McKinnon, leaving many relatives and life-long friends. John and Lucinda did much, as did their parents and grandparents before them, in building up the Fairview
2 CONC  community. John served in many different capacities, business and otherwise, such as city councilman. He was extremely generous with donations and helped whenever and wherever help was needed. John was chosen to be a counselor in
2 CONC  the McKinnon Ward bishopric, where he served faithfully with three different bishops for many years. He completed a stake mission in Lyman and Mountain View, Wyoming. He served on the school board and was a member of the irrigati
2 CONC on and reservoir company, using men and teams to build and keep reservoirs and irrigation ditches in repair. He was instrumental in the erection of the McKinnon Ward chapel, and be was one member who stayed on the job until he saw
2 CONC  its completion and dedication.
2 CONT
2 CONT  John, Lucinda, and all their children appreciate the friendship, love, and help given to their family. Our McKinnon friends and those nearby have been many staunch and true leaders and friends. There have been times when true fri
2 CONC endship has been needed in the John A. Anderson family, and neighbors and friends have always responded. One such time was when after years of toil, their house burned to the ground. This was a time when we really appreciated true
2 CONC  and loyal friends. There are also many other instances.
2 CONT
2 CONT - Mrs. Buena A. Tillotson
2 CONT -Anderson, Buena. History of John A Anderson & Lucinda Sanderson. Courtesy of www.mckinnonwyoming.com
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT MCKINNON, WYOMING--Mrs. Lucinda Sanderson Anderson, 88, McKinnon, died Thursday in a Rock Springs hospital of causes incident to age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born August 19, 1872, Fairview, Utah, to Henry W. and Rebecca Sanders Sanderson. Married to John A. Anderson December 17, 1890, Manti, Utah Temple, Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints. He died June 9, 1959, Fairview reside
2 CONC nt until 1921, when moved to McKinnon where resided since. Member LDS Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors: five sons, three daughters, John Clifton, David L., Owen L., Von A., Mrs. Crystal W. (Jessie) Youngberg, all McKinnon; Robert A., Mrs. Porter S. (Buena) Tillotson, both Salt Lake City; Mrs. James A. (Eva) Watson, Spring
2 CONC  City, Utah; 28 grandchildren, 60 great-grandchildren, sister, Mrs. Hyrum Fistcher, California. Funeral at McKinnon. Graveside services Monday, Salt Lake City Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Salt Lake Tribune, March 10, 1961, transcribed by Rhonda Holton* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/25491649/lucinda_anderson Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jul 
2 CONC 29 2024, 16:54:35 UTC''
