1 OCCU In 1918 working as a Laborer for Ida Irrigation Company in gooding, Idaho
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Enoch Trane Hargraves was obviously named after his father's brother of the same name who was born in 1856 as his parents were departing Europe for America on board the ship Enoch Train.  In the 1900 U.S. Federal C
2 CONC ensus for Bancroft, Bannock County, Idaho, Enoch T. Hargrave, was 2 years old, but the census taker listed his birth date as March, 1888.  She obviously must have meant 1898.  He was living with his parents and siblings who were l
2 CONC isted as follows in the census:  Samuel, 35, Mary Emily, 33, Eliza Jane, 14, Elizabeth, 9, Agnes, 5, Lewis A., 3, and also living in their home was Christian Mortensen, 23, a boarder.  Enoch's father was born in Utah, and his fath
2 CONC er in Scotland and mother in England.  His mother was born in Idaho, her father in New York and her mother in Utah.  All of the children were born in Utah, as was Mr. Mortensen.  Enoch's father was working as a farmer as was the b
2 CONC oarder, Mr. Mortensen, whose parents were both born in Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1910 the family was living in Pocatello, Bannock County, Idaho, and were listed as follows:  Samuel, 41, Emily, 36, Lewis, 13, Trane, 11, and older sister, Elizabeth "Bessie" Hargrave Stewart, 29, and her 9 month old son, Cliff
2 CONC ord Stewart, but not her husband Elmer Stewart.  Also in the home were two boarders, Mr. J. B. Ripliner and Miss Frances McNally, 27.  Enoch Trane's parents had been married for 14 years, and had given birth to 6 children, 5 of wh
2 CONC om were still living.  Samuel was working as a house carpenter, Mary Emily as a dressmaker, and Bessie as a helper in a restaurant.  Mr. Ripliner was working as a tailor and Miss McNally as a laborer in a yarn works.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Enoch Trade Hargraves completed his U.S. World War I Draft Registration Card on September 12, 1918, in Gooding, Idaho, he described himself as being of medium height, with a slender build, blue eyes and brown hair.  He was 18
2 CONC  years old, and stated he was born March 6, 1900, and was working as a laborer for the Ida Irrigation Company in Gooding, Idaho.  He listed his nearest relative as his sister Elizabeth "Bessie" Hargraves Leeper, living in Gooding,
2 CONC  Idaho.  
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a January 15, 1920, census record for Gooding City, Idaho, showing a 20-year old man named Hargraves (no first or middle name), living in a rented home on Main Street with 18-year old Fred Lewis, and they were both born i
2 CONC n Idaho.  They were listed as "inmates" in a "jail", but that information was crossed out and under the column for occupation the word "none" was listed for each of them.  Fred was listed as the head of the household and Hargraves
2 CONC  as a boarder.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a marriage record for Enoch Trane Hargraves who married Edna Faye Miller September 11, 1920, in Lincoln County, Idaho.  However, by the 1930 census, E.T. Hargraves was listed as a prisoner at McNeil Island Penitentiary i
2 CONC n Pierce County, Washington, and he was working as a porter.  He was 30 years old, single, was born in Idaho as were both of his parents.  A record from the penitentiary shows that E.T. Hargraves was received at the prison on May 
2 CONC 16, 1929, and that he had been sentenced May 13, 1929, and his crime was listed as "Vio.N.M.V.T.A."  The term of his sentence was 2 years and 6 months.  He had been tried and convicted in Salt Lake City, Utah.  His full term expir
2 CONC ed November 15, 1931, and his "good time" term expired on May 19, 1931.  He was 29 years old at the time, 5'-7-1/4" tall and weighed 138 and 1/4 pounds.  He had a ruddy complexion and blue eyes, and was born in Bancroft, Idaho Mar
2 CONC ch 6, 1900.  So this must be the same Enoch Trane Hargraves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime between 1924 and 1928, Enoch and Edna must have divorced, because on December 24, 1928, Edna Faye Hargraves married a man named Earl Cantrall, and in the 1930 census they were living in Round Valley, Idaho.  They were lis
2 CONC ted in the census record as follows:  Earl, 39, Edna F., 29, Jack W. Hargraves, 8, (listed as Earl's step-son, and obviously a child Edna had with Enoch Trane Hargraves), and Wallace T. Hargraves, 6, (also listed as Earl's step-so
2 CONC n and another child of Edna and Enoch).  Also living in the home was Lloyd E. Cantrall, 15, and Ruth Z. Cantrall, 10, obviously children Earl had via a previous marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to either a California or a Social Security Death Index record, an Enoch T. Hargraves, born July 17, 1898, in Idaho, passed away October 28, 1962, in San Francisco, California.  There seems to be some discrepancy about h
2 CONC is birth year, being either in 1898, 1899 or 1900, and the month may have been either March or July.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enoch and Edna's son, Jack Wesley Hargraves, was born May 15, 1921, in Montpelier, Bear Lake County, Idaho, 8 months after his parents were married the previous September.  Jack Wesley Hargraves married Margaret Belma Clark, and t
2 CONC hey had a son Ralph Charles Hargraves in 1944.  They all passed away while living in Georgia, Ralph in 1996, Jack in 2006, and Margaret in 2012.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enoch and Edna's son, Wallace Trane Hargraves, born August 17, 1923, passed away in September of 1987 while living in Portland, Oregon.  He married Marion L. Wilson or Marion L. McPherson, in Alameda, California, January 21, 1952.
2 CONC   There was another public record showing a Trane Wallace Hargraves, born August 17, 1925, married to a woman named Lola (perhaps Marion's middle name was Lola?), who passed away September 9, 1987, while living in Multnomah, Orego
2 CONC n.  I believe these two records may be the same person.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wallace may have served in World War II, since there are Washington Passenger and Crew Lists for Wallace Hargraves, born in 1924, traveling from Saipan Mariana Islands and arriving July 8, 1947, in Seattle Washington, aboard the s
2 CONC hip Clarksburg Victory.  He had been serving on the ship for 2 years as a deck/maintenance person, he was 23 years old, and 5'9" tall and weighed 170 pounds.  Wallace may have actually served in the Navy because there are also sev
2 CONC eral records for him traveling in 1951 from Manila to Honolulu via the ship President Cleveland, as well as 7 or 8 other records for him traveling on board other ships during those times.
