1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The following information about Orvin Lutz Emery was written by his son-in-law, Jearld Ross Thompson and was found on Family Search.org.  It is an eloquent and fitting tribute to the man:
2 CONT
2 CONT A Painting of My Father-in-Law, Orvin Lutz Emery
2 CONT
2 CONT Born:  May 28, 1912 - Alta, Wyoming
2 CONT
2 CONT Died:  December 3, 1989 – Salt Lake City, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of:  Laura Hatch Emery
2 CONT
2 CONT To paint an accurate picture of Orvin Lutz Emerywould require a rather large canvas,
2 CONT
2 CONT one made of sturdy material and woven tight, for of such stock was the man himself cut out.
2 CONT
2 CONT I would start with a rosy background to represent his outlook on life and would splash in some colors to signify the joy he brought into people's lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT I would paint a face of kindness, and eyes that twinkle with mischief and from which would radiate lines of laughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT I would paint ears that listened to each person who came to talk with him, and a nose which seemed to lead him  where he was needed.
2 CONT
2 CONT There would be a mouth with a pleasant and ready smile, One which would not let pass an unkind word to a dear one.
2 CONT
2 CONT My portrait of Orvin Emery would show broad shoulders that were capable of bearing life's burdens,
2 CONT
2 CONT Strong arms which would hold and cuddle a crying child or a hurt animal, and hands that would touch lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT There would be strong legs which would carry this loving father and husband many miles as he trudged along on his morning milk delivery runs.
2 CONT
2 CONT And, of course, there would be a heart as big as all outdoors.
2 CONT
2 CONT For this picture I would build a frame of the finest wood,
2 CONT
2 CONT Forming each piece with the same care that he used in shaping his family.
2 CONT
2 CONT I would then hang this portrait in a special place in my heart,
2 CONT
2 CONT To love him, to honor him and to remember him all the days of my life.
