1 OCCU FARMER; AGRICULTURIST: AUTO SALESMAN IN 1930
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Byron Morley Anderson was the son of Frederick Anderson of Denmark and his wife Sarah Ann Anderson of Iowa.  In the 1880 U.S. Federal Census for Manti, Sanpete, Utah, their family was listed in the census as follow
2 CONC s:  Frederick, 29, Sarah Ann, 29, Rosella, 7, Freddie, 4, Byron, 3, and Cordelia, 1 year old.  Frederick was working as a farmer.  He and his parents were born in Denmark, Sarah in Iowa, her father in New York and mother in Ohio. 
2 CONC  All of the children were born in Utah.  
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1900 they were listed as follows in the census:  Frederick, 49, Sarah A., 49, Frederick William, 24, Byron M., 23, Cordelia, 21, Mary C., 17, Lydia E., 13, Emerett, 9, and Ruth, 7.  Fred and Sarah had been married for 28 years,
2 CONC  had given birth to 9 children, 8 of whom were still living.  Frederick had come to America from Denmark in 1853 and was naturalized.  He was still working as a farmer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Living next door was Peter Anderson, 54, and his wife, Esther S., 51, and their children Ross, 21, Franklin D., 18, Eleanore, 14, Matilda, 12, and Peter S., 8 years old.  This was probably Frederick Anderson's older brother and hi
2 CONC s family, since he too was born in Denmark as were his parents.  He and Esther had been married for 33 years, had given birth to 13 children, 9 of whom were still living.  Peter had also come to America from Denmark in 1853 and wa
2 CONC s naturalized.  His wife's father was born in Massachusetts and her mother in New York.  Peter was also working as a farmer and his son, Ross, was working as a school teacher, and Franklin was working as a farm laborer.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the next page of the census, three doors down from Frederick Anderson was Christian Anderson, 56, and his family, Emily, 46, Isabelle L., 21, Christian P., 19, Edwin S., 15, Emily L., 13, Melvin W., 9, Lorence M., 6, Elva G., 5
2 CONC .  Christian was working as a heater engineer and his son, Christian P. was working as a sheep herder.  Next door to Christian was probably his son, Oliver Peterson, 26, and his wife, Christena C., 24, and her brothers, Frederick 
2 CONC Hansen, 17, and Laurence M. Hansen, 19.  Oliver was working as a farmer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Byron Morely Anderson married Eleanor Roseltha Lutz in 1902 in Salt Lake city, Utah.  When Byron Morley Anderson completed his U.S. World War I Draft Registration Card on September 12, 1918, in Albion, Cassia, Idaho, he described 
2 CONC himself as being tall with a stout build, grey eyes and brown hair.  He was 42 years old, having been born August 30, 1876, and was working as a farmer for himself.  He was living in Burley, Cassia, Idaho, with his wife, Rose, and
2 CONC  they already had 9 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1920 census for Heyburn, Minidoka County, Idaho, Byron M. Anderson, 43, and his wife, Rose L., 37, and children, Laurel, 17, Lealand, 15, Lucille, 13, Maurine, 11, Andrea, 10, Don L., 8, Cordelia, 6, DeWane 4 years, 3 month
2 CONC s old, Myrtis, 2 years, 5 months old, and Rulon, 7 months old, were living in a rented house on Riverside Drive and Byron was working as a farmer.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1930, they were living in Salt Lake City, at 146 Third Avenue, a home they owned valued at $5,500 and Byron was working as an automobile salesman.  They were listed in the census as follows:  Byron, 53, Rose L, 47, Laurel R., 2
2 CONC 7, Lucille, 24, Maurine, 22, Andrea, 20, Don L., 19, Cordelia, 17, DeWayne, 14, Myrtis, 13, Rulon, 10, and Betty J., 4 years old.  Laurel was working as a clerk in a dry goods store, Lucille as a sales lady in a dry goods store, M
2 CONC aurine as a bookkeeper in a dry goods store, Andrea as a stenographer in the bonds business, Don as a clerk in a grocery store, and Cordelia as a telephone operator.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sadly, just 9 years later, Byron passed away on July 15, 1939, at the relatively young age of 62 years old due to a pulmonary embolism subsequent to an injury he had when he fell on a slippery walk and fractured his femur bone in 
2 CONC his right leg on May 27, 1939.
2 CONT
2 CONT Byron's widow, Eleanor Roseltha Lutz Anderson lived another 12 years and passed away on December 9, 1951, in Salt Lake City, at the age of 69 years old from broncho-pneumonia and pancreatic cancer with metastases to the liver.  At
2 CONC  the time of her death she had been living at 780 Third Avenue.  She was buried in the Wasatch Lawn Memorial Park Cemetery in Salt Lake City, Utah, on December 10, 1951.  The informant on her death certificate was her daughter, La
2 CONC urel Anderson.
