1 OCCU Accountant for Utah Construction Company in Utah, Arizona, and Kansas
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Obituary:  DeWayne "Duke" Merrill Anderson....Leaving this life behind him with courage and grace, our sweet and witty husband, father, brother and friend, D.M. Anderson, died Saturday, March 26, 1994, at Holy Cros
2 CONC s Hospital of heart failure. He was 78 years old.  Grateful thanks to Dr. Stephen H. Nichols, the nursing and support staff at Holy Cross, and especially to Dr. John C. Luras, whose efforts on Duke's behalf were extraordinary--tho
2 CONC ughtful, compassionate, and imaginative.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born June 12, 1915 in Teton, Idaho, to Byron and Eleanor Roseltha Anderson, Duke spent his childhood in several small towns in southern Idaho before moving with his family to Salt Lake City in 1929.  He was an exceptionally talent
2 CONC ed athlete who excelled in tennis, swimming, and basketball, winning trophies in these competitive sports. In high school, his lovely, reedy tenor occasionally augmented popular local bands.  His final solo, given at the urging of
2 CONC  Holy Cross's nursing staff and characteristic of his sense of humor, was ''Don't Fence Me In.''  He married the irrespressible tomboy, Nancy Hesse, in 1945.
2 CONT
2 CONT Employed by Utah Construction Company, he held positions in Arizona, Utah, and Kansas.  As an accountant, he loved the language of numbers.  Their precision comforted him in a world whose injustices and random cruelty bewildered h
2 CONC im.  He was devoted to his large family, which included three brothers and seven sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survived by the strong, brave, Nancy (the perfect dancing partner despite the more than one-foot difference in their heights); daughters, Susan and Shawn; brother, Rulon of Hilton Head, South Carolina; sisters, Betty Davidson of P
2 CONC ortland, Oregon; and Andrea Whitney of Salt Lake City; 18 nephews and nieces, and many friends.  He took great pleasure in their company.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ace will miss their ongoing conversations about classic American film, especially which was the better Fred Astaire movie, ''Top Hat'' or ''Swing Time.''  Shawn will miss their arguments about the talents of various athletes, abou
2 CONC t whom they never, ever agreed.  Duke was a lover of all music, especially jazz.  He was a handsome, elegant, charming man, who, at his best, was an elation of the spirit. How we'll miss him.
2 CONT
2 CONT A private service will be held Tuesday, March 29th, at 12 noon. Interment at Mount Olivet Cemetery.  Funeral arrangements provided by Evans & Early Mortuary. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Salt Lake Tribune, March 29, 1994, page C8
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE:  Find A Grave.com
