1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Biography of Edith Jane Hilton as told to her Great-Granddaughter Patricia Cheney sometime in late 1950s:  Edith's father was Charles Hewitt Hilton and her mother was Anna Lavina Johnson.  Edith was born 17 Dec 187
2 CONC 1 in Virgin City, Washington, Utah.  She married Franklin Cheney 15 Dec 1886 in Snowflake, Arizona, by the Stake President, and was Endowed 4 Oct. 1911 and sealed to her husband that day.  When Edith was four years old her father 
2 CONC died.  Consequently she was raised with her four aunts of approximately her same age.  Her mother had gone to take care of her grandmother during this time.  When she was nine she moved to Arizona, and on that journey her mother d
2 CONC rove a horse team in the company which was taking herds of cattle and sheep to Arizona for the Church. 	
2 CONT Edith had an organ when she was young and played very well.  When she was older, she was called on often to be chorister at church.  Her schooling went only to the eighth grade because she was married when she was 15 years old.  E
2 CONC dith and her husband moved quite often.  He worked in a Mill and farmed.  After her marriage Edith was a Relief Society teacher and taught Sunday School and always sang in the church choir.	 	
2 CONT Edith and Frank, as he was called, had eight children:  Edith Lavinnie, Charles Franklin who died as in infant, Leo Hilton, John Hugh, Vernon Elam,  Jesse Wimmer, Ronald Aaron and Ara who died as an infant.  The couple always made
2 CONC  sure that the children had a red wagon to play with and the girl used corn cobs for dolls. 	
2 CONT One time when Edith and her daughter were alone in their house two Indians came to asked for bread and soap.  As they waited for her to get the things for them one of the Indians picked up a butcher knife and handled it.  The Indi
2 CONC ans left without harming the woman and child but left them terribly frightened. 	
2 CONT When Edith was about thirteen she contracted pleurisy and became very ill.  Her grandfather administered to her and it wasn’t long before she was well.  Similarly, years later one of her sons of age 3 or 4 became ill but no one kn
2 CONC ew what was wrong.  His father was away at the time so his mother sent for the Elders.  He was administered to and for six weeks thereafter the Bishop and a councilor came to see him every night.  He finally pulled through and Edi
2 CONC th knows it was by the power of the Priesthood.  Later they found that the child had had malaria and typhoid fever.   	
2 CONT The family generally had a hard time.  Just before her husband died the government took away much of their possessions.  After her husband’s death, Edith worked in the St. George Temple for a “couple years”. 	
2 CONT Since her husband’s death she has lived by herself and has enjoyed doing all types of handiwork.  In 1961 she had an operation for a cataract on one of her eyes which made her nearly blind.  As she lay in the hospital unable to mo
2 CONC ve she had a lot of time to think.  She told afterward that she felt sorry to think of the time she had wasted on her handiwork when she should have been doing her genealogy.  She said that she promised the Lord at that time that 
2 CONC if He would restore her sight she would get her records in order before her life was ended.   	
2 CONT Edith lived in San Lorenzo, California when I knew her and was close to her granddaughter Ilda Starr.  She died 22 September 1964 after living for a time with Ilda and her family.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE:  Family Search.org
