2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT From the book "The Charles Shumway Family 1806-1979" Compiled and Edited by Margaret Shumway Sevey, Printed in the U.S.A. by Lofgreen Printing Co., Mesa, Arizona.  Page: 433 Page 84 from the same book it states:  In a small day bo
2 CONC ok kept by his mother is the following notation: "Sixtus was killed by a sliding rock in a vanadium mine on June 3, 1941.  He lived a faithful life, had many sorrows, but was faithful to the end.  He did all the work he could in t
2 CONC he temple and wanted to spend his last days working for the dead but was taken too soon.  He was loved by all who knew him.  His heart and mind was clean and pure; he kept the Word of Wisdom, paid an honest tithe and was honest in
2 CONC  every way.  Was prayerful and humble. I have no knowledge of any wrong he ever did.  I feel so thankful my Heavenly Father gave me a son so pure and clean as he is."
2 CONT
2 CONT Infomation from Book Saga of San Juan, by Cornellia Adams Perkins. Marian Gardner Nielson, Leonora Butt Jones, Second Edition, Published by San Juan County Daughters of Utah Pioneers 1968.  Mercury Publishing Co., Page 335. Grand 
2 CONC County Library, Moab Utah call No: 979.2 P Joseph Sixtus Shumway, his wife, Alice Oliver Shumway, and children, Althea and Norris Oliver, came to Grayson, (Blanding, Utah) September 4, 1910.  They first camped at a spring west of 
2 CONC the highway about three miles below the townsite, then came in town and camped in a tent about 200 feet south of where the San Juan High School is now. Sixtus Shumway helped build the ditch from Recapture to Town.  He and J.B. Har
2 CONC ris developed the spring in West Water to make drinking water for the town.  He and Walter C. Lyman first started work on the tunnel in the spring of 1922.  He was killed by a falling rock June 3, 1941.  He was active in the L.D.S
2 CONC . Church.
