1 NAME John Clarence /Stewart/
2 GIVN John Clarence
2 SURN Stewart
2 _MARNM Stewart
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 APR 1863
2 ADDR
3 CITY Goshen
3 STAE Utah Territory
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 1905
2 PLAC Goldfield Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Goldfield
3 STAE Nevada
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 FEB 1905
2 ADDR
3 CITY Goldfield
3 STAE Nevada
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Clarence was the son of John Riley Stewart and Frances Ellen Van Hooser who were married while crossing the plains to Utah in 1862.  He married Mandana Editha Johnson, 11 Mar 1887, in St George, Washington, Ut
2 CONC ah.
2 CONT
2 CONT History - When John grew to manhood he went on a mission to New Zealand in the year 1883. He kept a diary of his labor among the natives and was successful in converting many to the Latter-day Saint faith. He served for four years
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT From his diary we quote: September 6, 1884: The people threw away their English prayer books and asked for baptism. We went four miles to Tikikino for the baptism and about twenty followed us. We held a meeting and I had to talk i
2 CONC n Maori again as the man who tried to interpret Bro. Newby was no good.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7th-Sunday. This was a day long to be remembered. Twenty three asked for baptism. Three children we blessed. 10:00 a.m. was set for the time. I let Elder Newby baptize them as he hadn't baptized any yet. After dinner was over they
2 CONC  spread mats on the ground and the sacrament on boxes covered with white cloths. I spoke to them and they were confirmed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8th-After breakfast 2 couples were to be married. So at 11 a.m. we sang a hymn and had prayer, then I married them. The Saints all sang songs of praise and thanksgiving–that we had come to them and brought the Gospel. They talked 
2 CONC till 11 p.m. and followed us for miles when we left.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11th-Went to Hastings and got letters from President Stewart saying 89 more were baptized there where he was. He told me I was to come up where he was real soon October 1- ... Finally a small boat took me to Warhur. I supposed the
2 CONC  town called Wairia was only four miles away but found it 20 miles.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5th-The Maori people followed me and asked for a meeting. There were 50 present and had good order and I talked for about an hour. After dinner I went three miles to the next place and they welcomed me. They rang the bell and the 
2 CONC people came. About 250 collected and I spoke for one hour. After we dismissed, the men started asking questions and I had to stay till 11 p.m., and was so tired I rolled up in a blanket and went to the corner to rest, but the Maor
2 CONC is talked all night. Not much rest as one blanket wasn't very soft.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6th-I came to Toha's (head Maori) and had a good sleep. Another crowd collected and I showed the pictures of the Prophets and Salt Lake.
2 CONT
2 CONT 16th-I left for Wai Whare 10 miles. About 16 Maori followed me ...Fifteen more Maori were baptized at 5:30.... As the meeting was about to close, Maoris brought their children to be blessed. Sixteen were blessed and fifteen were b
2 CONC aptized....
2 CONT
2 CONT His diary is filled with villages he labored in and the success followed him throughout his missionary labors in this faraway island. There were 144 baptisms recorded in this one year of his diary, also 220 mentioned in one of his
2 CONC  letters to his mother, all on one island while he was there as presiding Elder.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served for four years and was so loved that the chief of the tribe Chief Whaanga, gave his little eight year old nephew to Clarence in appreciation for his bringing the gospel to these people. This gift is on record in the Chur
2 CONC ch files. He was one of the first missionaries to the Islands. At this time the Church advised the missionary not to decline this offer for fear it might offend the Islanders. So he brought home this little Maori boy where he was 
2 CONC warmly accepted as a member of the family. His name was Piriki Whaanga. At that time he was about eight years of age. He lived only to his twenty-sixth year when he was fatally injured while riding a horse.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following gifts were presented to Elder John Clarence Stewart by the Maori people on his return to Utah: One jade idol, to be worn tied on a string around the neck; one jade piece fashioned as a boomerang with boar's teeth tie
2 CONC d to it; two straight pieces of jade to be used similarly; one piece unfinished jade; one broken white bone ring and 1 pair of red and brown wool wristlets. Before leaving New Zealand he gathered some beautiful articles for his br
2 CONC ide to be, Editha Johnson. Among them was a clear glass rolling pin which he watched the natives blow, some seed bracelets and a small hand-fashioned bone cross.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few years later seven or eight Maori came to Kanab, Utah, the president of the branch lived there. His name was [http://www.geni.com/people/Hirini-Te-Rito-Whaanga/6000000018535685194 Hirini Whaanga], and he was said to be of roy
2 CONC al descent. His wife, [http://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Hunonga/6000000018536252208 Mere Whaanga] came with him and also Hirini's brother's wife, Abigail, who was [http://www.geni.com/people/Pirika-Whaanga/6000000018542586377 Pirik
2 CONC i's] mother. There was also Piriki's little brother named Kanab for the town in which John lived. Three teenagers, named Watni Smith Sydney Christi and Edna Pomeroy and others came. Hirini named John's daughter after his wife, and
2 CONC  daughter, who had died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mere Whaanga had her picture in the Deseret News on the 20th of February 1943. One picture showed her with Rufus K. Hardy and a near relative who was in the air force, Tame Hawaiki-range Waerea of Nuhaka, New Zealand. The other pi
2 CONC cture was at age ninety-five with a quilt she had made and given to President Grant of the Latter-day Saint Church. The article said she came to Utah with her husband Hirini in 1890. He was called back to New Zealand on a mission 
2 CONC by President Smith and died soon after returning to Utah. She returned to New Zealand where she remained until she was ninety-five years old, then Rufus K. Hardy made arrangements for her to return to Utah so that she could be bur
2 CONC ied by the side of her husband when she passed away. While in New Zealand she fed and sheltered hundreds of missionaries and traveled all over the country in the interests of the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarence and Editha made a home in Kanab. Here their first four children were born. The fifth child was born in Nevada and the sixth in Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1904, Clarence died in Nevada while engaging in mining activities. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Riley Stewart (1840 - 1916)
2 CONT
2 CONT Frances Ellen Van Hooser Stewart (1845 - 1864)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT
2 CONT Editha Mandana Johnson Stewart (1865 - 1921)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Junius Clarence Stewart (1888 - 1891)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Ellen Stewart Hemsley (1893 - 1976)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Created by: S.M. Smith
2 CONT
