1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William Eustace Waldrom was born September 15, 1894, in Malakoff, Henderson, Texas, to Charles E., and Margaret E. Waldrom.  In the 1900 U.S. Federal Census for Justice, Henderson, Texas, William was living with hi
2 CONC s parents and siblings, who were listed as follows in the census:  Charles E., 52, Margaret E., 43, Ronelle O., 19, William Eustace, 6, Mary E. 4, Ruth, one month old.  Also living in the home was Charles' brother, James E., 69, w
2 CONC ho was a widower, Elizabeth Evans, 69, Margaret's widowed mother, and two boarders, Emmet Brogen, 58, and Annie Mayers, 18.  Charles and Margaret had been married 25 years, had 6 children, 5 of whom were still living.   William's 
2 CONC father, Charles was working as a farmer, and his brother, Ronelle O., was working as a farm laborer.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1910 U.S. Federal Census for Weatherford, Parker, Texas, the Waldrom family was listed as follows:   Charles E., 60, Margaret, 53, William,16, Nellie, 7, and James E., 79, Charles Waldrom's widowed brother.  The family was 
2 CONC living at 903 North Main Street.  Charles and Margaret had been married for 31 years, they had 7 children, 4 of whom were still living.  Charles, James, and Margaret were all born in Alabama, and William and Nellie were born in Te
2 CONC xas.  William's father, Charles, was working as a dry goods merchant.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1930 U.S. Federal Census for Bryce, Graham, Arizona, William Eustace Waldron, 36, was living with his wife, Amy Lucille, 30, and their 11 year old daughter, Charlotte.  They had been married for 14 years.  They owned their 
2 CONC own home, and they had a radio in their home.  William was born in Texas, Lucille and Charlotte in Arizona.  William was working as a farmer.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1940 U.S. Federal Census for Safford, Graham, Arizona, William Eustace Waldron, 45,  was living with his wife, Amy Lucille, 40, and their two children, Charlotte, 21, and Billie, 7 years old.  William was working as the man
2 CONC ager of a cotton gin and for the 52 weeks he worked at 48 hours per week in 1939,  his income was $2,400.  William had two years of college, Amy Lucille, four years of high school, Charlotte, four years of college, and Billie was 
2 CONC in the second grade.   They were living in a home they owned located at 1124 South Central Street, which was valued at $4,000. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I don't know where William and his family were living throughout the 1940's, but  William Eustace Waldrom passed away September 3, 1951, in Los Angeles, California.  I don't know if his was living there at the time or only visitin
2 CONC g.  He was buried in the Safford City Cemetery, in Safford, Arizona.
