1 NAME George Augustus /Hanks/
2 GIVN George Augustus
2 _MARNM Hanks
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Greenwood Memorial Park
2 ADDR
3 CITY San Diego
3 STAE California
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 JAN 1868
2 ADDR
3 CITY Park City
3 STAE Utah
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 JAN 1929
2 ADDR
3 CITY San Diego
3 STAE California
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Augustus Hanks was the son of Ephraim Knowlton Hanks and Jane Maria Capener,  He married Melissa Jane Merrill, December 20, 1900, Thatcher, Arizona.  After she passed away of typhoid fever in 1901 one month 
2 CONC after the birth of their first baby, Wilma.  After the death of his wife, he left baby Wilma in the care of her grandparents, Thomas Steven Merrill and Ester Ann Collette Merrill.  George went back to Utah and there married Bessie
2 CONC  Bennets Johns, on May 20, 1905.  Wilma never say her father again until she was 17 years old.  The following history of George Augustus Hanks was written by his son, George Ephraim Hanks Jr. of San Diego, California:  This short 
2 CONC story is to acquaint those, who may be interested in parts of the life of George Augustus Hanks, my father. As history goes, he was born January 3, 1868, in Park City, Utah.  Until the year 1905, when he was wed to my mother, Eliz
2 CONC abeth ‘Bessie" Johns, he worked at many different occupations and businesses.  Some of these, as I recall him telling me, were mining, butchering, contracting and hunting wild game to sell and trade with the Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT This brings to mind a little story he told me of his hunting experiences. He and his partner took off for the mountains with their pack train to hunt deer for the market. During the trip they came upon a cub bear up a tree. Dad cl
2 CONC imbed the tree and got the cub stuffed in a grain sack. On the way down his hand holding the bag got cramped so he tried to hold the cub between his knees. The cub bit his leg thru the sack and he didn't quite remember if he jumpe
2 CONC d or fell down the rest of the way. Now the real climax came after they got back to town with their outfit. He made a collar for the cub and used to lead it about with a chain. He was drumming around town for more business and hap
2 CONC pened to stroll into a saloon. The barkeeper right away claimed that the bear was his and had been stolen and, during the argument of "Yes it is", and "no it isn't" the barkeeper bent down and picked the bear up in his arms. Right
2 CONC  then he lost a piece of his cheek, so decided the bear really was dad's and not the one that had been stolen from him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad raised angora goats and sheep at one time, worked in a general store and kept rental property, (building and renting houses and store buildings), worked in the mines, hauled ore down from the mines to the railroad, worked at F
2 CONC argo's Department Store buildings, and also operated one of his rental buildings as a shooting gallery at times.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was the twelfth child born to my mother. Eleven were by her former marriage to Steven Johns. I was raised with three of them: Violet, Steve, and Stella, the youngest. The others had more or less gone out on their own. In 1918 we
2 CONC  visited some of the children who were living in San Diego, California.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year, 1919, Dad and mother sold everything in Park City and moved to San Diego. We bought a home here and Dad worked in a butcher shop quite awhile and also took care of a stable of horses for a downtown dairy. Meanw
2 CONC hile, he sold the first property and bought a smaller home a couple of blocks away. In January, 1921, Dad leased a farm at Dehesa, about twenty-five miles eat of San Diego. After the first year he managed to lease two or three mor
2 CONC e places. Along with the farming he started to dairy a little on the side.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1926 we moved back to San Diego and went into the dairy business on a bigger scale. During the five years Dad was farming, Mother lived in and kept the home in San Diego. In the latter part of 1928 Dad sold the dairy stock and 
2 CONC started back to contracting work with teams building and boat slip for repairing boats on the bay front.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad was taken by a heart attack while caring for the animals over the weekend, January 6, 1929. He is buried in the Greenwood Cemetery, San Diego, California, near the mausoleum where his mother is buried. 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE:  Find A Grave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=22982701
2 CONT
