3 STAE Massachusetts
3 CTRY United States
1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Capt. Stephen Crane=
2 CONT * BIRTH 19 May 1734 Putnam County, New York, USA
2 CONT * DEATH 14 Apr 1814 (aged 79) Kent, Litchfield County, Connecticut, USA
2 CONT * BURIAL Good Hill Cemetery, Kent, Litchfield County, Connecticut, USA 
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID  33699845  
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen was 21 years of age in 1775 when the war broke out. He was a private in Thompson's Co., Wessenfel's Regt; also Miller's Co., Philips' Regt., State troops, and Col. Spencer's Regt. Line L.B.T. 14.6.17.; He became a Captain 
2 CONC and Commissary of Artillery in the war of the Revolution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  On May 27, 1775, a committee was appointed by the Continental Congress to consider ways and means to supply the colonies with ammunition and military stores. It authorized General George Washington to appoint an officer known as 
2 CONC the Commissary General of Artillery Stores, the nucleus around which would grow a corps of officers and civilians assigned exclusively to ordnance duty.  In a military sense, Stephen Crane was an officer who was in charge of furni
2 CONC shing artillery for the army. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen Crane (110), (Joseph, Jonathan, Benjamin), married Mary Chapman, Jan 22, 1762. She was born in South East, N.Y., Jan. 4, 1745. It is said her father was a clergyman and from Cape Cod, and settled in New Milford, Conn., but
2 CONC  died May 10, 1814, at the home of his son Joseph in Kent, Conn., and was buried there. April 10, 1770, he purchased a house and lot, also a blacksmith shop, in New Milford 1 1/2 miles north of the meeting house.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Ezra F. Crane, grandson of Stephen Crane writes; "My grandmother Crane was one of the grandest women of her age, a perfect heroine in the Revolutionary war. My grandfather was living at Danbury, Conn., when the British burned
2 CONC  the town. He and all his men from the shop left for the fort near by, but my grandmother stood her ground, and stood in the door of the house when the British officer rode up, who had his men search the house; finding she was alo
2 CONC ne, no men near, caused a guard to be placed around the house 'till the army passed, and thus saved her house."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Chapman was the daughter of Isaac and Mary (Paddock) Chapman. Her father was born in Dennis, Mass., April 7, 1721, and settled in South East, N.Y., about the year 1740. Her mother, Mary Paddock, was daughter of Peter Paddock,
2 CONC  who was born June 3, 1724, and died at South East, Nov. 8, 1776. Isaac Chapman, father of Mrs. Crane, was son of Isaac, who was born in Barnstable County, Mass., Dec 29, 1692, and grandson of Isaac, born Aug 4, 1647, and great-gr
2 CONC andson of Ralph Chapman Southwark of Surrey County, England. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT There is conflicting information on both the birth and death locations. CDA
2 CONT
