1 OCCU Married James Byron Smith December 14, 1911, sealed June 12, 1912
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Cordella May Layton was born in May 4, 1894, in Safford, Graham County, Arizona, to Heber and Agnes Almeda Layton.  In the 1900 U.S. Federal Census for Graham, Arizona Territory, Heber and Almeda, both 33 years old
2 CONC , are living with their children, Lorenzo, 12, Agnes, 10, Leroy, 8, and Cordella, 6.  Heber and Almeda had been married for six years and had four children.  Heber was working as a dry goods dealer, and he owned his home free from
2 CONC  a mortgage.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1910 census, the family is still in Graham County, Arizona, and two more children have been born, Glenna, 7, and Genevieve, 4.  Lorenzo is longer in the home,  Agnes is 20 years old, and there is an Adam J., who is 18 years
2 CONC  old, (maybe he was listed as Leroy in 1900), and  Cordella is 16.  Heber and Agnes have been married for 23 years, had 7 children, 6 of whom are still living.  Heber is working as a livery man at a stable, as is his son Adam.  Th
2 CONC ey own their home free from a mortgage..
2 CONT
2 CONT Cordella M. Layton married James Byron Smith in about 1912.  James Byron was the son of Robert A. and Mary E. Smith.   In the 1910 census, James was listed as Byron, age 24, living with his parents, who were 72 and 62 years old, i
2 CONC n Safford, Arizona.  They had been married 47 years at that time and had 12 children, 9 of whom were still living.  Evidently, James Byron Smith was their youngest child.  In that census, James Byron and his father were both worki
2 CONC ng as nurserymen.  
2 CONT
2 CONT When Byron Smith completed his World War I Draft Registration Card on September 12, 1918, at the local board for Safford, Graham County, Arizona, he described himself as being tall, with a medium build, blue eyes and brown hair.  
2 CONC He was 32 years old at the time, having been born January 16, 1886, and he was working as a farmer and was married to "Della" Smith.  Evidently sometimes Cordella went by a shortened version of her name, Della.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1920 census, Cordella M. Layton, 25, has married (James) Byron Smith, 33, and they are living in Safford, Arizona, with their children, Ellwood, 7, Arlo, 5, Jennie, 3 years, 9 months old, and Margurite, one month old.  Byro
2 CONC n is working as a nurseryman at a nursery.  Byron was born in Arizona, his father in Tennessee, and his mother in Alabama.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1930 census, Byron and "Della" are 44 and 35 years old, and their children, Ellwood, 17, Arlo, 15, Janice, 13, Margurite, 10, and Paul is 7.  They are still living in Safford, on 1st Avenue, and are renting their home for $
2 CONC 30. per month.  Byron is working as a trucker in his own business.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1940 census, Byron and "Della" are 54 and 46, and in their home is their son Paul, 18, and a woman named Maggie Talley, 52, who is listed as a servant.  They are living at 611 1st Avenue, and were living at the same place i
2 CONC n 1935, but now they own their home which is valued at $3,000.  Byron is now working as a produce dealer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son Elwood, 27, was living with his wife, Velda (or Vilda) Maloy, 25, and their children, Barbara Ann, 3, and James Byron Smith, 2 months old.  They were also living in Safford, Arizona, at 222 3rd Avenue, a  home which they
2 CONC  owned valued at $3,500.  Elwood had achieved 4 years of high school, and in the year 1939 for the 52 weeks he worked at 48 hours per week, he earned $1,200. as a proprietor of a wholesale produce business. His wife Velda had achi
2 CONC eved 2 years of college.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a U.S. City Directory for 1951 where Elwood L. and Velda are living at 1537 S. Pearl Street in Compton, California.  He was working as a chemist.  
2 CONT
2 CONT James Byron Smith passed away at the age of 74 on July 4, 1960, and is buried in Mesa, Maricopa County, Arizona.  His wife, Cordella May Layton Smith, passed away on July 25, 1977, and is buried in Mesa, Maricopa County, Arizona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son Elwood Layton Smith passed away in Los Angeles on November 10, 1988, and is buried in Cypress, Orange County, California.  His last residence was in Norwalk, California, which is about a mile from where I was born.  Eldw
2 CONC ood's wife, Vilda, born December 12, 1914, passed away at the age of 80 on December 13, 1994, and is buried in Forest Lawn Memorial Park in Cypress, Orange County, California, in Plot:  Awakening Faith, Lot 3496, Space 2.  Her las
2 CONC t address before she died was 16332 Graystone Aveue, Norwalk, 90650, in 1992.  She had also lived in La Mirada, California, at one time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elwood and Vilda's son, James Byron Smith, born August 29, 1939, passed away at the relatively young age of 50 years old on April 4, 1990, and is also buried in Forest Lawn Memorial Park in Cypress, California, Plot:  Awakening Fa
2 CONC ith, Lot 3507, Space 4.
2 CONT
2 CONT I don't know what ever happened to Elwood and Vilda's daughter, Barbara Ann Smith who was born in about 1937, as I cannot find her in any other public records after the 1940 U.S. Federal Census.  I also wonder if Elwood and Vilda 
2 CONC could have had another daughter after 1940, whom they named Della, after Elwood's mother Cordella?  In the obituary for Cordella's mother, Agnes Layton Smith, it mentions that she had a granddaughter named Della Smith who was livi
2 CONC ng in California at the time of her death in November of 1943.  I did find a public record for a Della M. Smith who was born July 13, 1940, and who was living in Carson, California, which is not far from Compton, a place we know t
2 CONC hat Elwood and Vilda had lived in 1951.  Since my name is Della Dale Smith, I was certainly curious about this.  And since I was born in 1951 in Lynwood, California, the coincidence is remarkable.  Lynwood is just a few miles from
2 CONC  Carson and Compton, California.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cordella and James Byron Smith's son Arlo was living in Safford, Arizona, in the 1940 census, was 25 years old and living with him is his wife, La Rea, 21.  Arlo was working as a truck driver, and for the 52 weeks he worked at 60 
2 CONC hours per week in 1939, he earned $1,200 in income.  They are living in a rented home located at 504 Central 3rd Avenue for which they pay $23.00 per month rent.  Arlo achieved 4 years of high school and La Rea achieved 2 years of
2 CONC  college.  La Rea was working as a stenographer at a company called SCS.  For the 32 weeks she worked at 44 hours per week that year, she earned $450. in income.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arlo, who was born July 7, 1914, passed away at the age of 70 on December 10, 1984, and was living in Nampa, Canyon County, Idaho, at the time of his death.  Arlo's wife, LaRea Foster Smith, born January 26, 1919, passed away on J
2 CONC une 25, 1985, and is buried in Caldwell, Canyon County, Idaho.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cordella and James Byron Smith's daughter, Janice, married Rodney Phillips, and in 1940 they were also living in Safford, and Rodney was 25, Janice was 24, and their son, Ray, was 3 years old.  They were renting their home for $18
2 CONC .00 per month.  Their home was located on 4th East Street.  Rodney was working as a clerk for Western Auto, and for the 52 weeks he worked at 48 hours per week in 1939, he earned $1,600 in income.  Janice, born April 27, 1916, pas
2 CONC sed away at the age of 70 on May 6, 1986, and is buried in Thatcher, Graham County, Arizona.  She was living in Safford at the time of her death.  Her husband Rodney Kimball Phillips, born August 5, 1915, passed away at the age of
2 CONC  77 on October 26, 1992, and he is also buried in Thatcher.  He was previously living at 3227 W. Safford Bryce Road, Safford, Arizona, 85546.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cordella and James Byron Smith's daughter, Margurite, married James E. Woodford.  In the 1940 census for Safford, they are 20 and 21 years old, and are living at 918 Central Avenue.  They have each achieved one year of college.  J
2 CONC ames is working as a mechanic in an automobile garage, and for the 52 weeks he worked in 1939 he earned $1,500. in income.  James E. Woodford, born in 1918 in Graham County, Arizona, enlisted in the service on November 15, 1944, i
2 CONC n Phoenix.  He was skilled in mechanics and repair of motor vehicles.   James passed away in June of 1972, and his last known residence was in Pinetop, Navajo County, Arizona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cordella and James Byron Smith's son, Paul R. Smith, born April 20, 1922, in Graham County, Arizona, also enlisted in the service April 16, 1943, in Phoenix.  He was a semiskilled pattern and model maker, and was married at the ti
2 CONC me of his enlistment.  He was a private in the service.  Paul was 18 when he married Jean Elizabeth Crandall after she graduated from Safford High School at the age of 16.  They were married for 70 years when Jean passed away in 2
2 CONC 010.  Paul passed away at the age of 90 on July 20, 2012, and is buried in Pima, Graham County, Arizona. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul served in the U.S. Air force during World War II.  On his first bombing mission, his plane was shot down over Germany, and he had to parachute out of the plane.  When Paul hit the ground, he was captured by German soldiers an
2 CONC d put into a prison camp.  Paul was in prison camp for more than a year.  At the end of World War II, Paul would return home and attend college to further his education.  Paul graduated with a master's degree in agriculture from t
2 CONC he University of Arizona after returning home from the war.  He was a landscaper and a farmer all his life.  He farmed in Tolleson, Buckeye, and in the Gila Valley.  Paul built and ran the Pima Dairy and Creamery and later started
2 CONC  Smith Bros. Jerky and fast food businesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul was a lifelong, dedicated and active member of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and held many church positions. He was noted as an outstanding Gospel doctrine teacher.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul was survived by: his four children, Stan (Lynn Gable), Kristen (Bud McMurray), Kelly (Pam Hoyle) and Ross (GayLynn McEuen); many grandchildren, great-grandchildren and great-great-grandchildren.  Paul's body was cremated, and
2 CONC  the ashes were given to family or friend.
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point before his death he lived on East Foothills Drive in Pima, Arizona.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === !check sealing date !check sealing date ===
2 CONT !check sealing date !check sealing date
2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT From Della's words as recorded by my mother in conversations with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was the fourth child born to Heber Chase Layton and Agnes Almeda Welker on May 4, 1894 in the small town of Layton, Arizona (near Safford).  My father was born on November 2, 1867 in Kaysville, Utah of English heritage and came 
2 CONC to Arizona as a young man.  My mother was born on November 19, 1867 in Bear Lake, Idaho.  Her father was born in Illinois, her mother in Scotland.  She came to Arizona on her fourteenth birthday.
2 CONT
2 CONT My oldest brother, Heber Lorenzo, was born January 31, 1888.  My older sister, Agnes Ann, was born January 4, 1890.  My brother, Adam Leroy (Roy) was born March 5, 1892; and two years later I came into the picture.  When I was nin
2 CONC e years old, Glenna Leona was born on October 7, 1903 and Genevie followed on June 21, 1906.  We had a baby brother, Delmar Christopher, born August 19, 1908, but he died when he was just eleven days old.
2 CONT
2 CONT When I was four years old my father was called on a mission to Pueblo, Colorado.  I remember my brother, Lorenzo (so small he had to stand on the fence to reach the horse) putting the collars on the horses to take Father to meet t
2 CONC he train.  Mother had to work in her brother, Robert Welker's, mercantile store to support our family while Father was away on his mission.  After nine months he was brought home on a stretcher, ailing badly with Rheumatism.  Brot
2 CONC her Lorenzo hooked up the team to the wagon and took all of the family to the depot to meet father.  
2 CONT
2 CONT We then moved to a farm, (the old Capus Motes' home), just outside the town of Thatcher where I started to school.  I remember it was a long ways to walk to and from school every day.  The neighbor children, (the Jonathan Hoopes f
2 CONC amily), and us would all walk together.  Pearl was the nearest girl to my age.  Sometimes I would get into trouble with my teacher for talking too much.  I would have to stand in the corner with arms outstretched and hold a large 
2 CONC dictionary on each arm.  Boy, were those books ever heavy!  It sure kept me from opening my mouth, (just for a few days anyway!).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Every year on Velentine's day I and some of my close school chums, (President Spencer W. Kimball, Leslie Clawson, Than Curtis, Chloe Curtis Luster, Ella Tyler Lee, Grace Layton DeSpain, Sophronia Layton Clawson), use dto go knock 
2 CONC on doors, leaving a valentine with a string attached.  Then we would hide and jerk it away when they came to the door.  And we used to tie tac windows too, on Halloween, but on Valentine's day.  That was the night for all the pran
2 CONC ks in those days.
2 CONT
2 CONT I guess you could say that my cousin, Sophronia, and I were best friends, telling all our secretes to each other.  We went to many parties at Spencer Kimball's home and I remember one in particular when we played a game where you 
2 CONC sat on a bench blindfolded and they raised it.  Well, I fell off and bumped myself and Aunt Olive Kimball doctored me all up.
2 CONT
2 CONT My family lived on a farm all during my childhood.  I was a tomboy.  I loved to help my brother, Roy, rake the hay into stacks and drive the horse back and forth.  Roy and I spent a lot of time together on the farm because there w
2 CONC ern't any boys for him to play with and there weren't any girls for me.  We used to go catch poly wads in the ditches and romp through the fields.  I loved to be out-of-doors with him.  
2 CONT
2 CONT We made our own amusements in those days.  We didn't ahve television and movies, but I still think we had better times than the kids do these days.  We had to wait about an hour after school before Primary started so we used to st
2 CONC op at the dance hall in Thatcher to play marbles until it was time to go.  Father raised a melon patch and every Sunday morning he would make a big freezer of homemade ice cream and pack it along with some melons down in the bbase
2 CONC ment to keep cool until company came.  There were lots of cousins to play with then and we had the best times.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was about thirteen when I started going to the dances.  I was big for my age and Father used to say, "If they're big enough, they're old enough."  I remember one of my favorite teachers, (I can't recollect her name now), was an 
2 CONC old maid.  She had never learned to dance.  She used to live at Grandma Coleman's house and she used to invite all of us to parties there after school and the boys would teach her how to dance.  Pretty soon we girls lost our danci
2 CONC ng partners at the school dances because they were always dancing with the teacher.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a girl growing up, I had most of the housework, cooking and laundry to do for our family as Mother was quite ill with many miscarriages.  I used to scrub all the clothes on a scrub board.  Agnes sewed and played the piano.  I n
2 CONC ever had time for such things.  
2 CONT
2 CONT When I was in the 8th grade my family moved back to Layton and there I graduated when I was fifteen years old.  It was about that time that I started going with By Smith.  I remember he came by the house one day ask Agnes to go fo
2 CONC r a rid with him in his wagon, but Agnes to go for a ride so I said I would go riding with him.  Here I was just a kid of fifteen and he was a grown man of twenty-four; a returned missionary at that!  He laughed and said to come a
2 CONC long and from that time on we went together steadily.  By was a fun loving man, someone you could laugh with and have a good time.  He loved to sing and had a beautiful bass voice.  He serenaded everywhere he went.  He would even 
2 CONC drive his wagon around with his phonograph playing and that's how he learned all those songs.  By wasn't my first boyfriend.  I had gone out with Wellington and Than Curtis, and Earl Quinn.   But after I met By there wasn't anyone
2 CONC  else that could take his place.  Everyone used to say, "By, you're rocking the cradle, " . . but guess he thought he had the right cradle?" 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was when Father moved the family back to Thatcher that By decided we should get married . . . it was just too far to go back and forth from Thatcher to Layton in the horse and wagon!  He asked me one night, but I didn't know wh
2 CONC ether my folks would let me.  When Sunday came he asked Father.  Father said, "Well, I don't know how we will ever spare that girl, but when they're big enough, they're old enough."  
2 CONT
2 CONT We were married on December 14, 1911 in my home in Thatcher, (the old Roy Layton farm next to the river).  He was 25 and I was 16.  By invited everyone.  We had such a big crowd the upstairs and downstairs were all filled the day 
2 CONC before my wedding.  Mother had made fruit cakes several weeks before and we cut them in little squares.  Along with the cake we had punch, chicken sandwiches and ice cream for all that crowd.  
2 CONT
2 CONT By and I were sealed to each other in the Salt Lake Temple on June 12, 1912.  Quite a large group of people from the Gila Valley left Safford together on the train.  In those days people made the trip to Salt Lake only during cert
2 CONC ain times of the year when there was a cut rate on the train ticket.  We had to change cars in Maricopa and then on to Salt Lake City.  The night before the the sealing ceremony a group of us went swimming in the Great Salt Lake. 
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Our first home was a two-room brick house and this was where the first child, Elwood, was born on October 22, 1912.  By sold nursery produce and ran his father's fruit orchard.  He was a good provider, worked hard and was happy.  
2 CONC Elwood was a tiny child.  He weighed only 5 and a half pounds when he was born.  He looked like a girl.  But then in those days we dressed our children in bonnets and white dresses so who could tell the difference?
2 CONT
2 CONT Our family started coming.  Arlo was born at Grandma Smith's on July 7, 1914.  
2 CONT
2 CONT We bought a twenty-acre farm above San Simon and moved into a four-room adobe home that we built.  We raised produce, had a large garden and lots of strawberries.  I spent most of my time canning and scrubbing clothes over a was b
2 CONC oard.  I churned from 8 to 10 pounds of butter a day and sent it out on a wagon to sell.
2 CONT
2 CONT 20 months after the birth of Arlo, Jenes was born (she later changed her name to Janice).  By teased me that if it was a girl he would give it away.  But when she came he was as happy as I was to have a little girl, and he loved h
2 CONC er as much as me.  
2 CONT
2 CONT We sold the 20 acre farm to Ed Claridge and moved to Layton.  We bought a lumber home and moved it in close to Smith's where Margurite was born on November 19, 1919.  We named her after a song By used to sing.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Then we moved into a brick house close to Annie Halstead, where Paul was born on April 20, 1922.  We named him Paul Robert after By's father.  
2 CONT
2 CONT I guess the most serious sickness of any of the children was when Janice had the measles and the infection settled in her kidneys.  She was so sick.  We moved to California for awhile so she could see a specialist.  One morning sh
2 CONC e went out to play and couldn't walk.  She couldn't even stand on her foot.  We rushed her to the doctor and they operated, opening her foot in three places and draining it.  They put a cast on her leg and she had to be in the cas
2 CONC t for several weeks.  We stayed in California a long time, camping in once place and another until we were sure that Janice was well.  In the fall when she could walk on crutches we brought her back home to Safford.  She was old e
2 CONC nough to go to school now so we moved near the schoolhouse in the old Madison home.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The happiest time of my life was when I was serving in the Relief Society Presidency.  Sister Ruth Foote was President.  I served in the presidency for four years.  I also taught the religion class in Relief Society and was a Prim
2 CONC ary teacher for many years.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Elwood married Vilda Maloy on July 9, 1934.  Our first grandchild, Barbara Ann, was born August 26, 1936
2 CONT
2 CONT Janice was married on March 2, 1936 to Rodney Phillips and their first daugher, (our second grandchild), whom they named Kay Larue, was born January 31, 1937, the same day Arlo left for his mission to Canada.  NO MORE ROOM :-(
2 CONT === !SOURCE:
2 CONT 1-  Layton Family Bible;in poss ===
2 CONT !SOURCE: 1-  Layton Family Bible;in poss. of Marlo L Webb. 2-  Personal knowledge of Margo Allee;attended funeral with her Aunt Maxine Rhien and grandmother Glenna Smoak. 3-  FHL;(AFN:3P06-C8). 4-  Social Security Death Index;www.
2 CONC ancestry.com;soc.sec.#527-38-3840; residence at time of death,zip code 85203,Mesa,Maricopa,Arizona.
2 CONT
