1 NAME Theodore James /Wassmer/
2 GIVN Theodore James
2 SURN Wassmer
2 _MARNM Wassmer
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 JUN 1888
2 PLAC Taylorsville, Salt Lake, Utah
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 29 MAR 1975
2 PLAC Murray, Salt Lake, Utah
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE 1 APR 1975
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Would go to Dean and Shirley's house every Wednesday night for dinner. He  would sit in the chair and say, "I remember..." and start to reminisce.  Shirley would ask him a lot of questions and listen.   When he was a little boy he
2 CONC  went to stay with his brother and brother's wife. They lived out in the Granger/ Hunter area and had a dry farm.  One night his brother left and went in to town for something and left he and his sister in-law, he was only about 7
2 CONC  years old.  It got dark and he started to get frightened and his sister in law was expecting and so she was frightened, and the sun went down and it started to get dark and the coyotes started to howl.  The coyotes howled and his
2 CONC  sister-in-law howled and he howled and the three of them sat there and sang half of the night.  In the early dawn  he got on an old work horse, scared to death, with coyotes and snakes to be afraid of he set the horse loose and i
2 CONC t went on home (2 -10 miles) with him clinging to his mane.  When he got home he told his mother and father and his brothers rushed out and got his sister-in-law.  He said that he had never been more lonely in his life as he was o
2 CONC ut on that range.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a cute sense of humor, he would say things that were bound to make you chuckle. He liked to dance and once told Shirley that he had never asked a  girl to dance that turned him down. Dancing was a big part of his life.  Lat
2 CONC er on with Hester they went dancing every week.  He would stand up and do a funny little shimmy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He never showed emotions.  He was a workaholic and that was when he was happiest.  Every morning he would get up at 5am and milk the cows and then go to work for 8 hours and then return home to work until 10 pm.  He wanted the fam
2 CONC ily to be like he was and wanted to work a lot too.  He was concerned about the kids but would never show it.  Dean never saw his mother and father embrace or show emotions, and this was the same amongst the siblings. He had no re
2 CONC al social life until he was at least 50 and he and Hester joined a dance club and would go dancing every Saturday night.
2 CONT
2 CONT Every morning he made oatmeal for the kids' breakfast. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When he lived at 729 Roosevelt (when Dean was 2 or 3)he had a cow which was put out to pasture  over at the present location of the ball diamond.  It was a pasture for all those who had animals in that area.  The cow was put out e
2 CONC very morning and brought in every night. In that area there are a lot of artesian wells, two wells are capped with drinking fountains, one at the  south-east corner of Liberty Park and there is one at the baseball park.  At this s
2 CONC ame time this area was considered the very outskirts of town the only thing south was at 27th south , Atkin So. where Brigham Young had a farm. It was a half days drive down in a wagon for Brigham Young.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a horse and buggy until just before Dean was born.  His first car was a 1916 Model T.  He was the talk of the neighborhood.  He was a foreman of a lumber company and so they could afford a car and were living pretty well ev
2 CONC en with eight children.  There was as much food as you wanted and new clothes occasionally and new shoes every year.  Then the depression hit and the lumber company folded.  So he was without a job with no income, eight children a
2 CONC nd his sister came to live with them.  And it was pretty rough.  The car was the only thing they had of any real value. One year they had a contest at Liberty Park for the car that would go to furthest on a gallon of gas.  He ente
2 CONC red and adjusted the carburetor.  He won and they gave him a hundred dollar bill.  Hester thought they were rich!  They took his picture and put it in the paper.  The next week Don got appendicitis and he had to have it removed, i
2 CONC t took every penny.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Wassmers were the biggest family on Roosevelt Avenue.  The church kind of expected though that you have a child every year.  Dean remembers Hester  saying that her mother-in-law was upset with her because she didn't have more 
2 CONC children.  She thought that she should be having one every year.  There were eleven children in Theodore's parents family.  There was a theater, the Mission Theater, near Liberty Park.  Every weekend the family would go and there 
2 CONC were door prizes given. For example the oldest man, the newest mother and the biggest family.  Theodore always won.  The owners decided to change that to the tallest man because the Wassmers always won.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dean was the first  child born at 729.  In the little bedroom at the back of the house.  Ma Wassmer always called it the berthing room because it was by the kitchen stove where it was warm. There was no furnace or central heating 
2 CONC so the stove was the only source of heat.  Theodore  would build a fire in the stove and get the house nice and warm and then Doc Martin would come and spank the babies into life.  Dean, marge and Lois, and Bill were born there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Every Christmas they had a tree.  They would go out Christmas Eve when the tree places were closing and try to get a tree for free or for 10 or 20 cents which was still a lot of money.  And they would decorate it with the few deco
2 CONC rations they had and Ted and Vera would buy some if they could. One year they bought globes and it was the first year they had lights on the tree, the globes were all in the shape of toys and Dean thought that it was the most wond
2 CONC erful thing! Even though they had the lights they couldn't afford to keep them on.   They were turned on only if someone were coming.  Pa had a great idea that they wouldn't have lights at all but instead a spot light.  They got a
2 CONC  lamp and put purple cellophane in the spotlight and it shone on the icicles and made a sparkling purple tree but no one liked it, they all liked the individual lights.    It was never an outstanding tree, everyone else on the str
2 CONC eet had beautiful trees. They could afford icicles which were only 5 cents a box. Christmas only happened because of Ted and Vera buying presents. The kids looked forward to Christmas and could hardly wait for the day to come.   E
2 CONC very person got one present.  One year when dean was about 7 he was so excited and was expecting a lot for Christmas and he didn't want to go to bed.  He asked his Mom if he could sleep n the sofa so that he would be right near th
2 CONC e tree when everyone woke up in the morning.  He was asleep and in comes Vera and her boyfriend.  They weren't really bothering him but just there upset him and embarrassed him so he got up and went to bed, so disappointed.  Chris
2 CONC tmas day they usually ate Pork Roast.  They couldn't afford turkey so they had Pork since Ma said it tasted the most like turkey.  It was very cheap like 15 cents a pound.  The kids felt bad that they couldn't have a turkey and ev
2 CONC eryone else could.  Potatoes, onions and carrots were about the only vegetables they ever ate unless something was in season.  They had bananas, oranges, strawberries, and raspberries once a year.  Ma always put up lots of fruit. 
2 CONC  When peaches came in she would can 8-10 bushels.  That was the food for the winter.  They had an ice box to keep the food cold.  They couldn't afford delivery of the block so they would go down to the corner and get a 25lb block 
2 CONC for 10 cents, delivered it was 15 cents.  The would put it in the little red wagon and wrap it in burlap and hurry home so that Pa wouldn't yell at you.  The ice block would laugh for about 3 days.  What was funny was that they re
2 CONC ally didn't have any food to put in the icebox.  They didn't have left overs or food, occasionally they had some butter and that was the only thing Dean can remember in the ice box.  Generally they went to the store and bought wha
2 CONC t you needed for that meal.  Ma made 8 loaves of  bread every other day.  She always insisted on butter.  There was some white  margarine (it was against the law to sell yellow margarine) you could buy and you would break the yell
2 CONC ow capsule and stir it up.  She didn't like it.  Pa came up with the idea that in the old country, and as he was raised his father said you didn't need butter you put lard on your bread, and it is just as good and you can get used
2 CONC  to it.  During the depression he decided the family would try it,  that lasted a week and that was it.
2 CONT
2 CONT The coal stove was on the north wall and the boys' job was to cut the kindling wood, bring in the coal, and fill the coal scuttle and bring that in to the kitchen.  The hardest thing about cooking was to get the heat regulated.  I
2 CONC t was either too cold or too hot and it would burn.  Dean says that their bread was almost always burned.  Hester and Shirley got electric stoves at the same time and Hester was thrilled!!  She thought it was the nicest thing to b
2 CONC e able to just turn the dial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laundry was a huge task for 8 kids and 3 adults.  She had a washer.  Most people on the street didn't have washers and women were expected to wash clothes by hand on the scrubbing boards. Until 1936 their neighbor washed everythin
2 CONC g by hand.  To them a washer was a real luxury.  But even having a washer they couldn't afford the soap.  A 10 cent box of soap would only do one washing.  It was bar soap. Than you had to rub it on a scratcher and make it come ou
2 CONC t like sawdust.  Because it was soap and not detergent it left a scum on everything and that is why the clothes had to be rinsed three times.  The washer had two rinse tubs.  So after the clothes were washed you would put them in 
2 CONC the rinse tubs.  When the water got milky you would drain the water in the rinse tubs and put in fresh.  But the water in the washer was not changed until the whole washing was over.  You had to heat the water on the stove for the
2 CONC  washer.  The fabric was all cotton and wool and had to be cleaned in hot water.  Monday was washing day and then she would spend all day Tuesday ironing. It would take hours to iron those cotton shirts.  One day when Dean came ho
2 CONC me from school she said, "I ironed 14 shirts today!".   One day Hester wanted to teach her girls how to iron but as typical teenagers are they were not too interested so she started leaving some places on their dresses unironed or
2 CONC  creased.  The girls would  say "oh look what you did!", "oh I guess I didn't get it quite right maybe you'd like to touch it up".  It only took about 3 weeks and they were doing their own ironing.  When they had the stuff out she
2 CONC  would say as long as you are ironing why don't you do these two shirts.  That didn't work.  The boys all knew how to take care of their clothes and if they needed something to go out in they would have to wash it by hand and iron
2 CONC  it.  When Dean was a teenager it was very fashionable to wear a white scarf around your neck under your coat.  He would wash and iron  that scarf every other day just so that it would look nice.
2 CONT
2 CONT He never hired any work to be done but would fix everything himself, even his car.  He would build things all of the time.  He would surprise the daughters-in-law all of the time, he would come across some pipe and he'd make a bra
2 CONC ced little hangman's tree and that was to hang your Iron on, or a towel rack.  When Hester died he was so lonely and needed something to do that Dean asked him to make a closet for one of the kids for their downstairs that they we
2 CONC re finishing, he had so much fun that he made four of them.  He would be out in the shed for two weeks early in the morning and late at night and then would call up and say that he had it done.  He had done everything, all of the 
2 CONC drawers and did them very well.  He even made a front door for them.  They were trying to find something so that you could see people coming but there wasn't anything in the stores so he said that he could do it. And he would make
2 CONC  all of the frames for Ted's paintings.  If you ever came in and he was just sitting down you knew something was wrong.
2 CONT
2 CONT The house on Roosevelt had only a skeleton key which was never used.  When they sold the house they couldn't find the key. Houses didn't need to be locked.  The house was stucco over adobe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had always lived by himself but when he was older he moved in with Bill and Shirley.  For a break he would go over to Dean and Shirley's Saturday and He and Dean would out for breakfast and then he would sit in the back yard an
2 CONC d tell them how to do their garden.   Then on Wednesday night he would come over for dinner and then Shirley would go to a University class and when she got home they took him back. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Hester in 1909 and she died in  1958 at the age of 66.  He remarried a friend of many years the widow of Reuben Forbush.  Reuben died and then Hester died.  Nellie came over and made a couple of good meals for him and o
2 CONC ne thing led to another.  He came to Dean and Shirley and asked them if they would be offended if he remarried and they of course said that they thought it was a great idea.  Soon after they married she had a stroke and he cared f
2 CONC or her until she died. Then he lived alone for a couple years until he went to live with Bill for 6 months and then he died. He was 88.    
2 CONT
2 CONT In the family there was Rheumatism.  The men have prostate problems and two have died of cancer. Or at least they said it was cancer but medicine was very primitive before WWII.  Surgery was an appendectomy, they could fix broken 
2 CONC bones and take a bullet out but that was about it.  You took patent medicines like  Lydia Pinkums pink pills, or liver pills.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Depression- He was the foreman at a lumber company which soon folded.  Ted and Vera as the oldest worked and helped the family survive.  It was a community effort, they turned over every penny to the family.  There was no mone
2 CONC y they could not have new clothes and had almost no food.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family would go to church.  Hester had a beautiful voice and was in the choir.  One Sunday she went and  the choir leader came up to her and said that the next Sunday they would be singing at Stake Conference and that everyone
2 CONC  must wear a black skirt and a white blouse.  She said that she didn't have a black skirt or white shirt and the leader said that she should not bother coming.
2 CONT
2 CONT She not only didn't sing any more but she never went back.  The kids had the same experience since they couldn't dress well.  The first one up in the morning got to wear the dress pants. There was no welfare or help from any one. 
2 CONC You just got on as well as you could.
2 CONT
2 CONT They wouldn't have enough money to pay the house taxes so they would let it go as long as possible until they would threaten to sell the house and then they would scrape it together somehow.  It was better after they moved out to 
2 CONC the farm because they would at least have something to eat.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at the age of 86 of heart failure.  He had an enlarged heart.
