1 NAME Wyatt Tate /Brady/
2 GIVN Wyatt Tate
2 SURN Brady
2 _MARNM Brady
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 JAN 1870
2 ADDR
3 CITY Forest City
3 STAE Missouri
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 AUG 1925
2 ADDR
3 CITY Tulsa
3 STAE Oklahoma
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU American merchant, politician, and a founder of Tulsa, Oklahoma
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wyatt Tate Brady (January 20, 1870 – August 25, 1925) was an American merchant, politician, and a founder of Tulsa, Oklahoma.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Early life=
2 CONT Brady was born in Forest City, Missouri, in 1870. His family moved to Nevada, Missouri when he was 12, where he eventually took a job at a shoe store. Here, he was the victim of a robbery. In 1890, at the age of 20, Brady headed f
2 CONC or Creek Nation, Indian Territory, to seek his fortune in the as-yet-unfounded Tulsa, Oklahoma. In 1895, Brady married Rachel Davis, who was part Cherokee. Together they had two daughters and two sons. After the marriage, Brady wa
2 CONC s adopted into the Cherokee tribe and became a strong advocate for their tribal claims against Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Activities in Tulsa history=
2 CONT On January 18, 1898, Brady and other prominent businessmen signed the charter to incorporate Tulsa, thereby making it a city. Following the 1901 discovery of the Red Fork oil field, Brady built the Brady Hotel in 1903, hoping to t
2 CONC ake advantage of the oil boom by providing a hotel for oil executives and other traveling businessmen. It also served as a meeting ground for the Democratic Party. Active in politics, he was named to the Democratic National Commit
2 CONC tee in 1907, and backed anti-Klan candidate Jack Walton in 1922 gubernatorial election.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brady was known for hiring black people to work in his hotels and other businesses. Mabel B. Little, a survivor of the Tulsa Race Riot of 1921and once employed by Brady, writes in her book Fire on Mount Zion: My Life and History a
2 CONC s a Black Woman in America: "Another man, Mr. Tate Brady had good feelings for black people. He hired several black boys as porters. But he told them up front, "Listen, boys: I'm gonna train you so you can get your own businesses 
2 CONC someday."
2 CONT
2 CONT Brady was identified as one of the organizers behind the Tulsa Outrage of 1917, in which members of the Industrial Workers of the World (IWW) were tarred and feathered by the black-robed Knights of Liberty, a short-lived group ass
2 CONC ociated with the Ku Klux Klan. All 17 members of the IWW identified Brady as the man who applied the tar and feathers.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 6, 1917, Brady physically assaulted the owner of the Hotel Fox, E.L. Fox in broad daylight. Brady served as a night watchman during the 1921 Tulsa race riots. An article in the Tulsa Daily World stated "Tate Brady, pro
2 CONC prietor of the Brady hotel, who was a member of white men on guard duty along North Main street all night, said he counted the bodies of five negroes. One negro was dragged behind an automobile, with a rope about his neck, through
2 CONC out the business district."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1923, the Klan, established as the Tulsa Benevolent Society, paid $200,000 for the construction of a large "Klavern" or gathering hall that could seat 3,000 members. Beno Hall, as it was known, was located at 503 N. Main St. Ac
2 CONC cording to Tulsa County land records, that parcel of land was then owned by Rachel Brady, Brady's wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Supporting a 1923 military tribunal, Brady stated that he, like his father before him, was a member of the Klan. He stated that he had quit the Klan because he was a Democrat and would not be told how to vote.[8] Brady supported a
2 CONC nti-Klan gubernatorial candidate Jack Walton who "engaged in an out-and-out war with the Ku Klux Klan".
2 CONT
2 CONT =Brady Mansion=
2 CONT Brady, who was a member of the Sons of Confederate Veterans, built a mansion known as "Arlington" that was patterned after the ancestral home of the Lee family in Virginia. The Brady Mansion still stands at 620 North Denver Avenue
2 CONC  in Tulsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Death=
2 CONT Brady committed suicide on August 29, 1925, by shooting himself in the temple. He was said to be despondent over the death of his oldest son, John Davis Brady who was killed in a car accident in spring 1925 while studying law at t
2 CONC he University of Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Legacy=
2 CONT Brady was commemorated for his part in the founding of Tulsa by the names of Brady Street and Brady Heights.
2 CONT
2 CONT A controversy arose in 2013 over the appropriateness of naming a street for Brady, because of his membership in the KKK. On August 15, 2013, following months of debate, the Tulsa City Council voted 7–1 to change the street name to
2 CONC  MB Brady Street, honoring Mathew B. Brady rather than Tate Brady. Mathew Brady was a famed civil war photographer, but had no ties to Tulsa or Oklahoma. The Brady Arts District, the Brady Historical District, Brady Heights, and B
2 CONC rady Theater were not affected by the change, as they are named after the nearby street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W._Tate_Brady
