1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Article from Cassville Democrat, Wednesday, July 10, 1940 reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT Postmaster 22 Years Dies
2 CONT
2 CONT Prominent Resident of Cato Community and Country Merchant
2 CONT
2 CONT W.E. Stumpff, prominent resident of Cato community for thirty-five years, did Saturday, July 6th, at his home, following a long illness.
2 CONT For twenty-two years Mr. Stumpff had been postmaster at Cato where he had a store.
2 CONT He had been seriously ill of a prostrate gland trouble for a year or more. He had frequently been treated here at the Barry County Hospital.
2 CONT Funeral services were held at the Stumpff home at Cato Monday afternoon, July 8, conducted by Rev. Sterl Watson of Springfield.
2 CONT Burial was in the Mineral Spring cemetery, with the Horine-Culver undertakers in charge.
2 CONT Mr. Stumpff was born Aug. 19, 1866, being at the time of his death 73 years, 19 months and 17 days old.
2 CONT Surviving are his widow and four children, three sons, Olen, Ernest, and Paul of Cato; and one daughter, Mrs. Troy Short of Cassville. He is also survived by four brothers; Ben, Frank, Charles and George; and one sister, Mrs. J.B.
2 CONC  Bailie, who lives in Oregon.
2 CONT William Edgar Stumpff, a son of Francis and Sarah Stumpff, was born Aug. 19, 1866 in Johnson County, Mo.
2 CONT He was married to Minnie A Stahl Nov. 27, 1894. To them five children were born, one of whom, Walter, died in infancy.
2 CONT For more than forty years he had been a faithful member of the Church of Christ. During his long illness he bore his suffering with patience and Christian fortitude.
2 CONT Mr. Stumpff was one of the county's fine citizens and a useful man.
