1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Wikipedia Biographical Summary:==
2 CONT
2 CONT "...'''Joseph Ellis Johnson''' (April 28, 1817 – December 17, 1882) was an American Mormon activist as well as being a newspaper proprietor, councillor, and businessman who at various times had several enterprises across several s
2 CONC tates..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Johnson was born at Pomfret, Chautauqua County, New York, and moved to Kirtland, Ohio, in 1832 and was baptized as a Latter Day Saint in 1833. He accompanied the Kirtland Camp in 1838. He taught school in Springfield, Illinois
2 CONC  in 1840. He went to Nauvoo, Illinois, 1840, a center for Latter Day Saints, and when he married Harriet Snider on October 6, 1840, the marriage was performed by Joseph Smith, founder of the movement..."
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE'': Wikipedia contributors, 'Joseph Ellis Johnson','' Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia'', 3 January 2011, 05:04 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Joseph_Ellis_Johnson&oldid=405633957> [accessed 18 February 2
2 CONC 011] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=9271439 Find a Grave]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 	Apr. 28, 1817
2 CONT Chautauqua County
2 CONT New York, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 	Dec. 17, 1882 Tempe Maricopa County Arizona, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT An early member of the LDS Church and son of Ezekiel and Julia Hills Johnson. Joseph moved to Kirtland, Ohio in 1832 and was baptized as a Latter-day Saint in 1833. He was also a member of the Kirtland Camp in 1838 and taught scho
2 CONC ol in Springfield, Illinois in 1840. He moved to Nauvoo, Illinois in 1840 and was married to Harriet Snider by Joseph Smith later that year. Joseph accompanied Joseph and Hyrum Smith on their way to Carthage jail and was taken as 
2 CONC a prisoner when the mob entered Nauvoo. He settled in Miller's Hollow, now Council Bluffs in 1848 and built the first house in Pottawattamie County, Iowa (other than a log cabin). He was the postmaster at Council Bluffs for five y
2 CONC ears and was also a member of the first city council. In 1852 he established the Council Bluffs Bugle (newspaper). The Bugle office and store destroyed by fire in 1853 yet afterward restored the office and published the paper unti
2 CONC l 1856. The Bugle was instrumental in getting the capitol of Nebraska Territory located at Omaha. (Lincoln was later chosen as the state capitol in 1867) Joseph was elected to the Nebraska legislature but he said he was "too much 
2 CONC of a Democrat" to get a seat. He also opened the first store on the present site of Omaha and sent the first train of goods to the Denver, Colorado (Cherry Creek) mines. In 1854 he published the Omaha Arrow, the first newspaper pu
2 CONC blished on Nebraska soil and that same year accompanied the first party of explorers for a railroad crossing on the Missouri River and Loupe Fork of the Platte river. He wrote the first published article favoring the North Platte 
2 CONC route for the Pacific railroad. In 1850 he crossed the plains to Utah and back and in 1857 started the Crescent City Oracle and also laid out the town of Crescent. In 1858 he published the Council Bluffs Press and in published the
2 CONC  Huntsman's Echo at Wood River, Nebraska. In 1861 he moved to Utah and in 1863 established the Farmer's Oracle at Spring Lake Villa, Utah County. In 1864 or 1865 he moved to St. George and began a garden supply and nursery and in 
2 CONC published Our Dixie Times that was later named the Rio Virgin Times. In 1870 he published the Utah Pomologist and Gardener (a monthly). In 1876 he went to Silver Reef and put up a store and a printing office but sold part of offic
2 CONC e before the paper was started. In 1879 he was burned out with others yet afterward rebuilt the store on a larger scale. In 1882 Joseph E. Johnson once again moved at his church's call and settled what would become Tempe, Arizona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Harriet Snider md. 6 Oct 1840 - Nauvoo, Hancock, Illinois 2) Hannah Maria Goddard md. Dec 1850 Council Bluffs, Pottawattamie, Iowa 3) Eliza Saunders md. Jun 1856 - Council Bluffs, Pottawattamie, Iowa
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
2 CONT  Parents:
2 CONT *Ezekiel Johnson (1773 - 1848)
2 CONT *Julia Ellis Hills Johnson (1784 - 1853)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Spouses:
2 CONT *Harriet Eloise Snider Johnson (1823 - 1905)*
2 CONT *Eliza Saunders Johnson (1840 - 1903)*
2 CONT
2 CONT  Children:
2 CONT *Mary Julia Johnson Richards (1841 - 1928)*
2 CONT *Harriet Josephine Johnson Pickett (1850 - 1892)*
2 CONT *Joel Elmer Johnson (1853 - 1912)*
2 CONT *Diadamia Wheeler Johnson Carlton (1856 - 1899)*
2 CONT *Charles Ellis Johnson (1857 - 1926)*
2 CONT *Jessie Ellica Johnson Miller (1860 - 1881)*
2 CONT *Emily Maude Johnson Knowles (1863 - 1934)*
2 CONT *Benjamin Willard Johnson (1866 - 1939)*
2 CONT *Rosemary Johnson Johnson (1866 - 1958)*
2 CONT *Joseph Ellis Johnson (1868 - 1871)*
2 CONT *Gladiolus Ednetta Johnson (1870 - 1872)*
2 CONT *George William Johnson (1873 - 1943)*
2 CONT *Benjamin Franklin Johnson (1876 - 1931)*
2 CONT *Seth Guernsey Johnson (1879 - 1953)*
2 CONT *Rufus David Johnson (1882 - 1968)*
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: City of Mesa Cemetery Mesa Maricopa County Arizona, USA
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT An early member of the LDS Church and son of Ezekiel and Julia Hills Johnson. Joseph moved to Kirtland, Ohio in 1832 and was baptized as a Latter-day Saint in 1833. He was also a member of the Kirtland Camp in 1838 and taught scho
2 CONC ol in Springfield, Illinois in 1840.
2 CONT     He moved to Nauvoo, Illinois in 1840 and was married to Harriet Snider by Joseph Smith later that year. Joseph accompanied Joseph and Hyrum Smith on their way to Carthage jail and was taken as a prisoner when the mob entered N
2 CONC auvoo.  
2 CONT     He settled in Miller's Hollow, now Council Bluffs in 1848 and built the first house in Pottawattamie County, Iowa (other than a log cabin). He was the postmaster at Council Bluffs for five years and was also a member of the fi
2 CONC rst city council.  
2 CONT     In 1852 he established the Council Bluffs Bugle (newspaper). The Bugle office and store destroyed by fire in 1853 yet afterward restored the office and published the paper until 1856. The Bugle was instrumental in getting the 
2 CONC capitol of Nebraska Territory located at Omaha. (Lincoln was later chosen as the state capitol in 1867)
2 CONT     Joseph was elected to the Nebraska legislature but he said he was "too much of a Democrat" to get a seat. He also opened the first store on the present site of Omaha and sent the first train of goods to the Denver, Colorado (C
2 CONC herry Creek) mines.  
2 CONT     In 1854 he published the Omaha Arrow, the first newspaper published on Nebraska soil and that same year accompanied the first party of explorers for a railroad crossing on the Missouri River and Loupe Fork of the Platte river.
2 CONC
2 CONT     He wrote the first published article favoring the North Platte route for the Pacific railroad. In 1850 he crossed the plains to Utah and back and in 1857 started the Crescent City Oracle and also laid out the town of Crescent.
2 CONC
2 CONT     In 1858 he published the Council Bluffs Press and in published the Huntsman's Echo at Wood River, Nebraska.   In 1861 he moved to Utah and in 1863 established the Farmer's Oracle at Spring Lake Villa, Utah County. 
2 CONT     In 1864 or 1865 he moved to St. George and began a garden supply and nursery and in published Our Dixie Times that was later named the Rio Virgin Times.  
2 CONT     In 1870 he published the Utah Pomologist and Gardener (a monthly).   In 1876 he went to Silver Reef and put up a store and a printing office but sold part of office before the paper was started. In 1879 he was burned out with 
2 CONC others yet afterward rebuilt the store on a larger scale.
2 CONT     In 1882 Joseph E. Johnson once again moved at his church's call and settled what would become Tempe, Arizona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Harriet Snider md. 6 Oct 1840 - Nauvoo, Hancock, Illinois 2) Hannah Maria Goddard md. Dec 1850 Council Bluffs, Pottawattamie, Iowa 3) Eliza Saunders md. Jun 1856 - Council Bluffs, Pottawattamie, Iowa
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=9271439 Find A Grave Memorial] via mother [http://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000030795908178 Julia  Johnson (born Hills)] by [http://www.geni.com/projec
2 CONC ts/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jan 13 2015, 22:32:20 UTC''
2 CONT
