2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 NOV 1908
2 ADDR
3 CITY Spanish Fork
3 STAE UT
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} HISTORY of
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHANNA PETERSON JOHNSON
2 CONT
2 CONT “Grandmother Peterson we honor this day, And strive rather awkwardly your life to portray, Yours was a colorful life filled with joy & pain, Through these experiences full living you gained, The things you could relate were you wi
2 CONC th us today, With thrilling adventures your audience could sway, So bear with us please while in a few words we give, The trials that were yours in order to live, Our memories of you are dear to us yet, Now to give your history le
2 CONC st we forget”
2 CONT
2 CONT I sometimes wonder if in these days we were called upon to choose between home & loved-ones and our religion if we would have the faith and courage to be true to our convictions. Such was the case of our early ancestors when they 
2 CONC embraced the gospel and came to Utah to gather with the Saints. We are proud of Grandmother Peterson who knows what such an under-taking meant to our pioneers.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was of Swedish extraction, having been born in Norscheping, Esteryetland, Sweden, Oct.1, 1828. Of her childhood we know but little, however we are sure she must have enjoyed a normally happy life to have developed into the spl
2 CONC endid specimen of womanhood that characterized her as a pioneer Mother My earliest recollection were full of admiration for her. I can remember combing her hair while she would relate stories of her early life. I cherish those mem
2 CONC ories of her and she seemed to enjoy having me do the few little things I can remember doing as a child.
2 CONT
2 CONT She left Sweden in 1863 with five of her seven children leaving her eldest daughter Hannah with her father until they should be able to get means for all to come to Utah. She buried a small son, August, in Sweden. With her small c
2 CONC hildren she sailed from London, April 30, 1863 in the boat John Boyd landing in New York June, of that year. Her children accompanying her were Matilda, Hilma, Charles, John, and small Johan, the latter being buried on the plains 
2 CONC while the family was enroute to Utah. This was one of many hardships it was hers to endure while she sojourned here on earth. With the Atlantic Ocean between her & her loved ones in her native land, and that long trek across the p
2 CONC lains, her ultimate goal and for what? Together with Gods chosen people in the valley of the mountains where she was to help build a new common wealth.
2 CONT
2 CONT She gleaned wheat in the fields for family’s bread day after day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her life was full of hardships, but she must have had her Joys too as she worked to prepare shelter, clothing, & food for her little family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first home mother remembers was the old Pratt pace home next to the present Fred Mately residence. She knew the art of cording the wool, spinning it & making cloth for her use. She made lincey & jeans. She was a weaver of carp
2 CONC ets, many of which were for the church aisles. Mother, as a small child would sit & fill the spools for the loom & would play that they were dolls & she was dressing them as she helped her mother in her small way. She made rugs la
2 CONC ter in life and I wonder if many interesting dreams weren’t woven into those carpets & rugs as she sat day after day working hard to care for her little brood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Years passed by with no word from her children’s Father in Sweden after repeated efforts to reach him by letter she was estranged from him. Her daughter Hannah, coming to Utah the following year with an aunt, was the last time she
2 CONC  heard from her husband until the early 90’s. She was finally married to Christian Larsen, the father of her daughter Tean. Mother remembers him as a loving father but wasn’t for long however as he was slain by the Indians while o
2 CONC n duty with other men caring for the communities, cattle and horses. It must have been a terrible shock coming before the birth of her baby & the first and only child by this union. With one more added to her family she continued 
2 CONC to carry on.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thinking to help her out Olla Larsen & wife came & took mother to care for. A family took Uncle Charles. Mother wasn’t long away from as Grandmother called on the Larsen’s to see her little daughter & when she found Mother had bee
2 CONC n severely spanked, the marks still plain on her little body, and Grandmother took her back home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandmother used to sing Swedish songs for her daughter Hannah’s crowd to dance by at home. That was her way of keeping her children around her to supervise their amusements I remember hearing her sing many Swedish songs she seeme
2 CONC d to enjoy every word of them too as I enjoyed hearing her say them. She was completely happy in the company of her children. She visited them almost daily.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Mother lived on the bench she would walk there to visit her. Mother tells of being able to see her far down the road as she came to see her. When Mother moved down in our present home she relates how she & grandmother would w
2 CONC alk to Palmyra to visit Ant Tildy more than once, once in Particular they carried a bucket if pears between them for the family celebration. I can see her erect, graceful form as she visited back & forth with family & friends. Amo
2 CONC ng her friends I recall Mooster Lina meaning Aunt Leana who lived in Springville, also Mrs. Lendstrom.
2 CONT
2 CONT When my father died we were quarantined & Grandmother was unable to come & console Mother, How badly she felt to thing when her daughter needed her most she most go in, having been through the same experience herself.
2 CONT
2 CONT She owned the home occupied by the Gull Bryant family in third Ward & finally Uncle Charles bought it from her & she built a two room frame house south-east in the same lot where she remained to the last.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married three times, the third being Hans Neilson ,father of her daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT Ellen Neilson Giles Making in all nine children whom I am sure were a joy and satisfaction to her especially in her later life.
2 CONT
2 CONT She passed away in her own home very peacefully, Nov, 30, 1908 at the age of 78, leaving a posterity that reaches well into the hundreds. One humorous expression of hers I remember so well was “I am a poor vido, and weave hard for
2 CONC  a living,”
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma was baptized and confirmed July 9th 1861 by Olf Neilson Jorgensen.
2 CONT
2 CONT In closing the following tribute I give;
2 CONT
2 CONT “To say we are proud of you Grandmother dear,
2 CONT
2 CONT For the things you did while sojourning here,
2 CONT
2 CONT The one thing we thing we thank you for, more than another,
2 CONT
2 CONT You gave to us our own Swedish Mother.”
