1 OCCU Blacksmithing, farming, and threshing were his occupations, both in Utah and Idaho. 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Biography of
2 CONT
2 CONT 		  	  Charles David Johnson   (Pioneer )
2 CONT
2 CONT 				  Came to Utah in 1863
2 CONT
2 CONT 					Written by
2 CONT
2 CONT 				       Eunice Caldwell
2 CONT
2 CONT 				     His Granddaughter 
2 CONT
2 CONT 		               Read by his niece – Clara Ludlow
2 CONT
2 CONT 			         Of Camp Benjamin Stewart
2 CONT
2 CONT 			           Of Daughters of Pioneers
2 CONT
2 CONT 					Of Utah Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT 				         Benjamin, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Charles David Johnson Sr. was born in Norkoping, Sweden, June 25th, 1857. The son of Niels Johnson and Johannah Peterson.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	At 6 years of age he came to America with his Mother and four brothers and sisters in a sailing vessel of the “White Star” line. The journey required ten weeks during stormy weather.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	The family joined an immigrant train reroute for Salt Lake City. Privation and hardship, incident to the journey across the plains, they endured bravely.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	They remained in Salt Lake City only a few days. They then continued on to Spanish Fork, Utah where they made their pioneer home.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	 As a boy he herded cows on the outskirts of the forming section with many of his boyhood chums. The Indians were very un friendly and dangerous.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	 During his youth he assumed much of the responsibility of obtaining a livelihood for the family, to aid his Mother, who wove rugs and carpets. She came to this country with her children, leaving her husband in Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In 1880 he married Annie Beckstrom. To this union were born two daughters, Nora Boyack of Arco, and Augusta Strong of Hayward, California, and Charles D. Johnson Jr. of Moore, Idaho. 	The Mother died, leaving these three small ch
2 CONC ildren, the oldest eleven years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	On December 21, 1892, He married Augusta Elizabeth Beckstrom in the Manti Temple. She was a sister of his first wife. To this union was born the following; Iva Caldwell, Elmer, John, Earl and William Reed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	While in Spanish Fork he was chosen as 2nd counselor to Marinus Larsen in the 3rd Ward Bishopric.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thirty six years next Saturday, ( March  8th, 1941 ) since he arrived in Moore, in company with Mark Jenkins, William C. King and families.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	(Taken from a new paper account of his life given by him to his granddaughter Emire Caldwell)
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In the year 1905 he brought his wife and children, (7 in number) too the Lost River Valley. Here he homesteaded next to the hill west of the town of Moore. He made farming his occupation.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	He raised some of the first wheat in this valley. People were so eager to get some of it for seed that he was unable to keep but a little of his first crop.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	After wheat became a major crop, Charles Johnson went in partnership with Don & Will King. They jointly owned a threshing machine outfit and went up and down the valley harvesting. They trashed as many as 90,000 bushels in one se
2 CONC ason. The seasons usually lasted from September until November.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	For two consecutive years he remembers quitting on Thanksgiving Day. During these late seasons while threshing in Antelope Valley the grain was sometimes hauled to the thresher on bob sleds. This was his occupation for 21 years. 
2 CONC Following this he accepted a position as janitor of the Moore school house in 1926. This he held for ten years. His failing health at the age of 79 years forced him to retire.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Me. Johnson has been a highly respected citizen and is admired by all of his friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Blacksmithing, farming, and threshing were his occupations, both in Utah and Idaho.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	He was a member of the school board at Moore, when the new building was erected.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	When Lost River Stake was organized August 18, 1919 he become a member of the High Council and released Oct. 28, 1923.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	April 27, 1924 he was chosen a counselor in the High Priests quorum which position he held until Oct. 19, 1930.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In church and civic affairs he made generous contributions of time and means.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Uncle Charlie died March 1, 1941 in More, Idaho, at the age of 84.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	As I remember Uncle Charlie he was a lover of children, we all liked to be with him. He was a good singer, and at parties entertained with his singing. One of his favorite songs being, “The Old Arm Chair.”
