1 NAME Hilma Caroline /Johnson/
2 GIVN Hilma Caroline
2 SURN Johnson
2 _MARNM Hales
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 19 JAN 1860
2 ADDR
3 CITY Norrköping
3 STAE Östergötland County
3 CTRY Sweden
1 BAPM
2 DATE 23 DEC 1860
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 MAY 1945
2 ADDR
3 CITY Benjamin
3 STAE Utah
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 10 MAY 1945
2 PLAC Spanish Fork City Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Spanish Fork
3 STAE Utah
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 			                      HISTORY of  
2 CONT
2 CONT 		HILMA CAROLINE JOHNSON HALES SIMPSON
2 CONT
2 CONT 		Come to Utah 1863. 		 		Written by Wallace Hales a son.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		Of Camp Benjamin Stewart  		 		Of Daughters of the Pioneers of Utah Co. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 		Benjamin, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT 	 	The leaders of our church have said times that Scandinavia and Norway have furnished the backbone of its structure. In the very beginning of the missionary work, it was found that these people were of the House of Israel and ha
2 CONC d a ready ear for the Gospel message. Hundreds of them joined the church and soon there after the spirit of “gathering “manifested itself. Thus; as their forebears, Enick, the Red and Lief, the Lucky, responded to the call that of
2 CONC  conquest and adventure, they responded to an indefinable spiritual call that finally brought them to Zion in the land of the setting sun.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Among this sturdy race that early began the gathering movement, was the family of Niels Johnson and his good wife, Johannah Peterson Johnson. In the little town of Norscheping, Esteryetlund, Sweden, this couple began their voyage
2 CONC  of matrimony, and in a few years they were the proud parents of three girls, Hanna, Matilda and Hilma, and two sons, Charles David and John Adolph.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	The finances of the family were meager so that all could not come too America at one time, so it was decided that mother and four younger children should come first; the father and oldest girl Hannah ( uncle Marinus, wife) were t
2 CONC o remain until the means of the family would permit them to follow. They left London April 30, 1863, in the boat called John Boyd, and landed in New York, June 1, 1863.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Though grandmother had her failings, it must be admitted that she had the courageous heart of her Viking race to face the storms and tempests of the Atlantic Ocean and hardships and hazards of the prairies, in order to gather wit
2 CONC h the Saints in Zion. After years of hardship and separation from her husband, she reached Utah, having buried a baby boy on the plains and settled in what is now the “hub of the Universe”, Spanish Fork. Here she maintained her li
2 CONC ttle family as best she could by Weaving. At times, however, it was impossible for her to make ends meet and she was obliged to place some of the children in other homes.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Hilma, the subject of this history, third girl and fourth child of the family, was born in Norscheping, Esteryetlund, Sweden, Decenber 19, 1890. She was only three years old when the long journey was begun and therefore little or
2 CONC  no recollection of the childhood home or of the incidents of the perilous journey remains with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	When about five years of age, she was placed in the home of Ola Larsen, who promised to be good to her and who wanted to adopt her, but jealous and watchful eyes of the mother soon found strips all over the Childs body, and she w
2 CONC as taken home again.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	As this part is being written, the writer cannot help contrasting our days with those when mother was a girl. What a revolution, or shall I say revelation? Then the horse was the fastest means of transportation either of sound or
2 CONC  of mail. Today we have wireless, radio, and the airplane. Think, too, of the tallow candle, now the electric light; then the cycle and flail, now the self-binder and thresher. Picture, if you will, the hoop skirt and the dresses 
2 CONC of the pioneer women, and you will agree with me that one dress then would cover a dozen of our modern flappers.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Mother went to school in a little log school house where the Thurber School now stands. One of her teachers was Mr. Hillman. Some of the girl friends of mother were Emily Fereday, Annie and Mary Beckstrom, Sena Olsen and others a
2 CONC mong her boy friends were Has Olsen, Joe Packet, Jim Sorensen and Father. Of the latter mother tells an interesting story about the dime and snow.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Mother was married to Fred Hales when she was seventeen years of age. They were married in the old Endowment House at Salt Lake City, November 14, 1878 by Elder Daniel H. Wells. The trip to Salt lake City was made by wagon, Mrs. 
2 CONC Margaret Brown going along as a sort of chaperone, and as she was in the millinery business, went on business also.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Their first home and one in which the first five children, Clara, Nora, Lorena, Ruben, and Louis, were born, was the little brick home in which Jacob Christopherson now lives. John, Wallase, Eva and Lee were born at the Old Hales
2 CONC  home at or near the Denver & Rio Grande Railroad station.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Mother made starch from potatoes, by grating the potatoes, pouring water on the potatoes and the starch settled at the bottom. And when the last water was poured off, the starch was pretty and white; and when dry was broken in pi
2 CONC eces from the wooden tubs and used when needed. When we were small children I remember father going by team and covered wagon to Southern Utah to see about locating a new home. We were real lonesome and in the evenings we would si
2 CONC t on the front porch always watching for a dust and a covered wagon returning. While mother played her accordion and sang to us until bead time. We greatly enjoyed mother as she was always an entertainer with music and stories of 
2 CONC her childhood and girlhood days. She Ws never too busy too stop her work and play games with us or what ever we wished her to join with us. All of her children but Louis, who died March 28, 1899, when eight years of age; grew to m
2 CONC an and womanhood, and all have families.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Father worked away from much of the time, in the mines or on the railroad and in the canyons, and this left mother with the responsibly of raising the family. Thus she was prepared in a way for the added responsibility of assumin
2 CONC g the father’s duties which were soon to be hers, for on January 14, 1900, father passed away. When a young man, he had been injured by having his head caught between two wagons, and this, it was thought, caused his death, and not
2 CONC  smallpox, an epidemic at that time, as was then assigned as the cause of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	With a heavy heart she faced the new day. The welfare of he fatherless kiddies now occupied her mind, and with that idea in mind she set out to do the job as best she could. No one, who knows of her trials, will say that she did 
2 CONC not do it well. She succeeded by working as, nurse farming with her boys, and doing anything within her power to make a live hood for herself and family.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Mother was always an optimist. Never, or seldom, when asked how she was getting along, did she droop her head and say, “not very well” but she response to such query would be, “fine and Dandy”.	
2 CONT
2 CONT 	It was often said that Hilmy would soon re-marry and if she had, it would not have been to her discredit. But she stayed single until her baby was grown and gone to make his own way in life.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In the spring of 1917, while visiting her children in Salt Lake City, she met Robert T. Simpson, a wilder and retired railroad engineer. Their association, which was at first somewhat casual, soon took a more serious turn and the
2 CONC y were married June 4th, 1917, in Salt Lake City. This Marriage was proved satisfactory from practically every angle. The families of both were raised and both were alone and lonesome for companionship, and there can be no doubt t
2 CONC hat their married life has in the main, been very happy. Mother had worked in the Salt Lake Temple for the last twelve years having as her goal to do work for one thousand names. She almost has reached that satisfaction having don
2 CONC e some over nine hundred names.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Now at the end of this feeble attempt to compile some few of the incidents and events of one of the noble pioneer woman, let me express the hope that that this may be the nucleus from which her children may compile a complete and
2 CONC  accurate history to be preserved to her children and children’s children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	I close with this sentiment, which I am sure expresses, the feeling of all her children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 	        “With spirit calm as the summer sea. 	          Moving in sweet serenity. 	          Oh! We are very sure there is no other 	          In all this world like you,   “OUR MOTHER! 		   		 		A Supplement to the Original Hist
2 CONC ory of 		 		Hilma C. Johnson Hales Simpson
2 CONT
2 CONT 				 				Childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT As a small child in Sweden, Hilma was able to do many things in her home that the other children would be punished for. In those days children were to be seen and not heard, and very little of ether if the parents were busy. Yet s
2 CONC he could intrude on her father’s time without apprehension of being punished by him. He was a Taylor and like most fathers his work came first, and so it did with all but Hilma. She seemed to have special privileges. Her mother to
2 CONC ld her this as she was only three years old and has no memories of her childhood in Sweden. In America she was stricken with a terrible disease that, after several years, was diagnosed as Typhoid Fever. She was very young and the 
2 CONC impression of her sickness was that she was suspended in the air. However, her bed was on the floor so she must have been delirious from the fever. All of her hair came out and the new growth was very curly, more evidence of the d
2 CONC read typhoid. She also was assigned the task of herding the cattle and has often told us of the fear she had of Indian; and little wonder as her step-father was killed by them.
2 CONT
2 CONT 			  Girlhood
2 CONT
2 CONT She was put to work when very young. Her first job was with a family on a farm on the Bingham road. She was very homesick but the lady was kind to her. Her first experience at cooking chicken was rather disappointing when she was 
2 CONC asked whether or she had ever cleaned a chicken. She said yes, anxious to be as helpful as possible. Her brother-in-law used to clean and dress birds for the hotel in Salt Lake, and having watched him do so many she felt confident
2 CONC  it wouldn’t be hard. So it was her job to fix the chicken while the lady went into the city for the day.  Chicken soup was to be the evening meal and to this end mother prepared the chicken. It was soon ready for the pot and how 
2 CONC proud she was of her efforts. Supper time came and such appetizing smells bubbled forth. The lid was removed and there floating in the soup was wheat from the craw that mother didn’t know a chicken possessed. The lady didn’t scold
2 CONC  her but complimented her for being so willing to try and please. She threw the soup away and ate the chicken
2 CONT
2 CONT Later she came to Salt Lake to work and went from house to house applying for work. She was rewarded for her efforts but it was of short duration. The husband had utterly spoiled his wife, making her rather difficult to please. Mo
2 CONC ther’s friend, Harriett Hales, who later become her sister-in-law, came to see if she would like to have her job as she was going to her home in Spanish Fork. Her mistress, as they were called, was very kind and she knew mother wo
2 CONC uld like her. So she bundled what few things se had, put them out the window, told the lady she was going to spend the night with her friend, and never returned. Finally she got work at the Walker House and here she was completely
2 CONC  happy. She made a life long friend of Jesse Evens, who was working there and by a strange coincidence, after nearly fifty years they were to be neighbors in the 34th ward in Salt Lake Stake and renew friendships.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were circuses in those days, too, and mother and her girl friend, Sena Olsen, decided to take in the free sights and music. As always, there were smooth tongued strangers in those days as there are now and have always been s
2 CONC ince time began. Mother, being a very attractive girl with brown hair and eyes, a pink clear skin and a graceful trim figure, caught the eye of these “dudes” as she called him. Approaching her and Sena with a jaunty air, he asked 
2 CONC if they wouldn’t like to go in and see the show. One look at him and mother was sure he was the kind her mother had warned her about; the slick villainous kind with that handkerchief of knock-out fumes, used over the nose and mout
2 CONC h of the unsuspecting victim who was lured by his attractiveness and gay chatter. Mother had a feeling all was not well for Sena and her unless they proved equal to the situation at hand. He asked if he might bring a pal for Sena 
2 CONC and while he went for him, mother figured she must act now and quickly. Seeing a countrified boy, as she describes him, she chose to ask him to take them home. He got his friend for the girlfriend and they started back for the hot
2 CONC el. They were walking along, mother keeping the conversation at a merry pace, but the thought of what to do with their newly made acquaintances was always foremost in her mind. The one with mother asked where they lived and she an
2 CONC swered that it was several blocks past the Walker House. When they arrived at the gate, she thanked the boys for seeing them home, said good night, and went in with thankful heart for a safe arrival home.
2 CONT
2 CONT She tells an amusing story of a love letter to her best boy friend and who later was to be my father. Her schooling being very limited, it was quite a task to write a letter. Her brother-in-law had given her the alphabet on a slat
2 CONC e as an aid in spelling. Father went to get his mail and on asking if he had any, the mail carrier said, “I have one here but I can’t make out who it’s for.” Father asked to see it and said “yes, that’s mine.” She persevered to im
2 CONC prove and today she writes a very legible hand and can read any amount of good plane print. As her family, we are surely proud of her efforts to improve herself.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		            Courtship and Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Her love affair with father had its ups and downs. He was five years  older than she and was ready to settle down, while she was enjoying the best years of young woman hood. He must have been quite convincing, however, as they wer
2 CONC e married on November 14, 1878, a month before her 18th birthday. Her first home was on the east bench in Spanish Fork, Utah, near the cemetery, which was ever a horror in her young wifehood. She was young enough to imagine the de
2 CONC ad could come forth at the least provocation and believe the stories of people being buried alive and all the other mysteries that could happen in the early days when superstitions played no small part in the lives of our parent’s
2 CONC  years ago. She might well be nervous. Father used to go to the canyon for wood and leave around two A.M. in order to get his load and be back by night fall. She never stayed in bed but busied herself with cupboard cleaning and an
2 CONC ything else that would keep her from being too nervous until day light came. Her first baby came more than three years after she was married so she had many lonesome hours when occasions, such as the one above, took father away fr
2 CONC om home. Her mother and sisters lived in the North West part of town and she was in the south east part, making quite a distance to walk for their weekly visits. She was able to recognize her mother as soon as she turned by “found
2 CONC ry Larsen’s” corner on the road straight for her home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother’s early life was typical of most women in those days; hard work and so few conveniences and a large family to care for. She had to dip her water from the ditch into barrels to settle for the weekly wash day, with wood ashes
2 CONC  to soften it. And the drinking water was carried up the hill from a neighbor a few blocks west. One great help was the happy spirit and keen wit she possessed. Her presences at the town’s festivities were sought because she was s
2 CONC uch good company.  She knitted stocking for nine children and I suppose for herself and father, although she never mentioned them. If the children were supplied that was the chief concern. Her ability to knit in new heels and toes
2 CONC  and have them fit so well was what I thought “pretty neat.” 					 				Widowhood
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Mother was left a widow at the age of forty with eight children, ranging from three to nineteen years of age; one son, Louis having died the year before father at the age of nine. She did practical nursing and most anything else 
2 CONC she could get to provide the family with food and clothing.  Through her work as a nurse made many new and lasting friendships. One in particular, was Edna Payne who as much a daughter to mother as her own. She visits her in Los A
2 CONC ngeles at least ones a year.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Mother remarried June 13th, 1917 to Robert Temple Simpson, a retired railroad engineer, and moved to Salt Lake, to make her home. She traveled quite a bit with father, a new experience for her, having never been out of Utah befor
2 CONC e .She has been to the west coast times without number and at this writing is on her second trip to Washington D.C., visiting her son Wallace M. Hales. Mother lived twenty-two years with each husband which seems strange when you t
2 CONC hing of it. No one would ever have guessed father Simpson was our foster father. Our family was very fond of him and his family has always called mother their own. Since father’s death, February 15th, 1939, mother has made her hom
2 CONC e with her children; the greater part of the time with her oldest daughter Clara Ludlow in Benjamin, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	My contribution to the interesting life mother has enjoyed and still is enjoying could go on and on. The joys and sorrows that belong to those of us whose lives have been touched by them are innumerable. This thought in closing- 
2 CONC those who give loving service are the happy ones and reap the blessings of their unselfishness. This is indeed true of mother. Young and old alike enjoy having her around.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Of mother’s nine children, seven are now living; Clara Ludlow, Benjamin, Rena, Rueben, John, and Eva, Salt Lake; Wallace of Washington D.C., and Lee of Logan, Utah. A daughter, Nora Kirkman died in 1920 of influenza. 	May I striv
2 CONC e to be a worthy daughter to a mother who has fought the good fight.
2 CONT
2 CONT 								Eva Hales Stevenson
2 CONT
2 CONT 								November 11, 1940
