1 CONT
1 CONT Ransom Marion Stevens, was a graduate of the Brigham Young University, at Provo, Utah, and went on a mission to the Southern States when only eighteen years old and again at the age of thirty he went to Samoa on a mission. He was 
1 CONC president of the mission and died April 28, 1894, at Fagalii, Upolu, Samoa. The following obituary appeared in the Deseret News:
1 CONT
1 CONT    "Fagalii, Samoa, May 23, 1894. Since you  last heard from this far-off land, the hand of death  has taken from our midst, our honored and beloved  president, Elder Ransom M. Stevens. He had been complaining of not feeling well 
1 CONC for some time, and had been confined to his bed for ten days, when on the morning of Saturday, April 28th, 1894, his noble spirit was called hence, and we were left to mourn his loss, and try to comfort and console his grief-strik
1 CONC en wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "Brother Stevens had been president of the Samoan mission for some time previous to his demise, and as president and co-laborer he won the love and admiration of all with whom he was associated."
